{"item_id":"feed:https://ruckauthority.com/guides/goruck-ruck-shuffle-basic-rucker","version_id":"1ee84564c3fae6506fa8596ffdda0ca171a6d702feb3e3991d8d94de996e9bff","kind":"feed","publisher_id":"ruckauthority","title":"GORUCK's Ruck Shuffle: Technique, Gear and Research","url":"https://ruckauthority.com/guides/goruck-ruck-shuffle-basic-rucker","published_at":"2026-10-13T00:00:00Z","source_notes":{"perspective":"independent","notes":"Rucking gear reviews (packs, vests, plates, socks, base layers). Affiliate commissions; GORUCK supplied review units. Very high recent volume suggests bulk/SEO publishing; check enclosure MIME types (image enclosures)."},"selection_reason":"Under Relevance, Credibility, and Uncertainty, the pack specifications and described load-carriage studies provide citable evidence for Pieces 02 and 03 on carried-load tradeoffs, while leaving Basic Rucker field performance and GORUCK's knee-protection claim unestablished. Commercial incentives warrant caution because the publisher earns GORUCK affiliate commissions, received review units, and publishes at high volume; the article nevertheless offers substantive scrutiny rather than unsupported promotion. Freshness and novelty is satisfied by the newly incorporated airborne-shuffle research and corrected conclusions, although additional independent publishers are needed for a series article's central argument.","excerpted":true,"passages":[{"text":"Free newsletter\n\nRucking notes by email\n\nNew gear reviews and practical training notes. Join now to get the newsletter from its first issue. Unsubscribe anytime.\n\nThe shuffle is longstanding GORUCK guidance. Its white paper describes quick, low steps with one foot on the ground, using less weight once you have experience under a pack.\n\nThe September campaign pairs that technique with the Basic Rucker. Jason McCarthy's 30 lb example is his lighter load, not a universal starting prescription.\n\nThe Army calls the same movement the airborne shuffle, and two university studies have measured it under a combat load. It cost more oxygen and raised heart rate compared with walking at the same speeds.\n\nNo study compares a 30 lb shuffle with an unloaded jog, so GORUCK's claim that the swap is easier on the knees remains unproven.\n\nThe Basic Rucker has one elevated plate pocket rated for up to 30 lb of Standard plates. A suitable pack you already own may do the job.\n\nYou can keep walking. Shuffling is an optional way to pursue pace, not a requirement for getting value from rucking.\n\nGORUCK's September campaign makes a lively case for the ruck shuffle: reduce the weight, move faster, and give y"},{"text":"our cardiovascular system more to do. The archived email pairs that advice with the Basic Rucker.\n\nThe technique predates this campaign. GORUCK's Rucking White Paper, published years earlier, already described shuffling and distinguished it from running with a heavy pack. This is renewed promotion of an established technique, not a reversal of the company's walking advice.\n\nThe useful starting point is GORUCK's technique and intended audience: experienced ruckers looking for another way to train. Below, we explain the setup, what the Army's own studies of the movement found, the relevant pack features and where the safety evidence stops.\n\nRuck Authority earns a commission on GORUCK purchases made through our links.\n\nHow GORUCK describes the technique\n\nIn the September 24 email, co-founder Jason McCarthy describes shuffling as a sustainable effort between walking and running. His example is 30 lb, compared with the 45 or 60 lb he carries on other days. He promotes cardiovascular demand, stamina and calorie expenditure, and pitches a specific swap: if you jog an 8 to 9 minute mile, put 30 lb on your back and shuffle instead. The email's list of benefits includes saving your knees. He"},{"text":" also explicitly cautions against running with a heavy ruck.\n\nHis three setup cues are to use a manageable load, go without a hip belt, and hold the pack close and high. The email directs experienced ruckers toward a faster session and links the Basic Rucker to its Ruck or Die events.\n\nThe older white paper gives the movement more detail: short, quick steps, feet kept low, and one foot remaining on the ground. It describes this as an advanced technique to try with less weight after building experience. That matters: calling any loaded jog a shuffle doesn't faithfully describe GORUCK's instructions.\n\nThe email's 75% effort is a coaching cue. It doesn't specify a percentage of maximum heart rate, heart-rate reserve or a measured threshold. We shouldn't turn it into a precise Zone 2 target.\n\nWhat the airborne shuffle studies found\n\nUS soldiers sometimes use the same short-stride gait on loaded marches and call it the airborne shuffle. Researchers at Middle Tennessee State University have measured it against loaded walking in a 2014 master's thesis and a 2017 doctoral dissertation. Both were reviewed by university committees rather than journal referees, and both used small samples.\n\nB"},{"text":"renes (2014) tested 20 Army ROTC cadets, average age 22, carrying a standard combat load at 2.5, 3.0 and 3.5 mph. At every speed, shuffling raised oxygen cost, heart rate and respiratory exchange ratio compared with walking (p < .001). The thesis concludes that the shuffle is not a more economical way to carry a combat load than walking.\n\nClark (2017) studied 11 soldiers and defines the shuffle as a deliberately shortened stride. At 3.0 mph it produced shorter strides, shorter stance and swing phases and a quicker cadence than walking. Left to pick their own pace, soldiers shuffled faster than they walked, and oxygen use, heart rate, breathing and perceived effort all rose with it. Clark suggests the shuffle may reduce stress on the lower limbs, but the dissertation measured gait timing and energy cost, not joint forces. Its recommendation is to prioritize walking on loaded marches because the shuffle costs more energy and feels harder.\n\nRead together, the two projects support half of GORUCK's pitch: the shuffle is harder work than walking at the same speed, which is the point if you want more cardiovascular demand from a lighter pack. The knee half appears only as a possibility in"},{"text":" Clark's discussion. Both projects also compared the shuffle with walking at 2.5 to 3.5 mph, roughly a 17 to 24 minute mile. Neither tested GORUCK's actual swap of a 30 lb shuffle for an unloaded 8 to 9 minute-mile jog.\n\nWhat load-carriage running research adds\n\nFor the comparison GORUCK makes, against jogging, the closest evidence comes from studies of walking and running with and without load. They help explain why technique and load matter. They can't settle which option is safer for your knees.\n\nHow you move changes the forces\n\nKeller and colleagues' 1996 study measured ground reaction forces in 23 recreational athletes walking and running at different speeds. Its slow-jogging condition produced particularly high forces and loading rates, associated with more vertical movement.\n\nThat gives a biomechanical reason to pay attention to bounce. It doesn't validate GORUCK's loaded technique: participants weren't performing a standardized ruck shuffle, and the study didn't measure subsequent knee injuries. A form cue can be sensible without having its own clinical trial.\n\nAdding weight changes the task\n\nBirrell and colleagues measured increased ground reaction forces during military l"},{"text":"e claim, we'd want a direct comparison with clearly defined loads, speeds and technique, followed by relevant joint measurements or injuries over time. None of the sources on this page supplies that, including the two airborne shuffle projects. The fair conclusion is that a knee-protection advantage over jogging isn't established here, not that shuffling has been shown to damage knees.\n\nArmy injury-prevention guidance cautions about ruck running. Knapik's review discusses load-carriage injuries and conditioning. These support taking progression seriously; they aren't trials of GORUCK's shuffle.\n\nWe haven't tested the shuffle against jogging. This is an analysis of GORUCK's published advice, the airborne shuffle studies and related load-carriage research, not an injury-prevention trial or a medical recommendation.\n\nWhere the Basic Rucker fits\n\nThe current Basic Rucker listing offers 14L and 17L versions. Its elevated pocket takes up to 30 lb of Standard plates, including a 20-plus-10 combination. The 420D Robic body, 840D bottom, side storage and compact layout make it a plausible choice for carrying a plate and a few essentials.\n\nThat capacity is a product limit, not advice to star"},{"text":"t at 30 lb. McCarthy's example reflects his own training. Your familiar load may be much lighter.\n\nOur Basic Rucker introduction covers its layout and plate compatibility. We haven't established its performance in a controlled shuffle comparison, so there's no firsthand bounce or joint-comfort result to claim here.\n\nBasic Rucker or Rucker 5.0?\n\nChoose around the rest of your use:\n\nBasic Rucker: a compact bag with one plate pocket for lighter sessions and everyday essentials.\n\nRucker 5.0: more room, training handles and two plate pockets. GORUCK lists 20L and 25L versions with a combined plate capacity of 75 lb. That's capacity, not a shuffle recommendation.\n\nIf your current pack holds an appropriate load securely and fits comfortably, a new one isn't a prerequisite. The plate comparison can help check dimensions before buying weight for an existing bag.\n\nShould you try it?\n\nFor general fitness, brisk loaded walking remains a useful session on its own. Shuffling is an optional addition to that training. If you're training for a particular event, check its rules and practice the movement it requires.\n\nIf you're an experienced rucker interested in shuffling, start by understanding GOR"},{"text":"combat load. The shuffle cost more oxygen and raised heart rate, and the dissertation also recorded higher perceived effort. Neither compared it with jogging.\n\nGORUCK distinguishes the two and discourages running with a heavy pack. Follow the actual demonstration rather than treating any loaded jog as its recommended shuffle. The name alone doesn't establish how demanding a movement is.\n\nThere's no universal shuffle starting weight established by the studies here. McCarthy's 30 lb example is lighter than his usual load. It shouldn't become a default for someone with a different training history.\n\nNo study compares a loaded shuffle with an unloaded jog. The airborne shuffle studies compared it with walking; one suggests it may ease lower-limb stress but didn't measure joint forces. Ground-force and loaded-running studies measure related movements, which aren't knee injury outcomes. The claimed advantage is unproven rather than disproven.\n\nNo. A suitably fitted pack that holds your chosen load securely may work. The Basic Rucker is one option; check plate fit and comfort before changing your pace.\n\nCorrection, October 9, 2026: An earlier version overstated the novelty of GORUCK's adv"},{"text":"ice, treated an effort cue as a heart-rate target, and claimed Basic Rucker testing that wasn't documented. It also drew conclusions about shuffle safety that the cited studies don't establish. Those passages have been corrected.\n\nCorrection, October 13, 2026: Earlier versions of this article and our social posts treated the ruck shuffle as unstudied because we searched only for GORUCK's name for it. A reader pointed out the military name. The US Army calls the movement the airborne shuffle, and two university studies have measured it against walking. We added their findings and narrowed the open question to GORUCK's specific comparison with jogging.\n\nRelated\n\nGORUCK Basic Rucker: Introduction, Plate Fit and Current Options\n\nGORUCK Rucker 5.0 Review: 25L Tested With a 20 lb Plate\n\nRuck Running vs Fast Walking: What Should You Train?\n\nSpeed Rucking: Walking Intervals to Improve Your Pace\n\nWhich GORUCK Should You Buy?\n\nRelated Articles\n\nIndoor Rucking Plan: A 4-Week Treadmill Program\n\nA four-week treadmill rucking plan that holds pack load steady, separates incline from duration progressions, and uses two loaded sessions plus unloaded aerobic work.\n\nFall and Winter Rucking: Indoor Op"},{"text":"tions, Dark Evenings and Events\n\nChoose a safer route as daylight and weather change: indoor alternatives, cold-weather preparation, after-dark checks and verified fall 2026 events.\n\nIs Rucking Bad for Your Back? What the Science Says\n\nLearn how load, pack fit, training volume, and existing back conditions affect spinal stress during recreational rucking.\n\nHill Ruck Training: Elevation Workouts for Every Terrain\n\nHow to add hills to a rucking week: uphill and downhill form, five hill and stair workouts, treadmill and garage options for flat areas, and how to progress safely."}]}
{"item_id":"feed:https://ruckauthority.com/injuries/rucksack-palsy-numb-hands","version_id":"33e933df52c538cb87dd722a55fd3dcb7a1d574de307d1729f4a7f4e3232aa85","kind":"feed","publisher_id":"ruckauthority","title":"Numb Hands While Rucking? What to Do and When to Get Help","url":"https://ruckauthority.com/injuries/rucksack-palsy-numb-hands","published_at":"2026-10-11T00:00:00Z","source_notes":{"perspective":"independent","notes":"Rucking gear reviews (packs, vests, plates, socks, base layers). Affiliate commissions; GORUCK supplied review units. Very high recent volume suggests bulk/SEO publishing; check enclosure MIME types (image enclosures)."},"selection_reason":"Under Relevance, Credibility, and Uncertainty, the cited load-carriage review and military case series provide specific evidence for pack suspension and load-distribution tradeoffs relevant to multi-day Mammoth carry, while clearly separating proposed precautions from validated prevention or product-performance claims. Commercial incentives warrant caution: the publisher earns affiliate commissions, received GORUCK review units, and publishes at high volume; its GORUCK examples do not independently establish protection against nerve injury. This best supports Piece 03 under Suitability and official requirements, with military-to-competitor applicability qualified and required status left unspecified because no official passage is supplied.","excerpted":true,"passages":[{"text":"Free newsletter\n\nRucking notes by email\n\nNew gear reviews and practical training notes. Join now to get the newsletter from its first issue. Unsubscribe anytime.\n\nTingling under a loaded pack can come from shoulder-strap compression or traction, but numb hands do not prove rucksack palsy.\n\nStop, remove the pack, and end the loaded session. Persistent or recurrent symptoms and any weakness need assessment before another ruck.\n\nA hip belt, wider padded straps, lower load, and better adjustment may reduce nerve pressure; none makes nerve injury impossible.\n\nSeek prompt assessment for persistent numbness or weakness. Sudden one-sided numbness or weakness, facial droop, or speech trouble needs emergency help, even if it clears.\n\nWhat's happening\n\nRucksack palsy is a load-carriage-related nerve injury involving compression or traction of the brachial plexus, with shoulder straps as the usual mechanism. Symptoms can include tingling, numbness, pain, cramping, or weakness in the upper limb (Knapik et al., 2016). Similar symptoms can also come from the neck, a single peripheral nerve, circulation, or another condition, so numb hands under a pack are not a diagnosis by themselves.\n\nMild ting"},{"text":"ling that settles completely after the pack comes off may be transient pressure. That doesn't establish the cause or make continuing safe. Diagnosed rucksack palsy can take months to recover, and some patients have lasting symptoms (Nylund et al., 2011). Stop, remove the pack, and end the loaded session; don't wait for weakness or persistent numbness to wear off under load.\n\nThat evidence supports a simple boundary: treat any new tingling as a signal to remove the pressure, not as proof that no damage has occurred. Do not continue rucking through weakness or persistent numbness while waiting for it to \"loosen up.\"\n\nThe brachial plexus runs from the neck through the shoulder into the arm. A load-carriage review names heavier load, longer carrying distance, and poor load distribution as hypothetical risk factors, with strap compression or traction as the mechanism. It frames its prevention measures as ways symptoms may be reduced rather than as tested programs (Knapik et al., 2016).\n\n01Knapik and colleagues describe rucksack palsy as a compression or traction injury and recommend reducing load, improving distribution, adjusting and padding straps, and considering a hip belt.\n\n02Nylun"},{"text":"d and colleagues found that 30 of 38 military patients (79%) reported no symptoms at long-term follow-up; eight (21%) still reported symptoms. Symptom reports and measured muscle strength were separate outcomes.\n\n03Dorhout Mees and colleagues reviewed 63 Dutch military cases, including 52 with sensory symptoms (83%). Their recovery table lists four complete, 44 partial and one absent recovery: 49 recorded outcomes, with a median follow-up of nine weeks. Those short, incomplete records cannot establish a permanent-disability rate.\n\n04The exact rules in this guide, such as one finger of strap slack or a timed fit check, are practical field cues rather than clinically validated thresholds.\n\nFive fit factors worth checking\n\nThe following factors can increase shoulder pressure or nerve traction. They are useful fit checks, not a way to diagnose the source of numbness.\n\nHigh shoulder-strap pressure. Over-tightened straps can concentrate pressure across the shoulder. Straps should control the pack without creating a hard pressure line, but there is no universal \"correct\" notch for every body and pack.\n\nNarrow or poorly padded straps. Narrow straps can concentrate a given load over less su"},{"text":"rface area. A load-carriage review recommends wider, better-padded straps as one way to reduce traction and compression symptoms (Knapik et al., 2016).\n\nMore load than you or the pack can distribute comfortably. In the Finnish series, heavier recalled loads at symptom onset were associated with reduced muscle strength at follow-up; they did not significantly predict self-reported symptoms (Nylund et al., 2011). That association doesn't establish a safe weight. New symptoms mean the pack comes off.\n\nNo effective load transfer. A properly fitted hip belt can shift some load away from the shoulders. The Knapik review recommends considering one, but neither a 15-pound threshold nor a guaranteed percentage of transfer applies to every pack and torso.\n\nHolding the straps for long periods. Gripping the straps can keep the arms and shoulders in one position and can mask a poor fit. Let the arms swing naturally when terrain allows. This is a practical comfort cue; the cited studies do not establish strap-gripping as a measured cause of rucksack palsy.\n\nWhat to do if your hands go numb\n\nIf tingling or numbness begins under the pack, stop rather than trying to diagnose it while walking. The f"},{"text":"irst goal is to remove the pressure and see whether the symptoms settle completely.\n\nStep 1: Take the pack off. Set it down safely and note whether the tingling, numbness, pain, or weakness changes. Removing the load is more informative than making several strap changes while the nerve is still under pressure.\n\nStep 2: Let your arms rest in a neutral position. Gentle arm movement is fine if comfortable, but do not aggressively stretch or pull on a numb or weak arm.\n\nStep 3: End the loaded session, even if the tingling clears. Seek prompt medical assessment for numbness that doesn't settle completely or for any weakness. Sudden one-sided numbness or weakness needs emergency help, even if it clears; don't assume the pack explains it. The warning signs below take priority over fit adjustments.\n\nStep 4: Review fit before a later session only after symptoms settle completely. Any weakness, persistent numbness or repeated episodes need assessment before you return. Reduce the load, repack it symmetrically, and check for twisted straps, hard pressure points and an unstable load. These are proposed risk-reduction measures in the load-carriage review, not a treatment for diagnosed nerve inj"},{"text":"ury.\n\nStep 5: Refit the suspension without chasing a magic percentage. Wider padded straps and a well-fitted hip belt may reduce shoulder pressure. One finger of strap slack is a useful field cue, not a medical threshold.\n\nThere is no cited five-minute cutoff that separates harmless pressure from nerve injury. If numbness does not settle promptly and completely, or if you notice weakness, end the ruck and get medical guidance rather than testing the pack again.\n\nReducing shoulder pressure on future rucks\n\nGear can reduce specific sources of friction or poor load distribution, but no product handles a known percentage of brachial-plexus risk. Start with lower load, good fit, padded straps, and an even pack; treat the products below as equipment options, not medical devices.\n\nFit and load choices can reduce risk, but they cannot guarantee prevention.\n\nUse straps that spread pressure comfortably on your body. The 2016 load-carriage review recommends wider, better-padded, properly adjusted straps. The GORUCK GR1 is one option with substantial padding, but its fit still depends on torso, load, adjustment, and whether you add a compatible hip belt. Our best rucking backpacks guide compar"},{"text":"es those fit variables.\n\nUse a hip belt when it fits the pack and your torso well. The same review lists a hip belt among the measures that may reduce strap traction and compression, but there is no universal 20-pound cutoff and no zero-risk setup. If a pack cannot transfer load comfortably, reduce the load before buying around the problem. Body Glide Original can help with skin friction; it does not address nerve compression.\n\nIf your current bag can't hold the weight securely or accommodate a hip belt that fits you, consider those features when replacing it. The GORUCK Rucker 5.0 holds a plate high in an elevated pocket and accepts an optional hip belt. Its full review reports sore traps after a loaded hike without the belt, which is the shoulder-only carry this section is trying to avoid. That observation doesn't show that the belt prevents nerve injury; persistent or recurrent symptoms need assessment before another loaded session.\n\nLet your arms move when terrain allows. If you need to hold the straps to keep the pack stable, revisit the packing and suspension. This is a practical fit cue rather than a clinically tested prevention rule.\n\nCheck fit during long rucks. A 15-to-20"},{"text":"ove symptoms. If you are uncertain, see a clinician. The correct nerve and cause are not always obvious from symptoms alone, and published case series used neurological examination and sometimes electrodiagnostic testing.\n\nManagement and next steps\n\nIf brief tingling settles completely after removing the pack and there was no weakness, review fit and reduce the load before a later session. Repeated episodes need assessment before returning. If a clinician has diagnosed rucksack palsy, follow that plan rather than treating a gear change as the whole intervention.\n\nRecovery is variable. In the Finnish follow-up, 30/38 patients reported no symptoms after a median 4.5 years. Among those with total recovery, the median recovery time was 3.2 months, with a range of 0-9 months. Table 1 records symptoms in 8/38 (21%) and reduced strength in 5/32 examined patients (16%). The abstract's 15% prolonged-symptom figure doesn't match that symptom table. These are different outcomes in a small military series, not a personal recovery forecast.\n\nThe practical measures proposed in the load-carriage review are reducing load, improving symmetry, adjusting and padding straps, and considering a hip belt"},{"text":" that fits correctly. The available sources do not establish a prevention percentage for a commercial pack or fit technique.\n\nMost incidence and recovery data come from military populations carrying demanding loads, and they don't translate into a recreational incidence rate. The lesson that does carry over is to act early on symptoms and not assume a lighter civilian load makes nerve injury impossible.\n\nEvidence boundary: the strongest sources here are military observational studies, case series, and a load-carriage review. They support the mechanism and the fit/load recommendations, but they do not validate a recreational incidence rate, a safe percentage of bodyweight, or a guaranteed prevention effect from any product.\n\nIf your hands start tingling, remove the pack and end the loaded session. Before a later, lighter ruck, symptoms should have settled completely with no weakness. Persistent or recurrent symptoms and any weakness need assessment first; no strap adjustment or timed fit check clears you to return.\n\nFrequently Asked Questions\n\nSome nerve injuries leave lasting symptoms, although these studies don't establish a permanent-damage rate for recreational ruckers. In the F"},{"text":"Walking and strength work are first-line treatments for knee and hip osteoarthritis. How to add a light pack, why RA flares rule it out, and which joint signals mean stop."}]}
{"item_id":"feed:https://ruckauthority.com/pillars/rucking-injury-prevention","version_id":"c4217dc00958b969dee299f020f05279a6cb4b2c1689eb0037f74829dfcbc32f","kind":"feed","publisher_id":"ruckauthority","title":"Rucking Injury Prevention: Common Injuries and Stop Rules","url":"https://ruckauthority.com/pillars/rucking-injury-prevention","published_at":"2026-10-11T00:00:00Z","source_notes":{"perspective":"independent","notes":"Rucking gear reviews (packs, vests, plates, socks, base layers). Affiliate commissions; GORUCK supplied review units. Very high recent volume suggests bulk/SEO publishing; check enclosure MIME types (image enclosures)."},"selection_reason":"Under Relevance, Credibility, and Making the decision, the cited military load-carriage reviews provide specific evidence about hip belts, strap pressure, and load distribution that supports pack suitability for Mammoth's extended carries in Piece 03, despite the article's broader training focus. Commercial incentives and Uncertainty require noting affiliate commissions, GORUCK-supplied review units, and unusually high publishing volume, distinguishing research findings from untested product recommendations, and acknowledging that military conditions differ from Mammoth; official required status remains unspecified under Suitability and official requirements.","excerpted":true,"passages":[{"text":"Free newsletter\n\nRucking notes by email\n\nNew gear reviews and practical training notes. Join now to get the newsletter from its first issue. Unsubscribe anytime.\n\nMilitary load-carriage reviews describe blisters, metatarsal pain, stress fractures, back strain, knee pain, and rucksack palsy. Some problems accumulate over repeated sessions; blisters, nerve symptoms, and acute injuries can occur during one outing.\n\nBuild load one variable at a time. The 10% rule is a sensible planning heuristic, but a systematic review found very limited evidence that any specific weekly increase prevents injury.\n\nAdded pack weight raises ground-reaction forces roughly in proportion to the load, which is why tendons, bones, and feet need time between increases even when your lungs feel ready.\n\nUse a hip belt if your pack has one. Military reviews recommend one whenever possible because it reduces pressure on the shoulders, and it is one of the recommended steps against numb hands.\n\nStop for sharp or worsening pain, swelling, a limp, numbness, or weakness. Persistent nerve symptoms after removing the pack need prompt assessment; severe pain, major swelling, or inability to bear weight needs urgent help"},{"text":".\n\nReduce risk with manageable loads and good fit\n\nRucking is walking, and walking is one of the safest ways to train. The risk comes from the pack: more weight means more force through the feet, knees, and back, and military research on foot marching shows what happens when that load climbs faster than the body adapts. Knapik's 2014 review lists foot blisters, metatarsal pain, knee problems, and back problems as the common complaints, and an earlier review adds stress fractures and rucksack palsy (Knapik et al., 2004). Its prescription is plain: lighter loads, better load distribution, appropriate physical training, and proper equipment.\n\nThe sources reviewed here don't establish an injury rate for recreational rucking. The often-cited running-injury range of about 19% to 79% comes from studies with different definitions and follow-up periods, not a single yearly risk (van Gent et al., 2007). Military findings also reflect loads and conditions that differ from a recreational walk. Some injuries develop through repeated loading; blisters, nerve symptoms, and acute injuries can arise during one outing. Gradual progression, pack fit, and appropriate footwear are precautions, not a gu"},{"text":"arantee against injury.\n\nThis guide is educational, not medical. If you have an injury, pain that persists, or a pre-existing condition, see a clinician for diagnosis and a plan that fits you.\n\nDisclosure: some retailer links in this guide are affiliate links. If you buy through them, Ruck Authority may earn a commission at no extra cost to you. Safety recommendations do not change based on commission.\n\nAssess your risk\n\nThis tool uses editorial rules about load, experience and symptoms. Its low/moderate/high labels are not validated predictions of injury risk or medical clearance. Symptoms take priority over the result.\n\nThe most common rucking injuries\n\nA common complaint, especially in beginners. Pain around the kneecap or the outside of the knee usually follows weight added too fast, long downhill stretches, or a stride that reaches too far.\n\nPrevention: Add load in small steps and change one thing at a time (the 10% rule is a reasonable planning guide, though no exact percentage has been shown to prevent injury). Shorten your stride on descents. Squats, lunges, and glute bridges 2-3 times a week build the capacity the knee needs.\n\nFor the full clinical breakdown and return-to-"},{"text":"rucking progression, read Rucking knee pain.\n\nDiffuse, activity-related pain along the inner edge of the shinbone. A meta-analysis of risk factors found higher odds in women, heavier people, people with more foot-arch drop, and people with a previous running injury (Reinking et al., 2017). Rapid jumps in distance, load, or hills are the usual trigger.\n\nPrevention: Build volume gradually. A 2025 meta-analysis of prevention trials found the strongest support for neuromuscular training (strength and balance work), moderate support for overpronation insoles in people who need them, and no benefit from static stretching or shock-absorbing insoles (Marques et al., 2025). Focal bone pain, pain at rest, or pain at night needs a clinician, because it can be a stress fracture.\n\nFor early-warning signs and the recovery protocol, see Rucking shin splints.\n\nHeel or arch pain, worst with the first steps in the morning or after sitting. It is an overload problem of the plantar fascia, often after rapid volume increases, a sudden change of footwear, or tight, weak calves.\n\nPrevention: Supportive shoes, with any big change in drop or cushioning introduced gradually. Progress distance slowly. Calf s"},{"text":"trengthening, such as slow heel raises, alongside calf mobility.\n\nFor the full protocol, see Rucking plantar fasciitis.\n\nPain in the lower back during or after rucking. Usually mechanical: weight sitting low and far from the spine, excessive forward lean, or a trunk that tires before the legs do. Pain that radiates down a leg, or comes with numbness or weakness, needs assessment. If your hip flexors feel tight after long rucks, see our hip flexor guide for ruckers.\n\nPrevention: Keep the load close to your back. Military research favors high placement on even ground and low or mid-back placement on uneven terrain (Knapik et al., 2004). Stand tall rather than folding forward at the hips. Core work: planks, dead bugs, bird dogs.\n\nSoreness in the upper traps and shoulders from strap pressure or poor load distribution. Common causes are a load that sits low or away from your back, straps that dig in, and long distances with all the weight on your shoulders and no hip belt.\n\nPrevention: Use a hip belt whenever the pack has one. Military load-carriage reviews recommend it because it reduces pressure on the shoulders and improves comfort (Knapik et al., 2004). Keep the shoulders relaxed, a"},{"text":"nd check strap width and padding for your build.\n\nTingling or numbness in the fingers or arm during rucking. It is a compression or traction injury of the brachial plexus nerves, usually from straps pressing into the shoulder under a heavy load.\n\nPrevention: Lighter loads, wider and better-padded straps, and a hip belt, which a 2016 review lists among the prevention steps (Knapik et al., 2016). If numbness, tingling, or weakness starts, stop loaded walking and remove the pack. Persistent or recurring symptoms need assessment before another loaded session.\n\nFull clinical explanation: Numb hands while rucking.\n\nFoot blisters head the list of common injuries in military load-carriage reviews. They're most likely early on, with new shoes, wet feet, cotton socks, or a long ruck at a new load.\n\nPrevention: Merino or synthetic moisture-wicking socks, never cotton. Remove wet socks immediately after rucking. Break in new shoes on short rucks first. Apply Body Glide Original to heels, sides of feet, and toes before heading out.\n\nThe prevention framework: progress one variable at a time\n\nChange one thing at a time, in small steps, and repeat the session before adding more. Weight, distance, "},{"text":"n changes. Increase only when recent sessions remain comfortable, your gait is normal, and recovery is stable. A review date doesn't prove that bones or joints are ready for more weight.\n\nJoint considerations: Pre-existing arthritis, lower bone density, or previous joint injuries may require medical clearance and specific modifications.\n\nThe upside: Weight-bearing exercise supports bone health, though the evidence for loaded walking specifically is mixed. A 5-year program of weighted-vest exercise plus jumping maintained hip bone density in postmenopausal women (Snow et al., 2000), while a 2025 trial found that wearing a weighted vest during weight loss did not prevent hip bone loss in older adults (Beavers et al., 2025). Rucking is good training; it isn't an osteoporosis treatment.\n\nSee our rucking for seniors guide for age-specific progressions and research-backed recommendations.\n\nRucking with previous injuries\n\nBack injuries: Get medical clearance from a healthcare professional. Start with 5-10 pounds. Make core strengthening part of the plan, and avoid rucking if it reproduces your original pain.\n\nKnee injuries: Use a shorter stride (faster cadence, shorter steps). Stay lighte"},{"text":"r on weight. Do quad strengthening regularly. Consider a knee brace if recommended by a professional.\n\nShoulder injuries: Use a pack with a hip belt so some of the load comes off your shoulders. Keep loads light, and load the shoulder straps gradually as you progress.\n\nArthritis: Exercise carries a strong recommendation in the 2019 American College of Rheumatology guideline for hip and knee osteoarthritis; how much pack weight suits you depends on the joint. Our guide to rucking with arthritis covers what the research says and how to manage load on affected joints.\n\nAlways get medical clearance before starting rucking if you have significant injury history. Your doctor or physical therapist can give you personalized guidance based on your specific situation.\n\nThe best injury prevention tool is boring: start lighter than you think you need to, progress slower than you want to, keep up the warm-up and prehab, and pay attention to the signals your body is sending. Patience does most of the work.\n\nThe prevention stack\n\nBlisters are among the most common load-carriage injuries, so the feet come first: shoes that fit, merino or synthetic socks, and a small foot-care kit (tape, balm, patc"},{"text":"hes). Gear supports good progression; it can't stand in for it.\n\nThree pieces of kit cover friction, recovery, and monitoring: an anti-chafe balm for hot spots, a foam roller for post-ruck tightness, and a watch that makes it easy to see when your training load is climbing.\n\nGo deeper\n\nRucking knee pain: types, causes, and fixes: biomechanics research, symptom decision tree, and return-to-rucking progression\n\nRucking plantar fasciitis: prevention, treatment phases, and footwear recommendations\n\nRucking shin splints: early signs, what prevention evidence supports, and stress-fracture red flags\n\nRucking foot care and blister prevention: socks, lacing, hot spots, and what to carry\n\nNumb hands while rucking (rucksack palsy): why straps compress the brachial plexus and how to refit the pack\n\nHip flexor stiffness after rucking: what to try and when to get help\n\nRucking with arthritis: what the research says about loaded walking and sore joints\n\nHRV and the vagus nerve for ruckers: using recovery data to decide when to back off\n\nThe rucking prehab routine: 8 exercises targeting feet, knees, hips, back, and shoulders\n\nRucking mobility guide: warm-up, cool-down, and recovery routines\n\nIs ru"},{"text":"eight Calculator"}]}
{"item_id":"feed:https://ruckauthority.com/gear/goruck-mackall-45-first-look","version_id":"1b660b68127b0a769054c99615a30cf590fc83693faafcf0080a51f151655cd6","kind":"feed","publisher_id":"ruckauthority","title":"GORUCK Mackall 45 Review: Tested Under a 20 lb Plate","url":"https://ruckauthority.com/gear/goruck-mackall-45-first-look","published_at":"2026-10-10T00:00:00Z","source_notes":{"perspective":"independent","notes":"Rucking gear reviews (packs, vests, plates, socks, base layers). Affiliate commissions; GORUCK supplied review units. Very high recent volume suggests bulk/SEO publishing; check enclosure MIME types (image enclosures)."},"selection_reason":"Under Relevance, Credibility, and Freshness and novelty, this newly reported shoe test offers citable observations under a 20 lb plate: support and loose-ground traction, initial heel blisters, and rubbing that stopped after break-in. Commercial incentives warrant reduced weight for praise because GORUCK supplied the shoes and the publisher earns affiliate commissions; its high publishing volume also warrants caution, while wet-weather performance and long-term durability remain untested. It supports Piece 01's newly reviewed gear, Piece 02's comparison with GORUCK's out-of-box readiness claim, and Piece 03's practical advice to test heel fit on shorter loaded walks, with Uncertainty requiring attributed findings rather than broad Mammoth suitability conclusions.","excerpted":false,"passages":[{"text":"Free newsletter\n\nRucking notes by email\n\nNew gear reviews and practical training notes. Join now to get the newsletter from its first issue. Unsubscribe anytime.\n\nRuck Authority Score\n\n7.6/10\n\nMy verdict\n\nA dense, supportive sole and a sharp suede upper, on the trail or with jeans\n\nThe GORUCK Mackall 45 is great underfoot and great looking: a dense, supportive sole with trail grip under a suede shoe that still looks normal with jeans. I wore the Fog colorway with a loaded GR1. The heel rubbed on the first outing, but after break-in the rubbing stopped on a later loaded walk. The support and grip are what stayed with me.\n\nWhat I liked\n\nThe sole felt great under my GR1 and a 20 lb plate: plenty of support, like standing on a dense memory foam mattress\n\nThe tread bites into rough, loose ground like a trail shoe, so I felt sure-footed climbing\n\nI love the look: Fog suede, a leather spearhead and orange accents on a shoe that still looks normal with jeans\n\nWhat held it back\n\nThe heel rubbed on my first loaded outing; after break-in, the rubbing stopped on a later loaded walk\n\nThe test\n\nKey findings\n\nThe Mackall 45 in Fog is a good-looking suede rucking shoe, and the sole felt dense and "},{"text":"supportive under my GR1 with a 20 lb plate.\n\nGORUCK builds it on an EVA foam midsole with a three-compound rubber outsole, a 13 mm drop and a removable insert.\n\nThe tread bit into rough, loose ground, and the Fog suede with orange accents looks sharp with jeans.\n\nThe heel rubbed on my first loaded outing, then stopped rubbing on a later loaded walk after break-in.\n\nFrom the field\n\nOriginal photos from my testing. Free to use with credit and a link to this review.\n\nHow I wore them\n\nWhat I liked underfoot\n\nThe Fog suede\n\nHeel fit: the part to get right\n\nHow other reviewers found the fit\n\nWarranty and care\n\nShould you buy the Mackall 45?\n\nFrequently asked questions\n\nIs the GORUCK Mackall 45 comfortable?\n\nDoes the Mackall 45 need a break-in period?\n\nIs the Mackall 45 a backpack?\n\nHow high is the Mackall 45's heel drop?\n\nIs the Mackall 45 waterproof?\n\nDoes the GORUCK warranty cover the Mackall 45?\n\nThe GORUCK Mackall 45 is a shoe built for rucking that still looks normal with jeans. The 45 is for 1945 and Camp Mackall's wartime history, and you can see the old trainer shape in it. After a loaded outing in them, the part I'd lead with is underneath: the bottom of the shoe is great. Plent"},{"text":"y of support, and it feels like standing on a dense memory foam mattress.\n\nGORUCK sent me this pair in the Fog colorway, men's size 12, which is my usual shoe size. GORUCK didn't approve what I say here or ask for specific coverage. Links to GORUCK on this page may earn us a commission.\n\nHow I wore them\n\nThis review covers that first outing and a later loaded walk after break-in, so it's about fit, feel and grip; long-term durability and wet weather are still ahead of them.\n\nWhat I liked underfoot\n\nThe sole is the highlight. It has plenty of support for a low-cut shoe, and with the GR1 and a plate on my back it still felt like that dense memory foam mattress.\n\nGORUCK describes the midsole as a low-durometer EVA foam and pairs it with a dual-density insert, so the support comes from the combination rather than a hard sole. On my feet, under the added weight, it gave a little and then held. That's the \"dense\" half of my mattress comparison.\n\nOn rougher terrain, the texture on the bottom was enough to dig in and give me stability. I felt comfortable climbing rough ground because I knew I wasn't going to lose my footing. The lugs bite like a trail shoe's, and there's a good amount of g"},{"text":"rip.\n\nThat grip comes from an outsole built for more than one job. The lugs are deep enough to dig into dirt and loose ground, on a shoe GORUCK still designed to go from the trail to errands. Three rubber compounds generally let a maker put grippier rubber where traction matters and tougher rubber where the sole wears fastest.\n\nHere's what GORUCK lists for the Fog version:\n\nSource: GORUCK's Mackall 45 Fog product page, checked September 25, 2026.\n\nThe 13 mm drop puts your heel higher than your toes. GORUCK says it chose that offset to reduce strain on the calves and Achilles. A higher drop does ask the lower leg to stretch a little less on each step; whether that suits you depends on your stride and the shoes you're used to.\n\nThe Fog suede\n\nIt's a really good-looking shoe. The pigskin suede upper, the leather spearhead on the side and the reinforced double stitching at the toe and heel give it that older trainer character. I'm a big fan of the gray with the orange accents. That may be a millennial thing; it's roughly how I've accented my house.\n\nSuede is sanded leather, which is where the soft nap comes from, and it generally softens to your foot with wear. Fog is the lighter color"},{"text":"way, so a suede brush is worth keeping by the door after a dusty trail. If you'd rather not think about it, Black + Gum hides dirt.\n\nHeel fit: the part to get right\n\nOn my first loaded outing, the back of the heel rubbed, and you can see where on my pair: the collar lining there is roughed up, with some pilling. I finished with blisters and wore my old shoes on the next outing. After breaking the Mackalls in, I took them on another loaded walk and the rubbing stopped.\n\nThat was a break-in issue for my feet, not an ongoing problem with this pair. A different heel may feel comfortable from the start, or need a different fit altogether.\n\nGORUCK designs the Mackall to be ready out of the box, and SEALgrinderPT reported a secure heel from the start. Mine needed time; now the support and traction are the story.\n\nIf a new pair rubs your heel, try it on shorter walks before committing to a long loaded route. Check heel hold with your usual socks; our foot care guide covers lacing and hot spots. Break-in solved my rubbing, but it won't fix every fit problem.\n\nHow other reviewers found the fit\n\nOther reviewers have had a smoother start, which fits the different-heels point. These are their a"},{"text":"ccounts, on different colorways, not mine.\n\nSEALgrinderPT wore the Black + Gum version and reported a comfortable initial fit, a secure heel and a planted feel in the first hours.\n\nMindBodyDad wore the Americana for rucking and yard work. He liked the support under load and the traction, and wanted a little more room in the forefoot.\n\nPut together with my follow-up, the sole and traction remain the clear strengths. Heel fit varies from foot to foot; mine settled after break-in.\n\nFor how the Mackall compares with other shoes on pavement, trail and mixed routes, see our rucking shoes by terrain guide.\n\nWarranty and care\n\nGORUCK's SCARS guarantee works differently for footwear than for its bags. The footwear terms cover manufacturing defects and exclude normal wear, which is narrower than the lifetime guarantee on its packs.\n\nTo clean them, GORUCK says to take the insert out, rinse the shoes and let them air dry, with no washer or dryer. GORUCK doesn't list the Mackall 45 as waterproof.\n\nShould you buy the Mackall 45?\n\nBuy it if:\n\nYou want one low-cut shoe for loaded walks and everyday wear. It's built for rucking and still looks normal with jeans.\n\nYou like a dense, supportive sole. "},{"text":"That was the best part of the shoe for me under a 20 lb plate.\n\nYou ruck on rough or loose ground. The tread dug in like a trail shoe's and gave me solid footing on climbs.\n\nYou like a raised heel under a pack. GORUCK lists a 13 mm drop, which it says is meant to ease strain on the calves and Achilles.\n\nSkip it if:\n\nYou want a flatter shoe or a different style. Our rucking shoes by terrain guide covers other picks for pavement, trail and mixed routes.\n\nYour routes are often wet. A waterproof trail shoe is the better tool there, and the same terrain guide covers those.\n\nYou mostly walk without a pack. A cushioned daily trainer is the better tool, and Kit Authority ranks the best walking shoes for that kind of mileage.\n\nFrequently asked questions\n\nIs the GORUCK Mackall 45 comfortable?\n\nYes, underfoot especially. The sole felt dense and supportive with a loaded GR1 on my back, like standing on a dense memory foam mattress. My heel rubbed at first but stopped on a later loaded walk after break-in.\n\nDoes the Mackall 45 need a break-in period?\n\nGORUCK designs it to be ready out of the box. Mine rubbed on the first loaded outing; after break-in, the rubbing stopped on a later loaded walk."},{"text":" Your heel may differ.\n\nIs the Mackall 45 a backpack?\n\nNo. It's a low-cut rucking and training shoe. The name refers to Camp Mackall and 1945, not a 45-liter pack.\n\nHow high is the Mackall 45's heel drop?\n\nGORUCK lists a 13 mm heel-to-toe drop, so the heel sits higher than the forefoot. GORUCK says the offset is meant to reduce strain on the calves and Achilles.\n\nIs the Mackall 45 waterproof?\n\nNo. GORUCK doesn't list it as waterproof, and the suede upper is perforated across the forefoot. For routes that are often wet, a waterproof trail shoe is the better pick.\n\nDoes the GORUCK warranty cover the Mackall 45?\n\nGORUCK's footwear coverage is for manufacturing defects. It excludes normal wear, so it's narrower than the guarantee on GORUCK's bags.\n\nSpecs, photos and where to buy\n\nHands-on tested · Gifted by GORUCK\n\nGORUCK Mackall 45\n\nGORUCK's heritage-styled rucking shoe. Underfoot it's dense and supportive, the tread bites on rough ground and the Fog suede looks sharp off the trail.\n\nDense, supportive cushioning underfoot\n\nTread that bites into rough, loose ground like a trail shoe\n\nHeritage suede look that works off the trail too\n\nReinforced, double-stitched toe and heel with a three"},{"text":"-compound outsole\n\nRemovable dual-density insert\n\nThe heel rubbed on my first loaded outing, though it stopped after break-in on a later loaded walk\n\nYou're welcome to quote the findings or use the original photos with credit to Ruck Authority and a link to this page.\n\nRelated Articles\n\nGORUCK Rucker 5.0 Review: 25L Tested With a 20 lb Plate\n\nHands-on GORUCK Rucker 5.0 review: the 25L with a 20 lb plate on hilly Wisconsin trails, the lumbar pad, plate pockets, and how it beats the GR1 and GR2.\n\nGORUCK Rucker 5.0 vs 4.0: Should You Upgrade?\n\nWhat changed from the GORUCK Rucker 4.0 to the 5.0, what the new lumbar pad and magnetic pockets felt like on my back, and who should upgrade.\n\nGORUCK Achilles 30 lb Weighted Vest: Hands-On Review\n\nMy hands-on review of the 30 lb GORUCK Achilles: quick dog walks, flexible sewn-in weight, fit adjustment, pellet noise and shoulder fatigue.\n\nGORUCK Spy Ruck 12 lb: Hands-On Review\n\nI like the black 12 lb Spy Ruck for comfortable weighted walks. Its sliding straps let me set the fit, but the load is permanent and essentials need a separate place."}]}
{"item_id":"feed:https://ruckauthority.com/training/weighted-vest-vs-rucksack","version_id":"ab775725b47f4b9f9ef5bacc991ec90e412d48130bd2a8de435e5af8b3fec077","kind":"feed","publisher_id":"ruckauthority","title":"Weighted Vest vs Rucksack: Which Is Better for Fitness?","url":"https://ruckauthority.com/training/weighted-vest-vs-rucksack","published_at":"2026-10-10T00:00:00Z","source_notes":{"perspective":"independent","notes":"Rucking gear reviews (packs, vests, plates, socks, base layers). Affiliate commissions; GORUCK supplied review units. Very high recent volume suggests bulk/SEO publishing; check enclosure MIME types (image enclosures)."},"selection_reason":"Under Relevance, Credibility, and Uncertainty, the cargo, fit, and load-bearing belt distinctions support Mammoth carry decisions, while the described studies and calculator limitations support only qualified comparisons, not equivalent vest-and-pack outcomes. Freshness and novelty is supplied by the October correction withdrawing unsupported claims; Commercial incentives requires flagging affiliate commissions, GORUCK-supplied review units, and unusually high publishing volume. This supports Piece 03's practical tradeoffs, with required status unspecified under Suitability and official requirements because no official Mammoth passage is supplied.","excerpted":false,"passages":[{"text":"Free newsletter\n\nRucking notes by email\n\nNew gear reviews and practical training notes. Join now to get the newsletter from its first issue. Unsubscribe anytime.\n\nA pack carries weight plus gear; a vest keeps the load around the torso and can suit exercise circuits.\n\nEqual pounds don't prove identical calorie burn, comfort, posture or long-term results.\n\nVest studies haven't established equivalent bone-density benefits for backpacks.\n\nFit, weight adjustability and the equipment required by your event are more useful buying criteria than a universal winner.\n\nChoose for what you need to carry\n\nA rucksack gives you room for water, a jacket and other gear as well as weight. A vest keeps the load around your torso and may be easier to manage during an exercise circuit. That's a useful distinction without pretending either is universally better for your body.\n\nDoes the same weight burn the same calories?\n\nBoth add mass to the walk. That alone doesn't establish identical energy use. Placement, fit, restriction, pace and the person can matter.\n\nOur calorie calculator uses the Pandolf load-carriage model. It has no vest-versus-pack input. Matching numbers from that calculator are therefore "},{"text":"a model limitation, not a head-to-head experiment proving the devices equivalent.\n\nWhat the research actually compares\n\nFat and bone outcomes across nine vest trials\n\nNine randomized trials, 368 middle-aged and older adults\n\nVest + exercise vs usual activity-2.67 kg\n\nVest added to existing exercise-0.73 kg\n\nFemoral neck (hip)+0.59\n\nLumbar spine+0.73\n\nAdded to existing exerciseNo confirmed benefit (1 trial)\n\nFat-mass evidence graded high certainty; bone outcomes low to moderate. Trials used vests, not packs, in middle-aged and older adults.\n\nWang and colleagues' 2026 review pooled nine trials with 368 middle-aged and older adults. Vest-plus-exercise programs improved some bone outcomes versus usual activity, with low-to-moderate certainty. Against exercise without a vest, the extra bone benefit wasn't clear in the one trial contributing that comparison. These weren't trials of packs against vests, or a single walking-only protocol. Our bone-density guide explains that distinction.\n\nBarman and colleagues measured spinal movement in 47 adults carrying a light front load versus no load. It changed trunk position and lumbar curvature. They didn't compare a balanced walking vest with a b"},{"text":"ackpack, so the study can't prove that a vest fixes posture.\n\nOrr and colleagues compared three military packs in 12 soldiers carrying 30 kg. Selected physiological and performance measures didn't differ significantly. A small study of three backpacks isn't evidence that all packs and vests feel or perform the same.\n\nSee the 2026 research review for study designs and limitations.\n\nFit and loading matter more than category slogans\n\nA pack sits behind you, and your body adjusts to the load. A vest may carry weight front and back, around the torso or in a fixed pattern. Designs differ; neither category guarantees an upright spine or a particular distribution of muscle work.\n\nA suitable load-bearing hip belt can transfer some pack weight away from the shoulders. Not every waist strap does that, and there isn't a threshold at which all vests become uncomfortable while all packs stay comfortable. Heavier vests rest largely on the shoulders, so fit matters more as the load climbs.\n\nBoth can become uncomfortable. Remove or reduce load when it changes gait or causes symptoms. Existing shoulder, back, heart, lung or balance problems call for individual guidance rather than a different produc"},{"text":"t category as an assumed fix.\n\nWhen to choose a pack\n\nFor a longer walk with supplies, a pack is the practical choice. It also matches the equipment used in many ruck events. Check the actual event requirements rather than treating every obstacle race as a backpack event.\n\nThe 5.11 RUSH 24 offers cargo space, but it doesn't have a dedicated ruck-plate pocket or an included load-bearing hip belt. Secure the weight properly. A purpose-built rucking backpack may offer a more convenient loading system.\n\nWhen to choose a vest\n\nA vest can be convenient for a short walk or exercises where you want less cargo bulk. Check that it stays put during the movements you intend to do. Some have removable small weights, some take specific plates, and others have weight sewn in. Don't assume you can reduce a fixed 20 or 30 lb vest after buying it.\n\nOur CAP Barbell review covers an adjustable option; the Hyperwear Hyper Vest PRO offers a different low-profile design. For fit alternatives, see the women's gear guide. A women's pattern may help fit, but the label doesn't guarantee comfort.\n\nDo you need both?\n\nOnly if they solve different problems for you. A pack for gear and a vest for circuits can mak"},{"text":"e sense. You can also keep using one comfortable setup. Equipment variety is optional; managing the total workload still matters if you alternate.\n\nFrequently asked questions\n\nThe mass matches, but identical results haven't been established. Fit, placement, movement and the training program matter. Our calculator can't resolve the comparison because it doesn't model equipment type.\n\nNo. Check the exact vest's compatibility. Some take specific plates, others use small removable weights, and fixed-weight models aren't intended for reloading.\n\nNeither is a proven universal winner. Vest exercise studies don't establish the same effect from a pack, and a change in posture during loaded walking isn't proof of a lasting improvement.\n\nThere isn't a universal cutoff. Fit, duration and belt design matter. A loose stabilizing strap and a load-bearing hip belt don't do the same job.\n\nFor another layout comparison, see Ruck Plate Carrier 3.0 versus Rucker 5.0.\n\nCorrection, October 9, 2026: Removed claims of identical fitness outcomes, automatic posture benefits and universal comfort thresholds. The calculator and research sections now state what was actually compared.\n\nRelated Articles\n\nIndoor "},{"text":"Rucking Plan: A 4-Week Treadmill Program\n\nA four-week treadmill rucking plan that holds pack load steady, separates incline from duration progressions, and uses two loaded sessions plus unloaded aerobic work.\n\nFall and Winter Rucking: Indoor Options, Dark Evenings and Events\n\nChoose a safer route as daylight and weather change: indoor alternatives, cold-weather preparation, after-dark checks and verified fall 2026 events.\n\nIs Rucking Bad for Your Back? What the Science Says\n\nLearn how load, pack fit, training volume, and existing back conditions affect spinal stress during recreational rucking.\n\nHill Ruck Training: Elevation Workouts for Every Terrain\n\nHow to add hills to a rucking week: uphill and downhill form, five hill and stair workouts, treadmill and garage options for flat areas, and how to progress safely."}]}
{"item_id":"feed:https://ruckauthority.com/weight-loss/rucking-for-women","version_id":"20f721d5647cef2e73060b9f7115ccfde4156b261a5ccc48086e1118abbd3427","kind":"feed","publisher_id":"ruckauthority","title":"Rucking for Women: Fit, Starting Weight, and Training Guidance","url":"https://ruckauthority.com/weight-loss/rucking-for-women","published_at":"2026-10-10T00:00:00Z","source_notes":{"perspective":"independent","notes":"Rucking gear reviews (packs, vests, plates, socks, base layers). Affiliate commissions; GORUCK supplied review units. Very high recent volume suggests bulk/SEO publishing; check enclosure MIME types (image enclosures)."},"selection_reason":"Under Relevance, Credibility, and Suitability and official requirements, the loaded-pack fit checks and cited study of seven women carrying military packs provide specific evidence relevant to Mammoth load carriage, supporting Piece 03’s advice to test harness and hip-belt fit under load. Under Commercial incentives, the publisher earns affiliate commissions and has received GORUCK review units; its high publishing volume warrants caution, and this item supplies neither independent product testing nor Mammoth-specific experience. Under Uncertainty, use the fit evidence with attribution, without extending the general fitness and health guidance into Mammoth performance claims.","excerpted":true,"passages":[{"text":"Free newsletter\n\nRucking notes by email\n\nNew gear reviews and practical training notes. Join now to get the newsletter from its first issue. Unsubscribe anytime.\n\nA useful first load is often 5-10 lb for 20-30 minutes. The right starting point leaves your gait and posture unchanged.\n\nBuy for your measured torso length and how the harness fits under load; a men's or women's label is only a starting point.\n\nRucking raises the work of walking, but there is no credible women-only fat-loss shortcut. Bone-health evidence from weighted-vest trials is mixed, and no trial has tested a rucksack.\n\nResearch finds menstrual-cycle effects on performance trivial on average, with no basis for a universal phase-based rule. Adjust to the symptoms you actually have.\n\nWomen do not need a separate set of rucking laws. The durable rules are the same for everyone: start with a load you can control, keep it close to your back, wear a pack that fits, and increase only one variable at a time.\n\nWhere women can run into different problems is equipment fit. Torso length, shoulder-strap spacing, chest-strap position, hip shape, pregnancy or postpartum status, and previous training all change how a loaded pack f"},{"text":"eels. Those are individual measurements and circumstances, not a reason to assume every woman needs the same pack or training plan.\n\nA sensible starting weight\n\nFor a first session, try 5-10 lb for 20-30 minutes on level ground. Treat it as a test load; there's no need to outgrow it quickly.\n\nUse the lower end if you are returning from inactivity, have a smaller frame, are postpartum, or have a history of foot, knee, hip, back, or pelvic-floor symptoms. A person with years of strength and hiking experience may be comfortable beginning above that range, but the first session should still feel controlled.\n\nThe useful checks are visible and repeatable:\n\nYour normal walking gait does not change.\n\nYou can stand tall without leaning forward.\n\nThe pack does not bounce, pull away from your back, or create numbness.\n\nYou finish feeling as though you could have continued another 10 minutes.\n\nOrdinary muscle fatigue settles; joint pain, tingling, numbness, or increasing back pain is a stop signal, and symptoms that persist after you stop deserve a clinician's look.\n\nA classic review of load carriage found that energy cost, forward trunk lean and ground forces all rise as pack weight goes up ("},{"text":"Knapik et al., 1996). Fit can need individual attention too: in a small study of seven women carrying military packs at up to 50 lb, some needed the padded hip belt modified so the weight spread evenly around the pelvis (Ling et al., 2004). Neither study supports one load percentage that is right for every woman.\n\nTreat the calculator result as a planning ceiling. It isn't an instruction to carry that much on day one.\n\nFit the pack to your body\n\nMeasure your torso from the prominent C7 vertebra at the base of your neck to the line between the tops of your hip bones. Then try the pack with weight inside it. Height and gender labels are only shortcuts; the loaded fit is what counts.\n\nCheck these five points:\n\nHarness range: The pack's stated torso range includes your measurement.\n\nShoulder spacing: The straps wrap without cutting into the neck or sitting at the edge of the shoulders.\n\nSternum strap: It can sit comfortably across the upper chest without pressure on breast tissue.\n\nLoad position: Dense weight stays high, secure, and close to the back instead of sagging away from it.\n\nHip belt, when used: It lands on the hip crest and transfers load without digging into the abdomen.\n\nWo"},{"text":"men-specific harnesses can help when their strap shape or torso range matches you, but a smaller unisex ruck may fit better than a poorly matched women's model. Try both when possible.\n\nThese are three useful starting points for fit, load, and friction management:\n\nOur women's rucking backpack guide compares torso range, harness design, and load control in more detail.\n\nFat loss and bone health: what the evidence can say\n\nAdding load raises the energy cost of walking. That can make rucking a useful part of a fat-loss plan, particularly if it is more enjoyable and repeatable than running. It does not create a women-specific hormonal fat-loss effect. Long-term weight change still depends on total activity, food intake, recovery, and consistency.\n\nThe bone-health case deserves similar restraint. Weight-bearing and resistance exercise are important for bone and muscle health, but no trial has tested rucking for bone, and the weighted-vest trials point in different directions:\n\nA 2026 meta-analysis of nine trials in middle-aged and older adults found that vest-plus-exercise programs raised hip and spine bone density compared with usual activity, but adding a vest to exercise people alre"},{"text":"ady did showed no confirmed extra bone benefit, and the bone evidence was rated low to moderate certainty (Wang et al., 2026).\n\nIn the OsteoACTIVE trial, 80 postmenopausal women with low bone density and a healed wrist fracture did six months of weight-vest exercise plus education. A year on, their strength, balance and bone density were no different from a group that got education alone (Hakestad et al., 2015).\n\nIn a 2025 randomized trial of 150 older adults, three-quarters of them women, wearing a weighted vest did not prevent hip bone loss during intentional weight loss (Beavers et al., 2025).\n\nThat makes rucking a reasonable form of weight-bearing exercise. It isn't a proven osteoporosis treatment, and anyone with osteoporosis, a previous fragility fracture, or a high fracture risk should get individualized guidance from a clinician before adding external load.\n\nMenstrual cycles and training\n\nYou may notice that energy, cramps, temperature tolerance, sleep, or perceived effort change during the month. Track those patterns if they affect your rucks, but do not assume a calendar phase automatically requires a heavier or lighter session.\n\nA 2020 systematic review and meta-analysis"},{"text":"\n\nACOG notes that pelvic floor exercises can begin in the immediate postpartum period; a pelvic-health physical therapist can help you judge when adding a pack makes sense. Walking with a pack is lower impact than running, but that doesn't make it symptom-proof: the extra load is carried through the trunk and pelvis, and pelvic symptoms are a signal to scale back.\n\nA conservative four-week start\n\nThis plan is suitable for a healthy beginner who can already walk for 30 minutes without pain. It is intentionally modest.\n\nKeep the route level at first. Add time before pace, hills, or load. If form changes or discomfort accumulates between sessions, repeat the week or step back.\n\nA lighter load that keeps you training is more useful than a heavier load that turns every walk into a test. The goal of the first month is to establish a repeatable baseline.\n\nWomen can and do adapt to demanding load carriage. In a study of 11 recreationally active women, ten weeks of physical training lowered the oxygen cost of a 5 km walk carrying a 23 kg (about 50 lb) vest and improved push-up and lower-body strength scores (Wills et al., 2020). It supports gradual practice; it doesn't argue for a heavy fir"},{"text":"st day.\n\nIf you lift and ruck on the same day, lift first. In a small 2026 crossover trial, a fast 4.8 km walk carrying 30% of body mass lowered deadlift strength in women more than the same walk without a pack, while in men the drop was similar either way (Lavoie et al., 2026). With 20 people and one heavy load, it's a hypothesis rather than a rule, but it points the same way as a 2026 meta-analysis of 23 studies showing that loaded marches reduce leg and trunk strength afterward (Sax van der Weyden et al., 2026).\n\nRelated reading\n\nRucking research 2026: includes the small 2026 trial in which a loaded walk lowered women's deadlift strength more than an unloaded one\n\nBest rucking backpacks for women: compare torso range, harness shape, and load support\n\nBest rucking gear for women: footwear, socks, and friction-management options\n\nRucking for bone density: the evidence and its limits\n\nWomen's rucking loadout: a complete setup with exact product destinations\n\nFirst 30 days of rucking: a longer progression after the four-week baseline\n\nFrequently Asked Questions\n\nNot automatically. A women-specific harness can be useful when its torso range and strap shape match you, but a smaller un"},{"text":"isex pack may fit just as well. Measure your torso and test the pack under load instead of buying by label alone.\n\nUse less weight or begin with unweighted walking. The starting range is a test, not a minimum. Progress only after your gait, posture, and recovery remain normal.\n\nOnly if your actual symptoms or effort warrant it. Research does not support a universal phase-based loading rule. Track your own response and adjust the session in front of you.\n\nRucking is endurance-oriented load carriage, not a bodybuilding program. It can improve work capacity and muscular endurance. Large increases in muscle size generally require sustained, progressive resistance training plus adequate nutrition.\n\nThere is no evidence-based maximum that applies to every woman. Body size, training history, route, duration, pack fit, and medical history all matter. For general fitness, there is little reason to chase a maximum; use the lightest load that creates the training effect you want without changing gait or causing pain.\n\nEither is possible. A well-fitted, modest load may be tolerated, while a heavy or poorly positioned load can aggravate symptoms. Existing or recurrent back pain deserves individ"},{"text":"ualized advice, and increasing pain, weakness, or numbness is a reason to stop and get assessed by a clinician.\n\nRelated Articles\n\nRucking Heart Rate Zones: Zone 2 and Beyond\n\nA rucking heart rate zone guide: how Zones 1 to 5 apply to loaded walking, how load, grade, terrain and heat shift your effort, and how to program Zone 2 rucks.\n\nRucking vs Running for Cardio: Which Is Better?\n\nRucking vs running for cardio, fat loss, injury risk, and long-term fitness. Includes calorie tables, adaptation framework, and when to choose each.\n\nCan Rucking Be Your Only Exercise? What It Covers and Misses\n\nRucking covers aerobic fitness and loaded endurance, but it misses upper-body strength, power, and full-range mobility. Learn how to fill the gaps.\n\nLong-Term Rucking for Weight Loss: A Periodization Approach\n\nAn 18-week rucking plan in three phases that progresses load, volume and intensity with planned recovery, plus how to pair each phase with a sensible calorie deficit."}]}
{"item_id":"feed:https://ruckauthority.com/gear/goruck-rucker-5-0-review","version_id":"66af62696466704fa6aa3667b98d7310f8b80deddad80459b13077cda96833c3","kind":"feed","publisher_id":"ruckauthority","title":"GORUCK Rucker 5.0 Review: 25L Tested With a 20 lb Plate","url":"https://ruckauthority.com/gear/goruck-rucker-5-0-review","published_at":"2026-10-09T00:00:00Z","source_notes":{"perspective":"independent","notes":"Rucking gear reviews (packs, vests, plates, socks, base layers). Affiliate commissions; GORUCK supplied review units. Very high recent volume suggests bulk/SEO publishing; check enclosure MIME types (image enclosures)."},"selection_reason":"Under Relevance, Credibility, and Freshness and novelty, the September 2026 hilly-trail outing provides new, attributable observations of pack stability, lumbar comfort, sweat, and subsequent shoulder soreness relevant to Mammoth training and carrying tradeoffs. Commercial incentives warrant reduced weight for praise because GORUCK supplied the pack and the publisher earns affiliate commissions; its high publishing volume also warrants caution, although the described outing supplies specific evidence. Under Suitability and official requirements and Uncertainty, this supports Pieces 01, 02, and 03 with limits: total load and distance were unmeasured, testing covered only initial dry-weather use, hip-belt benefits remain untested, and Mammoth requirements and multi-day suitability are unestablished.","excerpted":true,"passages":[{"text":"Free newsletter\n\nRucking notes by email\n\nNew gear reviews and practical training notes. Join now to get the newsletter from its first issue. Unsubscribe anytime.\n\nRuck Authority Score\n\n8.2/10\n\nMy verdict\n\nFor pure rucking, this is the GORUCK bag I'd pick\n\nThe GORUCK Rucker 5.0 is built for one job, carrying a plate, and it does that job better than any other GORUCK bag I've carried. On a filmed September 2026 hike on hilly Wisconsin trail with a 20 lb plate, the 25L stayed locked to my back and the lumbar pad felt supportive. I carried it without the optional hip belt and my traps were sore afterward, so for heavier loads I'd set up the sternum strap and try the belt.\n\nWhat I liked\n\nStayed solid against my back over uneven, hilly ground with a 20 lb plate and extra gear\n\nThe large lumbar pad felt supportive without demanding attention during the walk\n\nThe 25L left room for camera gear alongside the plate; internal pockets were useful for keys\n\nWhat held it back\n\nMy traps felt sore after I got home; I carried it without the optional hip belt\n\nThe test\n\nKey findings\n\nI carried the 25L GORUCK Rucker 5.0 on uneven, hilly Wisconsin trail with a 20 lb plate plus camera gear and other sup"},{"text":"plies I didn't weigh.\n\nThe GORUCK Rucker 5.0 stayed stable against my back on rough ground, and I didn't notice the plate shifting inside.\n\nThe GORUCK Rucker 5.0's large lumbar pad felt comfortable and supportive, and I soon stopped noticing it while walking.\n\nI carried the GORUCK Rucker 5.0 without its optional hip belt, and my traps were sore once I got home, so for heavier loads I'd add the sternum strap and try the belt.\n\nThe GORUCK Rucker 5.0 has two dedicated plate pockets that hold the plate up high against your back instead of letting it sink to the bottom of the bag.\n\nOf the three GORUCK bags I've carried with a 20 lb plate, the Rucker 5.0 is the better pure ruck, because the GR1 and GR2 hold the plate in an open sleeve and have no lumbar pad.\n\nFrom the field\n\nOriginal photos from my testing. Free to use with credit and a link to this review.\n\nWhat I carried and where I used it\n\nFit, stability and shoulder comfort\n\nThe 25L felt right on my back\n\nThe lumbar pad was less intrusive than it looks\n\nMy traps were sore afterward\n\nExpect contact with your back, and some sweat\n\nPlate storage, pockets and everyday use\n\nFeatures shown in the video\n\nHow it compares with the GR1 and GR"},{"text":"2\n\nWho I would recommend it to\n\nFrequently asked questions\n\nThe GORUCK Rucker 5.0 stayed solid against my back on a hilly, uneven trail with a 20 lb plate and extra gear. I was wobbling around on the ground more than the bag was moving on me. That secure feel is the strongest reason I would choose it for dedicated rucking.\n\nThe large lumbar pad felt supportive, and I soon stopped noticing it while walking. My traps were sore once I got home, which I put down partly to carrying without the optional hip belt; that's the next thing I'll try.\n\nGORUCK supplied this 25L pack for review. The GR1 and GR2 comparison further down comes from my own time with both bags. If you already own a Rucker 4.0, my Rucker 5.0 vs 4.0 upgrade guide covers what changed and whether it's worth replacing.\n\nThe full review film plays at the top of this page. The watch page has chapter jumps and the complete transcript.\n\nWhat I carried and where I used it\n\nThe 20 lb figure is the plate weight. I also carried camera equipment, water and other supplies, but did not weigh the complete load.\n\nThe side pouches visible in some trail footage are third-party accessories. They are not included with the Rucker. The table"},{"text":" breakdown also shows the pack with those attachments removed.\n\nFit, stability and shoulder comfort\n\nThe 25L felt right on my back\n\nGORUCK sent the larger size because I'm 6'2\". The size felt good, and the pack stayed close and stable even where I had to work to keep my own balance. I didn't notice the plate shifting around inside.\n\nI'd give most of the credit to where the plate sits. A plate held high and tight against your spine keeps the weight close to your own center of mass, so when you step down off a root or catch yourself on a slope, it has very little leverage to swing with. The same 20 lb riding low and a few inches off your back works like a longer lever: it pulls you backward on climbs and keeps moving after you've stopped. If you want the same planted feel, snug the shoulder straps until there's no gap between the pack and your back, and pack anything heavy beyond the plate close to the back panel rather than toward the front.\n\nIf you're choosing between the 20L and 25L, pay attention to where the lumbar pad and bottom of the pack sit on your back, as well as how much room you need.\n\nThe lumbar pad was less intrusive than it looks\n\nOn the table, the lumbar pad looks e"},{"text":"normous. On my back, it felt supportive and soon faded into the background. I didn't have any lower-back discomfort during the filmed hike.\n\nMy guess is that it works by filling the hollow of the lower back. Without a pad, the bottom of a pack tends to bridge that curve and rock against you with each step. With one, the pack gets a second point of contact below the shoulder blades, which probably helped the stability as much as the comfort. That only works if the pad lands in the curve. If it sits on your belt or lower, the pack is riding too low or it's the wrong size for your torso, and that's the thing to check before anything else.\n\nMy traps were sore afterward\n\nI didn't notice a specific shoulder problem while walking. The soreness in my traps showed up after I got home. Without a hip belt, the shoulder straps carried the whole load, and they rest right on the upper traps. Those same muscles spend the walk holding your shoulders up against the pull of the pack, so sore traps afterward aren't much of a mystery. My load was also more than 20 lb, since the camera gear and supplies came along unweighed.\n\nNext, I'd like to try the same load with the optional hip belt. My own condit"},{"text":"ioning is probably part of the story too.\n\nIf you get the same thing, the setup is the first place to look. Snug the straps enough that the pack rides high, but not so tight that you're shrugging; your shoulders should sit down and back. The Rucker's removable sternum strap pulls the shoulder straps in toward your chest, which can take pressure off the outer edge of the shoulders and stop the straps sliding outward. Both the sternum strap and a hip belt matter more as the plate gets heavier. On a pack this size, a belt is likely more about stopping sway and taking some pressure off your shoulders than carrying the load the way a framed hiking pack's belt does. Our rucking form guide covers strap setup, and how heavy your ruck should be covers building up the weight gradually.\n\nExpect contact with your back, and some sweat\n\nThe close fit that keeps the Rucker stable also means it sits flat against your back, so like any bag worn this close, it's a warm place to be; my shirt was damp across the back when I took it off. A synthetic or wool shirt and a dry one for the drive home take care of it. In hot weather, plan water and pace too; our summer heat safety guide covers that.\n\nI didn'"},{"text":"rried the GR1 and the GR2 with a 20 lb plate. They're GORUCK's everyday and travel bags, and both hold the plate in an open sleeve with no lumbar pad, which is exactly where the Rucker pulls ahead. For pure plate rucking, it's the one built for the job; the other two win when the bag has more to do than carry weight.\n\nEach column is my own use of that bag with a 20 lb plate, not a side-by-side test. My GR1 review and GR2 review cover each bag in full.\n\nWho I would recommend it to\n\nThe Rucker 5.0 makes sense if you want a dedicated bag for carrying plates and doing ruck workouts. Its secure feel on rough ground, comfortable lumbar support and room for my extra equipment made a positive first impression.\n\nIf you want to compare it with packs from other brands first, our best rucking backpacks guide covers the field.\n\nBuy it if:\n\nMost of what you carry is a plate, water and keys, and you want the plate held high and still.\n\nYou ruck on rough or hilly ground, where a load that stays put matters most.\n\nYou want lumbar support built into the bag rather than a flat back panel.\n\nYou want room for a few extras, like camera gear, alongside the plate without moving up to a bigger bag.\n\nSkip i"},{"text":"pace for camera gear alongside my plate. I have not compared it with the 20L. Choose around torso fit, where the lumbar pad sits and the cargo you need to carry.\n\nNot for stability: mine stayed stable without one. My traps were sore after I got home, though, so for heavier loads I'd try the optional belt along with the sternum strap, which likely takes some pressure off the shoulders.\n\nGORUCK describes it as highly water resistant, and it has drain holes to let water out. It isn't a waterproof case, so on wet days I'd keep electronics in a dry bag.\n\nA 20 lb plate, plus camera gear and other supplies. I didn't weigh the complete load. GORUCK's 75 lb plate-pocket rating is its specification, not a load I carried.\n\nSpecs, photos and where to buy\n\nHands-on tested · Gifted by GORUCK\n\nRucker 5.0\n\nDedicated rucking pack with dual elevated plate pockets, magnetic internal storage and a 30mm lumbar pad. In our review the 25L stayed stable with a 20 lb plate plus gear on hilly trails, and the lumbar pad felt supportive. For heavier loads, add the optional hip belt.\n\nTwo dedicated plate pockets hold the plate high and still\n\nLarge contoured lumbar pad that felt supportive on the trail\n\n1000D "},{"text":"Cordura shell with a lifetime warranty\n\nMagnetic internal pockets and room for camera gear beside the plate\n\nMy traps were sore after a loaded hike without the optional hip belt\n\nYou're welcome to quote the findings or use the original photos with credit to Ruck Authority and a link to this page.\n\nRelated Articles\n\nGORUCK Rucker 5.0 vs 4.0: Should You Upgrade?\n\nWhat changed from the GORUCK Rucker 4.0 to the 5.0, what the new lumbar pad and magnetic pockets felt like on my back, and who should upgrade.\n\nGORUCK Mackall 45 Review: Tested Under a 20 lb Plate\n\nI wore the GORUCK Mackall 45 in Fog with a loaded GR1: a dense, supportive sole, tread that bites on rough ground and a suede upper that works with jeans.\n\nGORUCK Achilles 30 lb Weighted Vest: Hands-On Review\n\nMy hands-on review of the 30 lb GORUCK Achilles: quick dog walks, flexible sewn-in weight, fit adjustment, pellet noise and shoulder fatigue.\n\nGORUCK Spy Ruck 12 lb: Hands-On Review\n\nI like the black 12 lb Spy Ruck for comfortable weighted walks. Its sliding straps let me set the fit, but the load is permanent and essentials need a separate place."}]}
{"item_id":"feed:https://ruckauthority.com/gear/goruck-rucker-5-0-vs-4-0-review","version_id":"54479d2596b49a0ecdf91bff88af124cc281bbeee27e613103538767d586a5d2","kind":"feed","publisher_id":"ruckauthority","title":"GORUCK Rucker 5.0 vs 4.0: Should You Upgrade?","url":"https://ruckauthority.com/gear/goruck-rucker-5-0-vs-4-0-review","published_at":"2026-10-09T00:00:00Z","source_notes":{"perspective":"independent","notes":"Rucking gear reviews (packs, vests, plates, socks, base layers). Affiliate commissions; GORUCK supplied review units. Very high recent volume suggests bulk/SEO publishing; check enclosure MIME types (image enclosures)."},"selection_reason":"Under Relevance, Credibility, and Making the decision, the reported 25L pack trial with a 20 lb plate on hilly Wisconsin trails provides citable observations about stability and lumbar comfort relevant to Mammoth, supporting Pieces 01, 02, and 03. Commercial incentives and Uncertainty require qualifying that GORUCK supplied the pack, the publisher earns affiliate commissions and has unusually high review volume, and the reviewer never carried the 4.0; comparative comfort, heavier-load benefits, and long-term closure durability remain unverified. Freshness and novelty is met by newly reported field experience with the 5.0, while Suitability and official requirements leaves required status unspecified and multi-day event suitability unresolved.","excerpted":false,"passages":[{"text":"Free newsletter\n\nRucking notes by email\n\nNew gear reviews and practical training notes. Join now to get the newsletter from its first issue. Unsubscribe anytime.\n\nWhat changed from the 4.0 to the 5.0\n\nWhat the changes felt like on my back\n\nThe lumbar pad is the upgrade you'd feel\n\nThe magnetic pockets are a quality-of-life change\n\nWhat didn't change\n\nShould you upgrade from a Rucker 4.0?\n\nBuying your first Rucker\n\nFrequently asked questions\n\nThe Rucker 5.0's two documented changes are a larger, contoured lumbar pad of 30mm high-density foam and magnetic closures on the internal pockets, which replace the 4.0's hook-and-loop.\n\nThe shell, frame sheet, carry handles and SCARS lifetime guarantee carry over; both come in 20L and 25L.\n\nCarrying the 25L Rucker 5.0 with a 20 lb plate, the lumbar pad felt supportive and the pack stayed stable on rough, hilly ground.\n\nKeep a 4.0 that fits you well. Upgrade if its lumbar pad bothers you or its hook-and-loop pockets have worn out. First-time buyers should get the 5.0, or a discounted 4.0 if saving money matters more.\n\nThe Rucker 5.0 refines the 4.0 where you feel it most. GORUCK kept the dedicated plate pockets, the 1000D CORDURA shell, the fr"},{"text":"ame sheet and the lifetime guarantee, and changed the two things you touch most: the lumbar pad against your lower back and the closures on the internal pockets.\n\nI've carried the 25L Rucker 5.0 with a 20 lb plate on hilly Wisconsin trails, and my full Rucker 5.0 review covers that in detail. I haven't carried a 4.0, so the older bag's side of this comparison comes from GORUCK's published information. GORUCK supplied my 5.0 for review.\n\nWhat changed from the 4.0 to the 5.0\n\nGORUCK released the 5.0 on May 8, 2026. For the full launch breakdown, including why the magnetic closures matter at pre-dawn training and events, see our Rucker 5.0 launch analysis.\n\nWhat the changes felt like on my back\n\nThe lumbar pad is the upgrade you'd feel\n\nOn the table, the 5.0's lumbar pad looks enormous. On my back, it felt supportive and soon faded into the background, and I had no lower-back discomfort on the filmed hike.\n\nThe reason a bigger, contoured pad matters is the curve of your lower back. A pad that fills more of that hollow gives the pack a firmer second point of contact below your shoulder blades, so the bottom of the pack has less room to rock with each step. That second contact probably "},{"text":"did as much for the 5.0's stability on rough ground as the plate pocket did.\n\nSo the upgrade question mostly comes down to your 4.0's lumbar pad. If you finish a ruck with a sore or rubbed lower back, or you feel the bottom of the pack moving, that's the problem the 5.0 was built to fix. If you never think about your lower back in a 4.0, you already have most of what the 5.0 offers.\n\nThe magnetic pockets are a quality-of-life change\n\nI kept my keys in a magnetic internal pocket, and it was simply easy: one hand, no rip of hook-and-loop. Magnets are quiet and don't clog with lint, sand or dog hair the way hook-and-loop does over years of use. They're a genuine quality-of-life upgrade, and if your 4.0's pockets have worn out and stopped holding, they count for even more.\n\nWhat didn't change\n\nThe plate still rides high and tight against your back, which is why both generations carry better than an everyday bag with a plate sleeve. With a 20 lb plate, the 5.0 stayed stable where I had to work to keep my own balance. Strap setup, the sternum strap and the optional hip belt work the same way in both generations, and my full review goes into what I'd change for heavier loads.\n\nShould you "},{"text":"upgrade from a Rucker 4.0?\n\nUpgrade to the 5.0 if:\n\nYour lower back gets sore, rubbed or hot in the 4.0, or you feel the bottom of the pack rocking.\n\nThe hook-and-loop on your 4.0's internal pockets has worn out and no longer holds.\n\nYou're moving up to heavier plates, where lumbar contact and stability matter more.\n\nYou've been meaning to change sizes anyway.\n\nKeep your 4.0 if:\n\nIt fits well and you finish rucks without lower-back complaints.\n\nMost of your frustration is shoulder soreness. The lumbar pad won't fix that; a snug sternum strap, a hip belt or building up the weight more gradually will do more. Our rucking form guide covers strap setup.\n\nYou'd rather put the money toward a heavier plate or better shoes.\n\nGORUCK hasn't announced a way to retrofit the new lumbar pad or magnetic closures to a 4.0, so upgrading means replacing the bag.\n\nBuying your first Rucker\n\nIf you don't own either, I'd buy the 5.0. It's the version I've carried, and the lumbar pad is the change you'd notice on every ruck. A discounted 4.0 is still a proper dedicated ruck with the same plate-first design, so if you find one on sale and you'd rather keep the difference, it's a sensible buy. If you're on"},{"text":"ly starting out with light loads, GORUCK's cheaper Basic Rucker is worth a look first, and our best rucking backpacks guide compares the field beyond GORUCK.\n\nDeciding between a Rucker and GORUCK's everyday bags instead? I've carried all three with the same 20 lb plate, and my GR1 review and GR2 review compare them directly.\n\nFrequently asked questions\n\nThe Rucker 5.0 adds a larger, contoured lumbar pad of 30mm high-density foam in place of the 4.0's 20mm pad, and magnetic closures on the internal pockets in place of hook-and-loop. The shell, frame sheet, elevated plate storage, carry handles and lifetime guarantee carry over.\n\nYes, mainly because of the lumbar pad. In my own use of the 5.0, the pad felt supportive and the pack stayed stable on rough ground. If a 4.0 already fits you comfortably, the difference is smaller.\n\nYes, especially at a discount. It's still a dedicated rucking pack that holds the plate high, with the same construction and guarantee. You give up the newer lumbar pad and magnetic pockets.\n\nGORUCK released the Rucker 5.0 on May 8, 2026.\n\nGORUCK hasn't announced a retrofit. The lumbar pad and magnetic closures are built into the 5.0, so getting them means buyin"},{"text":"g the new bag.\n\nQuick Comparison\n\nRelated Articles\n\nGORUCK GR1 vs GR2: Which Ruck Should Ruckers Buy in 2026?\n\nGORUCK GR1 vs GR2 for ruckers. Which compartment design, capacity, and use case wins, plus a 5-question buyer's framework and FAQ.\n\nGORUCK Rucker 5.0 Review: 25L Tested With a 20 lb Plate\n\nHands-on GORUCK Rucker 5.0 review: the 25L with a 20 lb plate on hilly Wisconsin trails, the lumbar pad, plate pockets, and how it beats the GR1 and GR2.\n\nGORUCK Achilles 30 lb Weighted Vest: Hands-On Review\n\nMy hands-on review of the 30 lb GORUCK Achilles: quick dog walks, flexible sewn-in weight, fit adjustment, pellet noise and shoulder fatigue.\n\nGORUCK Spy Ruck 12 lb: Hands-On Review\n\nI like the black 12 lb Spy Ruck for comfortable weighted walks. Its sliding straps let me set the fit, but the load is permanent and essentials need a separate place."}]}
{"item_id":"feed:https://www.treelinereview.com/gearreviews/rei-co-op-magma-30-sleeping-bag-review","version_id":"4c742dc416136bc209dcfd4b570e69002f847ac2b7c81e9b48b874b0de19bfd8","kind":"feed","publisher_id":"treelinereview","title":"REI Co-op Magma 30 Sleeping Bag Review","url":"https://www.treelinereview.com/gearreviews/rei-co-op-magma-30-sleeping-bag-review","published_at":"2026-10-15T15:46:00Z","source_notes":{"perspective":"independent","notes":"Hands-on outdoor gear testing: boots, socks, gloves, layers, sleep systems, packs. Affiliate model."},"selection_reason":"Under Relevance, Freshness and novelty, and Credibility, this review adds two years of field experience, including over 24 backpacking nights, warmth at 33°F with specified layers, damp-weather use, and pack-volume tradeoffs relevant to Mammoth. Commercial incentives and Uncertainty require disclosing affiliate commissions, attributing findings to this reviewer, and qualifying temperature claims because testing stopped at 33°F; inconsistent size labels and comparison weights also need verification. It supports Pieces 01–03 under Suitability and official requirements, with required status unspecified because no official passage is supplied and further independent publishers needed for an article's central argument.","excerpted":true,"passages":[{"text":"REI Co-op Magma 30 Sleeping Bag Review\n\nA three-season backpacking and camping sleeping bag that comes in nine size options\n\nOctober 15th, 2026Home > Gear Reviews > Backpacking\n\nOur verdict\n\nREI Co-op’s Magma 30 is a great all-around bag for three-season backpackers and campers with nine size options.\n\nThe size range of nine options means there is something for backpackers of varying heights and widths and fills gaps in size inclusivity that have long existed for consumers who need bags outside of a “standard size.” The size inclusivity doesn’t compromise on performance, and the Magma 30 has a solid warmth-to-weight ratio and is surprisingly packable despite being slightly bulky. With high-quality 850-fill-power down and a silky exterior, and for just 1 lb 10 oz in the Regular Wide, the Magma 30 is both true to its temperature rating and durable despite how soft it is.\n\nI first tested the Magma 30 in 2024, and have used it as my primary backpacking sleeping bag since then on over 24 nights backpacking and over 25 nights car camping during weeklong road trips where we car camped and/or backpacked.\n\nHaving a bag as light and cozy as the Magma 30 that fit me comfortably as a size 20/2"},{"text":"es.\n\nBackpackers who prefer traditional mummy style bags but may want slightly more room or who have wider shoulders.\n\nWhat we liked\n\nThe warmth-to-weight is pretty great especially when considering the price. The 850-fill water resistant goose down is lofty and efficient at warmth retention, while the 1 lb 10 oz weight in the Regular Wide is fairly light—especially for a bag that comes in wider sizes.\n\nNine size options for a bag of this performance and quality is unheard of across any other sleeping bag offered on the market right now. The genderless sizing does not assume gendered fit and instead is made for a wide variety of body shapes and heights, as well as types of sleepers who benefit from extra space for movement.\n\nValue for the price: most higher-end backpacking sleeping bags with similar 850-fill power down cost hundreds of dollars more\n\nWhat could be better\n\nThe Magma 30, while light enough for many backpackers, is bulky, and can take up a lot of space in your pack when not in a compression sack.(For example, I fit it in the sleeping bag compartment of my Gregory Kalmia 60 L (now discontinued), but it took up a significant amount of space in my Gregory Amber 44 L pack."},{"text":")\n\nThe Magma 30 lacks some of the premium features found in bags like the Therm-a-Rest Boost 650 or the NEMO Disco Endless Promise, such as arm holes, chest vents to manage temperature control, and a draft collar–though these features do add weight so for some this could be a positive. It's more of a minimalist bag for gram-counting backpackers.\n\nHow we tested\n\nI first used the REI Co-op Magma 30 in 2024, and have car-camped and backpacked with it extensively over the past two years. I often take trips where I both car camp and then backpack, often car camping after the backpacking trip has ended, and I appreciate the versatility of the Magma 30 for both settings. I have slept in the REI Magma 30 in my REI Basecamp 4 tent, in my Big Agnes Blacktail 3 tent that I take backpacking along with my partner and dog, along with several nights sleeping under the stars in the redwoods in Northern California. I have tested the Magma 30 across three seasons in Southern Idaho, in early September and October in the Teton National Forest, in mid-June in Southern Montana, and late October in Humboldt County, CA. Low temperatures have ranged from 33°F to around 54°F and weather conditions I tested "},{"text":"the bag in damp and misty weather, warm summer and chilly fall rain, and dry nights in the high desert. On warm nights, I unzipped the Magma 30 and used it as a quilt.\n\nWe also tested the REI Co-op Magma 30 sleeping bag alongside similar models in our Best Backpacking Sleeping Bags and Best Camping Sleeping Bags guides.\n\nPerformance in the field\n\nWarmth and temperature accuracy\n\nI found the Magma to be accurate in its listed temperature, if not a little warm. The Magma 30 has a tested range of 34°F and a tested lower limit of 24°F. The coldest night I’ve tested the Magma 30 the temperature was down to 33°F in the early morning, and I did not feel cold in the bag while wearing wool baselayer leggings and a long sleeve top along with a lighter weight midlayer and wool socks on.\n\nI tend to find that I am a hot sleeper with the exception of my feet, which get cold and stay cold easily. I did not experience cold feet while sleeping in the Magma, and would unzip it on nights above 45°F when I felt too hot. If I needed to sleep in temperatures down to 30°F I would still use the Magma 30 but would not push it much further below freezing.\n\nComfort and sleep position\n\nThe Magma 30 is a bag m"},{"text":") which is 2 lbs 8 oz in the Regular Wide. (Read more about the Therm-a-Rest Boost 650 in our full review.)\n\nREI Magma’s bag weight is so light because it uses a high-end 850-fill-power, water-resistant goose down that is bluesign approved, making for a lofty and warm bag that remains light. Unlike the Therm-a-Rest or the NEMO Disco, the lack of features such as vents, extra zippers, and arm holes also contribute to a lighter bag weight. The 15-denier shell fabric is also lighter weight than the shell fabric found on the Disco and shell fabric on the Boost..\n\nPackability\n\nREI includes a stuff sack and a mesh sack for storage. The provided stuff sack is fine for car camping or trips where I want it packed down but don’t need it to be as small as possible. For that, I’d use a compression sack (not included) to help me get it down in size.\n\nOn backpacking trips with my 60L backpacking bag (which is admittedly bigger than I usually need, but I tend to pack additional items for a dog) the Magma 30’s packed size has met my needs and expectations. I tend to start my trip with my bag in a compression sack but will ultimately stuff it loosely to fill space as I hike, and I tested the Magma "},{"text":"less bulky than the colder temperature variant, with 4.7 L of compressed volume in the Medium Wide size.\n\nMagma 15°F\n\nThe Magma 15 is made to handle colder, shoulder season temperatures and even into winter months that don’t dip into the single digits or lower teens. It’s a heavier model at 2 lbs 8 oz in the Medium Wide and has a compressed volume of 8.6 L, extra bulk and weight that is necessary to handle frosty nights.\n\nMagma 30 Down Trail Quilt\n\nThe Magma 30 also comes in a Trail Quilt, REI’s offering for those who want to carry even less weight than the 30°F variant. It weighs 1 lb 6.3 oz in the Medium Wide, nearly 4 oz less than the mummy bag version. The quilt isn’t as size inclusive as the bags though, with only four size offerings: Medium, Medium Wide, Long, and Long Wide.\n\nREI Co-op Magma 30 FAQ\n\nIs the Magma 30 a men’s or women’s sleeping bag?\n\nREI Co-op designed the Magma line to be gender neutral. Instead of assuming a set design based on binary understandings of body size and gender, REI created a bag built on extensive testing that is ultimately made to fit a variety of bodies/widths, rather than a specific gender.\n\nIs the REI Co-op Magma 30 ideal for side sleepers?\n\n"},{"text":"h features, like arm holes on both sides for venting and for easily using your hands while still warm and enclosed in the bag. The Therm-a-Rest Boost 650 lacks the same size range as the Magma, but the bag itself is still fairly inclusive, with the hip girth of the Medium Wide size at 74 inches, much larger than the Magma 30’s 62.5 inches of hip girth in the Regular wide. The extra space in the Boost 650 lends itself to cold pockets in the bag, and overall we didn’t find the Boost 650 to be consistently true to its temperature comfort rating, with the actual rating hovering about 5°-10° above what’s listed.\n\nUltimately, the Therm-a-Rest Boost 650 is for those who seek camp comfort but also want a high-quality bag that can be taken backpacking. Those who want a lighter bag that’s warmer should choose the Magma line of bags, even though they lack some of the fun features of the Therm-a-Rest Boost 650. Learn more in our in-depth review of the Therm-a-Rest Boost 650 sleeping bag.\n\nNEMO Disco 30 Endless Promise\n\nPrice: $280-300Temperature rating: 30°FInsulation: 650 fill downShape: Semi-rectangularWeight: 1 lb 15 oz men’s, 2 lbs 4 oz women’s\n\nThe NEMO Disco 30 Endless Promise is another"},{"text":" in-depth review of the NEMO Disco Endless Promise Sleeping Bag.\n\nShould you buy the REI Co-op Magma 30 sleeping bag?\n\nBuy if:\n\nYou want a custom feeling fit without the custom price, with 9 different size and height offerings.\n\nYou’re an active or side sleeper and need a few extra inches at the chest/shoulder area and footbox.\n\nYou’re looking for a straightforward, backpacking-friendly bag with high performance features at a more approachable price\n\nSkip if:\n\nYou are looking for an abundance of built-in features and accessories.\n\nYou sleep cold, or just prefer to have a fuller coverage draft collar instead of the insulated yoke.\n\nYou prioritize absolutely minimal packability and compression. The Magma is pretty minimal but you're likely best served by a backpacking quilt.\n\nWhere to buy the REI Co-op Magma 30 sleeping bag\n\nThe REI Co-op Magma 30 sleeping bag is available at REI stores and online.\n\nREI\n\nPros: 100% satisfaction policy for 1 year; 10% dividend for members.\n\nWhy you should trust us / About the author\n\nI am a former outdoor educator and camp professional who knows that choosing the right gear for your outdoor adventures can make or break your experience. For many years "},{"text":"I did not have access to a lighter bag for backpacking that was also size inclusive, and I spent many miserable hikes and trips carrying a heavy and bulky bag.\n\nI know the value of finding gear that is made to truly fit a wider variety of bodies, and have been writing and testing gear and outdoor clothing in plus and extended sizes for 5 years with Treeline Review. I have tested bags and gear in rain, snow, and humid and dry conditions, carried them ten steps from car to campsite and over miles in the Sawtooth National Forest or Caribou-Targhee National Forest, where I spend most of my time camping, hiking, backpacking, and paddling. Finding gear that fits and keeps you going is essential, and I am lucky to share my learnings and experiences through guides with Treeline Review.\n\nRead more of Gabi Reyes-Acosta’s gear reviews at her author bio at Treeline Review.\n\nWe tested the REI Co-op Magma 30 sleeping bag while camping and backpacking across three seasons to test its warmth, comfort, durability, packability, and more.\n\nFind the best running pants for every season and terrain. Tested for comfort, breathability, and performance to keep you moving on road and trail runs in all kinds"},{"text":" of weather.\n\nFind the best women's down vests for hiking, camping, and everyday wear. Compare six tested picks for warmth, fit, weather resistance, and packability."}]}
{"item_id":"feed:https://www.treelinereview.com/gearreviews/best-running-pants","version_id":"28be243117027254a6e4779fa7e5a25dbdf399fec0a6fbae1b105b46113f8b20","kind":"feed","publisher_id":"treelinereview","title":"Best Running Pants of 2026","url":"https://www.treelinereview.com/gearreviews/best-running-pants","published_at":"2026-10-15T14:35:00Z","source_notes":{"perspective":"independent","notes":"Hands-on outdoor gear testing: boots, socks, gloves, layers, sleep systems, packs. Affiliate model."},"selection_reason":"Under Relevance, Credibility, and Freshness and novelty, this newly reported comparison supplies over a year of cold and wet trail testing plus a described water-spray protocol, with specific observations about moisture retention, abrasion, restrictive waistbands, and chafing that inform Mammoth clothing choices. Commercial incentives requires disclosing Treeline’s affiliate commissions and giving its praise less independent weight; under Suitability and official requirements and Uncertainty, these are attributed running-test findings rather than demonstrated loaded-ruck performance, required status remains unspecified, and this single publisher cannot alone support an article’s central argument.","excerpted":true,"passages":[{"text":"Best Running Pants of 2026\n\nBest joggers and wind pants for runners\n\nOctober 15th, 2026Home → Gear Reviews → Apparel\n\nRunning pants are useful when shorts aren’t enough but tights aren’t your first choice. They add protection from wind, rain, cold, and trailside brush while still giving you room to move, layer, and adjust as conditions change.\n\nOur main tester is an FKT-setting runner and ultramarathoner, and he tested running pants for more than a year across the Northeast on roads, rail trails, tracks, and technical mountains. He wore them in pouring rain, snow, slush, cold wind, and warm, humid weather.\n\nWe tested both single- and double-layer running pants and evaluated each pair for comfort, breathability, durability, weather resistance, fit, and how well it performed across different kinds of runs and conditions. We also paid attention to practical details like pockets, zippers, elastic, fabric feel, mobility, and whether the pants stayed comfortable as my effort and the temperature changed.\n\nTop Pick: The men's and women's REI Swiftland Hybrid Running Pants are our Best Overall running pants. Their wind- and water-resistant front panels, softer stretch fabric, useful storage"},{"text":"ey are large enough that most phones will bounce in the pockets. There is also one back zippered pocket; all pockets are nylon and inside the pants. The Mier joggers are made using 95% recycled and 5% new fabric. They’re offered up to a 3XL, with 46” waist and 51.5” hips.\n\nBreathability\n\nThe Mier joggers have some breathability, though they breathe less than most other pants we tested. They showed light sweat staining oat the extremes of heat and running intensity, but for shorter runs, these pants performed well. They do absorb rain and sweat, and would do best with fair weather conditions.\n\nOur Verdict\n\nWhile they lack some features of more expensive pants, they’re a great option for runners seeking utilitarian running pants for road running around town or trail running in a nearby park, and runners can feel good knowing the pants are made almost exclusively from recycled fabric.\n\nBest lightweight running pants: Zpacks Trail Cool Joggers\n\nPrice: $90Comfort subjective rating: 4/5Breathability subjective rating: 4/5Weather resistance subjective rating: 3/5Available sizes: S-XLMaterials: Toray PrimeFlexWhat we liked: Smooth and flexible fabric, quick-drying, durable What we didn’t l"},{"text":"their design with a midweight fabric, these were the warmest pants we tested. When tested in heat and high humidity, they did show moderate sweat staining, but for shorter and cooler runs, these pants performed well, with good breathability.\n\nSizing\n\nThe Active Pursuits Jogger offers the best range of dimensions of any of the running pants we tested, available in sizes up to a 3XL, with 50” waist and 55” hips. They matched the sizing for the REI Swiftlands, which won our award for Best Overall running pants, but are a more elastic and less-fitted style. These pants are welcoming of all body shapes, including runners with longer legs, larger thighs, or bigger butts.\n\nThe biggest issue our tester found with these pants was the baggy fit of the lower leg. The extra fabric on the lower legs made it possible to catch one leg on the other while walking or running. Additionally, these pants tend to run large, so if you are in between sizes, consider sizing down.\n\nIf you’re looking for more plus-size outdoor apparel options, check out our Best Plus-Size Outdoor Clothing and Best Outdoor Clothing for Big and Tall Adventurers guides.\n\nBest breathable running pants: Arc’teryx Incendo Pants\n\nP"},{"text":"rice: $130Comfort subjective rating: 4/5Breathability subjective rating: 5/5Weather resistance subjective rating: 3/5Available sizes: XS - XXLMaterials: Fortius Air 50, 84% nylon / 16% elastane, with FC0 DWRWhat we liked: Adjustable ankle closures, wide size range, quick-drying, non-PFC DWR finish, bluesign-approved fabrics What we didn’t like: Cut is tighter on the thighs, expensive\n\nOur Best Breathable running pants winner is the Arc’teryx Incendo Pants. They’re a lightweight running pant that has a soft brushed nylon texture. There are two open front pockets that very loosely fit a phone, and one smaller fold-over mesh outer pocket on the side that can fit keys, snacks, or trash. These pants are sustainably made, with a non-PFC DWR finish and using only bluesign-approved fabrics.\n\nThe Arc’teryx Incendo pants are ready for any kind of warm weather adventure you might want–be it running up a mountain or jogging on a rail trail. Photo by Justin Martino-Harms.\n\nFabric and Fit\n\nThe Incendos are tapered but flexible, and offer up to a XXL size (44.5” waist, 50” hips). They have good wind resistance, though their lightweight nature means they get wet from rain fairly quickly. However, "},{"text":" we liked: Soft and flexible nylon fabric, quick-drying, uses some recycled fabric What we didn’t like: Limited sizing that favors long and lean bodies, only pants we tested that required air drying (no clothes dryer)\n\nThe Craft ADV Essence Training Pants 2.0 are made of a soft, flexible nylon fabric. There are two front zippered pockets that can hold a phone, though the pockets are large enough that most phones will have some bounce (nylon, inside the pants).\n\nThe Craft ADV Essence pants are fitted, and the brand notes their products fit “long and lean,” which reflected our tester’s experience. Their XXL (39” waist, 48” hips) is second only to Janji for the smallest large size offered of all 10 tested running pants. While the fabric dimensions tended smaller, the fabric was very elastic and our tester found the fit to be comfortable, though he did size up as recommended by the Craft website.\n\nThe Craft website does not mention any DWR finish, but we noticed water beading during our water-resistance testing. These performed similarly to other lightweight, single-layer pants with DWR finishes, like the Zpacks Ultralight Trail Cool, Arc’teryx Incendo, Janji TTech, and Patagonia Terre"},{"text":"pants tend to be more durable than tights, and more resistant to rips and tears. This means they also generally offer more skin protection from trailside brush, plants, and trees.\n\nAdditionally, some people don’t like the look or feel of running tights. This can be for many reasons, including sensory or modesty concerns, or lack of pockets/storage. Running pants offer protection and warmth without constriction or exposure.\n\nRunning pants vs. tights\n\nChoosing between running pants and tights comes down to factors like weather conditions, personal comfort, and the type of running you do. Here’s how they compare:\n\nWhen to choose running pants\n\nBest layer for protection from precipitation and wind\n\nGreat for variable conditions and temperatures during your activity\n\nCustom level of warmth through adding base layers or rolling up pants\n\nOffers a less restrictive but tapered fit\n\nIf modesty is an important factor for you\n\nOffers durability and abrasion resistance, key for trail running\n\nWhen to choose running tights\n\nGood for mild to cool weather when you need lightweight warmth\n\nSimilar conditions are expected for the entire activity\n\nOffers a snug fit, with no fabric that can trip you "},{"text":"est Reflective Gear for Running in the Dark guide.\n\nDurability and abrasion resistance\n\nRunning pants are designed to prioritize breathability, flexibility, and moisture wicking. Due to all the pants’ polyester or nylon construction, they’re all quite durable within standard running conditions. The only product damage sustained during testing was due to a hard fall on pavement. Overall, lightweight pants are more likely to struggle with durability issues than midweight or heavyweight pants.\n\nRunning pants offer durability and abrasion resistance, all key for trail running. Photo by Aubri Drake.\n\nCare and maintenance of running pants\n\nMost running pants are advertised as safe to be machine washed and dried. One model we tested allowed machine washing but required air drying. Since the pants are all synthetic materials, they’ll maintain elasticity longer if air-dried or dried with lower heat. We air-dry our running pants when convenient and utilize the dryer on low to medium heat when clean clothes are needed more quickly.\n\nLike all synthetic fabrics, running pants can get a little funky, sometimes holding onto odors even after washing. We recommend using a sports fabric-specific det"},{"text":"d interiors or fleece-lined fabrics that trap warmth while staying breathable. Pairing them with a moisture-wicking base layer and weather-resistant shell helps keep you warm and dry during cold-weather runs.\n\nWhat’s the best material for running pants?\n\nSynthetic fabrics like polyester and nylon blends dominate because they dry quickly, wick sweat, and resist abrasion. Elastane (spandex) adds stretch and mobility. Some brands now use recycled polyester or sustainable performance fabrics that reduce environmental impact while maintaining durability.\n\nHow do I choose the right running pants for trail running?\n\nFor trail running, prioritize durability, freedom of movement, and protection. Thicker fabrics with some water or wind resistance are ideal, and ankle cuffs or tapered fits help prevent debris from getting inside. Reflective details and a secure waistband with zippered pockets are also helpful when running uneven terrain or remote trails.\n\nShould I wear running pants or shorts in the rain?\n\nIt depends on temperature and personal preference. Running pants made from water-resistant or quick-dry fabric can keep you warmer in cold, wet weather. In milder rain, some runners prefer "},{"text":"ay. We then evaluated how much water the paper towel strip had absorbed.\n\nAbout the author / Why you should trust us\n\nAubri ('Data') Drake is a white queer and transgender hiker, backpacker, and ultrarunner located in the northeast. They first started running in 2012, in their mid-20s, and after a few years of local road races (5K -> half marathon), they decided to mesh their love of hiking with running and started trail running.\n\nAubri’s always looking forward to his next big outdoor adventure, in between working full-time in research. Sometimes those adventures are backpacking on a long trail but more often than not, the adventure is a long unsupported trail run through the mountains and tough rocky terrain of the Northeast.\n\nAubri has set more than FKTs, and is currently training for their fourth 100-mile race, with lots of opportunities to test running pants in disparate conditions.\n\nYou can learn more about Aubri on their Treeline Review author page, website, or Instagram.\n\nFind the best running pants for every season and terrain. Tested for comfort, breathability, and performance to keep you moving on road and trail runs in all kinds of weather.\n\nFind the best women's down ve"},{"text":"sts for hiking, camping, and everyday wear. Compare six tested picks for warmth, fit, weather resistance, and packability.\n\nWe tested men’s down vests on hikes, backpacking trips, alpine runs, bike rides, and cold camps for warmth, comfort, fit, packability, and durability."}]}
{"item_id":"feed:https://www.treelinereview.com/gearreviews/best-down-vests","version_id":"22e47cddced54dfcbfaf1a331606c33343356dcc4b8c256dce2a2ea8ed1ab743","kind":"feed","publisher_id":"treelinereview","title":"Best Down Vests for Women of 2026","url":"https://www.treelinereview.com/gearreviews/best-down-vests","published_at":"2026-10-13T16:20:00Z","source_notes":{"perspective":"independent","notes":"Hands-on outdoor gear testing: boots, socks, gloves, layers, sleep systems, packs. Affiliate model."},"selection_reason":"Under Relevance, Credibility, and Making the decision, months of backpacking and layering tests provide citable observations relevant to Mammoth: restricted Patagonia pocket access under a hip belt, Rab performance in light rain, and Stretchdown abrasion resistance and faster wetting support Pieces 02 and 03. Under Commercial incentives and Uncertainty, Treeline earns affiliate commissions, so attribute its observations, distinguish listed specifications from measured results, and exclude untested models from performance conclusions; this single publisher cannot alone establish an article's central argument. Under Suitability and official requirements, evaluate warmth, mobility, and carried-weight tradeoffs while leaving required status unspecified because no official passage is supplied.","excerpted":true,"passages":[{"text":"Best Down Vests for Women of 2026\n\nThe top Down and Synthetic Insulated Vests for warmth, weather resistance, comfort, fit, and style\n\nOctober 13th, 2026, updated with a new vestHome > Gear Reviews > Apparel\n\nA good insulated vest adds warmth around your core without the bulk of a full puffy jacket. It’s an easy layer for cool hikes, chilly mornings at camp, travel, and everyday wear, and it can fit over a base layer or under a shell when temperatures drop.\n\nWe tested women’s down and synthetic vests over several months of hiking, camping, backpacking, travel, and everyday use. Testing included time in Colorado’s high country, along with trips in Idaho and Wyoming, in conditions ranging from cool, dry weather to drizzly evenings and colder nights at camp.\n\nWe evaluated warmth, fit, comfort, durability, weather resistance, packability, pockets, and how well each vest worked within different layering systems. We also compared the vests side by side for warmth and water resistance.\n\nTop Pick: The Patagonia Down Sweater Vest is our Best Overall women’s down vest. It stood out for its warmth, comfort, packability, and versatility across both outdoor and everyday use. It was also one of "},{"text":"others. The outer shell is constructed with a bluesign-approved recycled nylon ripstop that’s treated with a DWR coating. With dual zippered hand pockets and two spacious internal drop-in pockets, there’s ample space to carry essentials.\n\nWarmth\n\nWhile this vest is not the warmest in our test, it’s still quite cozy and is on the lighter side and small enough to pack into the backcountry as long as going ultralight is not a concern.\n\nFlaws but not dealbreakers\n\nWhile the REI Co-op 650 vest offers excellent value, there are a few things it lacks compared to others in our test. The fill power is lower than all other down vests in our test, and notably lower than the 800-fill of the Patagonia Down Sweater. It’s also not quite as warm or weather resistant as others, but overall the performance for the price is very good.\n\nOur verdict\n\nWith any budget item there are features you’ll have to sacrifice and we still think this is a great piece of gear. However, if you’re looking for a warmer vest with a higher loft and better down insulation, consider others in our review.\n\nWarmest Down Vest: Rab Microlight Vest\n\nWeight: 8 oz.Available sizes: XXS-XXLFit: standardShell fabric: 30D Pertex Quan"},{"text":"side. It’s still light enough for most outdoor adventures, but if weight savings is a priority you’ll want to consider other options, though you will sacrifice some of the weather-resistant qualities that this vest offers.\n\nThe Rab Microlight Vest is the overall winner in our Best Men's Down Vests guide where we especially liked the balance of function and sustainability.\n\nBest Packable Vest: Mountain Hardwear Stretchdown Light Vest\n\nWeight: 8.3 oz.Available sizes: XS-XLFit: standardShell fabric: 20D Durable Stretch Doubleweave, 86% Nylon, 14% ElastaneInsulation type: 700-fill RDS-certified downPockets: What we liked: Stretchy, comfortable, great for freedom of movementWhat could be better: price, shell fabric could be more water-resistant and breathable\n\nEven with the lack of sleeves, not all vests offer a wide range of motion due to the stiff shell materials used for down insulation. Mountain Hardwear changes that with their Stretchdown shell material. For active pursuits, whether hiking, backpacking, or rock climbing, the Stretchdown vest offers the most comfort and range of motion of any vest in our test. And it packs into its right-hand pocket, making it easy to stash in a bac"},{"text":"kpack. We used this on long and tough late summer backpacking trips in the high alpine of northern Colorado and only took it off in the heat of the day. If you want a vest that can stand up and stay comfortable for all sorts of outdoor adventures, this is it.\n\nMaterials\n\nThe Stretchdown Light vest is made with a stretchy, 20-denier shell material that’s both durable and comfortable. On boulder field scrambles, we had no issues with abrasion when wearing this vest. The Stretchdown has 700-fill RDS-certified down in the staggered baffles.\n\nIt’s not as lofty as other vests in our test, but that sleeker design is part of what makes it great for aerobic activities like hiking and climbing. We were less likely to overheat in this vest than any others in our test.\n\nWeatherproofness\n\nOn our testing hikes and backpacking trips, we found that the vest blocks wind well in 25-mph gusts above treeline, but the water resistance was not as good as others in our test; it wetted out faster than the others.\n\nPockets\n\nFor pockets, it’s got two zippered hand pockets.\n\nFunctional and stretchy\n\nIf you want the functionality of the stretchy shell material you’ll have to fork over the extra cash, but that"},{"text":"’s worth it if you plan to use this vest primarily for active outdoor pursuits.\n\nFlaws but not dealbreakers\n\nWhile this vest is stretchy and comfortable for moving in, the fabric could be more water-resistant and breathable. But only a certain level of breathability is to be expected with an insulated garment like this in the first place, so this is a minor complaint.\n\nThe only real downside to this vest is that it’s on the pricey side.\n\nOur verdict\n\nIf you want a vest that’s ideal for a lot of movement and big outdoor adventures, we think this should be your top choice. We also had the most confidence in the durability of this vest; it didn’t snag or tear when bushwhacking and scrambling for an entire day off-trail. No other vest in our test was as comfortable on the trail (or scrambling and bushwhacking off trail), and the freedom of movement that comes with the stretch shell is tough to beat.\n\nBest Plus-Size Vest: Cotopaxi Fuego Down Vest\n\nWeight: 14 oz.Available sizes: XXS-4XShell fabric: 20D nylon ripstop with DWR Insulation type: 800-fill RDS-certified downPockets: 2 hand, 2 internalWhat we liked: great size range, fun colors and style, warm, made with sustainable and ethical"},{"text":"pe that in future seasons Mountain Hardwear will bring the women’s model back. The last thing we need is fewer technical options for women’s-specific gear!\n\nWho needs an insulated vest?\n\nPuffy vests are useful pieces of gear for taking the chill off without needing a full jacket. They can also be layered over other insulating pieces, like fleece jackets, and under more protective pieces like rain jackets. A puffer vest is more compressible and packable than a fleece vest, making it better suited for travel and outdoor adventure.\n\nIf you’re heading out into bitterly cold conditions, a vest probably isn’t the best option, and you’ll want to reach for your parka. But for times when it’s chilly or cold, but not quite frigid, a vest can be the perfect companion to keep your core warm. Ultimately, whether a vest is right for you will come down to personal preference.\n\nWhen to wear an insulated vest\n\nInsulated vests shine in that tricky in-between weather when it’s too cold for just a shirt or fleece but too warm for a full jacket. They’re perfect for keeping your core warm while allowing your arms to move freely—ideal for hiking, layering, or just getting things done around town on cool "},{"text":"AQ\n\nWhat do you wear under a down vest?\n\nIt depends on the weather and activity. For hiking or other high-output movement, wear a lightweight base layer underneath to avoid overheating. Around camp or in cold weather, add a fleece or light midlayer for extra warmth. Because down vests aren’t waterproof, pair them with a shell or rain jacket in wet conditions—or wear them under a ski jacket for added insulation on cold winter days.\n\nShould a down vest fit tight or loose?\n\nA down vest should fit snug but not tight. It needs enough room for the down to fully loft and trap warm air, but not so much space that cold air seeps in. If you plan to layer underneath, choose a slightly roomier fit; for wearing over a base layer, a trimmer cut keeps warmth close to your core.\n\nWhat's the difference between down and synthetic insulation?\n\nDown insulation (made from goose or duck feathers) is lighter, warmer for its weight, and more compressible—perfect for packability and dry, cold conditions. Its main drawbacks are cost, delicacy, and loss of warmth when wet.\n\nSynthetic insulation is typically heavier and bulkier but stays warm when damp, dries faster, and is easier to care for. It’s also more "},{"text":"affordable and vegan-friendly, making it a great choice for wet or unpredictable weather.\n\nYou can read more in Which is better: a down or synthetic jacket?\n\nAre insulated vests warm enough for extreme cold?\n\nNot on their own. Insulated vests keep your core warm but leave your arms exposed, so they work best as a layering piece in very cold weather. For extreme cold, pair a vest under a parka or insulated jacket for maximum warmth.\n\nAre insulated vests easy to pack?\n\nYes—especially down vests. Most compress into a small stuff sack or their own pocket, making them easy to stash in a backpack or travel bag. Synthetic vests pack slightly larger but still offer excellent warmth for their weight.\n\nWhat temperatures are best for wearing a puffer vest?\n\nPuffer vests are versatile across seasons. They’re ideal for cool to cold conditions, such as early fall hikes, chilly camp mornings, or layered under a shell on subfreezing days. They work well anywhere you need warmth without the bulk of full sleeves.\n\nWhy do bankers wear vests?\n\nThe primary reason why bankers wear puffer vests, according to Marketplace, is because they offer a more casual and comfortable look that still maintains a cert"},{"text":"e. Along with her editorial expertise, she spends almost every day getting outside, whether hiking, backpacking, mountain biking, or backcountry skiing. When working as a backcountry guide in Alaska, she was particularly hard on gear and is now always highly critical of outdoor gear and apparel, including what makes a good vest. Both because of her outdoor experience and outdoor industry journalism experience, she’s able to dive deep into materials, quality, and performance when testing.\n\nStasia is a lifelong backpacker, skier, hiker, mountain biker, and trail runner passionate about lowering barriers to entry for outdoor sports. When she’s not writing or cooking, you’ll find her in the mountains, probably above treeline, and probably testing gear.\n\nMore of her work and adventures can be found on her website.\n\nTo see all Stasia’s Treeline articles, see her author profile.\n\nWe tested the REI Co-op Magma 30 sleeping bag while camping and backpacking across three seasons to test its warmth, comfort, durability, packability, and more.\n\nFind the best running pants for every season and terrain. Tested for comfort, breathability, and performance to keep you moving on road and trail runs i"},{"text":"n all kinds of weather.\n\nFind the best women's down vests for hiking, camping, and everyday wear. Compare six tested picks for warmth, fit, weather resistance, and packability."}]}
{"item_id":"feed:https://www.treelinereview.com/gearreviews/best-womens-rain-boots","version_id":"8f101ae52afcbdf84cb5b62b856bf5eb774f8104d77dc3e4c7d87fb8f059ff72","kind":"feed","publisher_id":"treelinereview","title":"Best Rain Boots for Women of 2026","url":"https://www.treelinereview.com/gearreviews/best-womens-rain-boots","published_at":"2026-10-13T14:55:00Z","source_notes":{"perspective":"independent","notes":"Hands-on outdoor gear testing: boots, socks, gloves, layers, sleep systems, packs. Affiliate model."},"selection_reason":"Under Relevance, Credibility, and Freshness and novelty, the newly added Ultra Raptor 3 Mid GTX assessment provides citable wet-weather evidence: four months of testing, about 35 miles, a week in Iceland, and 15-minute creek submersion comparisons, with narrow fit and break-in drawbacks relevant to Mammoth. Commercial incentives requires flagging Treeline’s affiliate commissions despite its stated absence of brand sponsorship; under Uncertainty and Suitability and official requirements, attribute these single-publisher findings, acknowledge missing loaded-ruck and Mammoth experience, and leave required status unspecified.","excerpted":true,"passages":[{"text":"Best Rain Boots for Women of 2026\n\nWaterproof boots and rubber rain boots for mud, puddles, warmth, and staying dry\n\nOctober 13th, 2026, updated with a new winner for best waterproof hiking bootHome → Gear Reviews → Footwear\n\nRain boots need to do more than keep your feet dry. Depending on how you use them, they also need enough traction for slick surfaces, a comfortable fit for long days on your feet, and the durability to handle mud, rain, and repeated wear.\n\nWe tested women’s rain boots in Alaska, New England, Iowa, the Pacific Northwest, New York City, and California. Testing included rainy commutes, farm work, long walks, rafting trips, muddy trails, and flood cleanup, giving us a chance to use these boots in everything from everyday rain to demanding wet-weather conditions.\n\nWe evaluated waterproofness, traction, fit and comfort, durability, and how easy each boot was to get on and off. We also considered how well different styles worked for specific uses, from ankle boots for everyday wear to taller boots for deeper water and muddy conditions.\n\nTop Pick: The XTRATUF Legacy Salmon Sisters Boot is our Best Overall women’s rain boot. Our testers have worn XTRATUF Legacy boots f"},{"text":"or long days on Alaska’s rivers and in wet, cold conditions, and they continue to stand out for their waterproofing, traction, comfort, and durability.\n\nLooking for men’s rain boots? See our Best Men’s Rain Boots guide.\n\nPairing them with a shell? See our Best Lightweight Rain Jackets guide.\n\nWe create reader-supported, objective gear reviews independently selected by our editors. This story may contain affiliate links, which help fund our website. When you click on the links to purchase gear, we may get a commission — without costing you an extra cent. Thank you for supporting our work and mission of outdoor coverage for every body! Learn more.\n\nComparison table\n\nThe winners\n\nBest overall women’s rain boots: XTRATUF Legacy\n\nPrice: $175Sizes available: 6 - 11Shaft height: 15”Waterproof: YesMaterial: Rubber/neopreneFootwear closure: Pull onBest for: Wading in rivers or puddles, chores, spring hiking, running errands, working on boats, slogging through marshes What we liked: waterproofness, durability, ease of walking, comfort, versatility, temperature and height adjustment with roll-top, arch support, excellent traction on slippery surfacesWhat we didn’t like: not as warm as other b"},{"text":"functions for less than $40.\n\nThe Asgard ankle boots were waterproof when walking in puddles of water.\n\nThe Asgard ankle boots were waterproof when walking in puddles of water.\n\nFit and Comfort\n\nThe over-the-ankle design of these boots helps to keep muck and rain out of your footbed, but make sure you wear a mid-calf sock along with it! Our tester did have a bit of chafing with ankle socks and jeans. The openings are narrow but extremely elastic, which helps for ease of pulling on and taking off these boots. One tester with regular-to-wide feet did note that the narrow footbed did become uncomfortable on one afternoon of walking. Their toes had room to wiggle, but the middle of their feet started to rub and hurt after multiple hours out walking.\n\nDesign and Function\n\nAsgard boots utilize removable EVA foam insoles for comfortable strides, and anti-slip rubber soles to keep you stable and upright in slick weather. These boots are made from waterproof rubber that held up well in our testing — the rubber held up to bending, stretching, and scraping along sidewalks and short hikes without compromising waterproofing.\n\nElastic-goring by the ankles added ease of pulling on and taking off "},{"text":"t calf in true sizes\n\nWe knew these boots were popular, but were hesitant to believe they could live up to their fame. However, after 7+ years of testing, we can confirm that these boots fit the hype: they are comfortable, chic, and completely waterproof. From the riding arena to the barn to the streets of Boston, these boots tackle puddles and slick surfaces like champs.\n\nIn addition to being stylish, the Hunter Women’s Original Tall boots were waterproof and durable enough for muddy chores.\n\nThe Hunter Tall Boots were comfortable to take on 10-mile walks in New York City.\n\nThe tall length of the Hunter Women’s Original Tall boots protected our pants in wet conditions.\n\nFit and Comfort\n\nThese boots are crafted from natural rubber harvested from forests that are independently audited to Forest Stewardship Council standards to keep the forests and their inhabitants (both human and animal) protected. The footbeds fit snugly for stability and comfort without being too narrow, although the author’s leggings-clad calves did have a hard time fitting up top without a little extra effort on the most expanded belt option. (Larger calves should consider Hunter’s back-adjustable rain boot, wh"},{"text":"calf-deep water in driveways after heavy flooding in Maine this winter, and kept our testers warm and dry while walking in torrential downpours.\n\nWalking might take some getting used to if you aren’t used to the classic Wellington walk, but Hunter uses pliable rubber to make your jaunt around the garden easier. As your ankle moves, you will feel the rubber bend and roll over itself in the front –– this is normal! The elevated heel helps add a chic edge to the galoshes effect, and gives you a cushion as you walk on harder surfaces.\n\nOverall, Hunter’s Original Tall Rain Boots were our favorite tall boot for the sleek design, incredible waterproofness, and comfort while walking. They are a pricier option, but worth the money for longevity’s sake.\n\nMost comfortable women’s rain boots: Grundens Deck Boss\n\nPrice: $115Sizes available: 5 - 11Shaft height: 6”Waterproof: Yes (neoprene ankles)Material: RubberFootwear closure: Pull onBest for: Cleaning up the deck, grocery runs in sleet, trekking from one end of town to the other, and winter airport travelWhat we liked: comfort of wearing slippers with thick insole, easy on-and-off, anti-slip tread, waterproofness, anti-odor liningWhat we didn"},{"text":" anti-odor and HeiQ SmartTemp thermo-regulating lining that fights the overheating and stink of sweaty feet, which are all too common in rubber-encased rain boots. Even on hot days when you still need enclosed toes and slip-resistant boots, these comfortable ankle slip-ons will avoid the pungent odor of sweaty gym socks and keep you cooler. However, if the weather’s more brisk, this liner will also help to insulate you and keep your feet at an optimal temperature.\n\nA unique feature of the Deck Boss boots comes in the form of their razor-siped outsole: the treads are angled at just the right slant to push water out from under the shoe, just like providing more traction to tires in slick conditions. This means you get a better grip without the slickness of water obstructing your gum rubber outsole from the surface. What does all of that translate to? You stay upright better on rainy walks.\n\nBest women’s rain boots for walking: Blundstone Women’s Original Chelsea Boots\n\nPrice: $200Sizes available: 6 - 11Shaft height: 4.5”Waterproof: No, water-resistantMaterial: Water-resistant leatherFootwear closure: Pull onBest for: City walking, wearing to the office, short hikes, walking dogs What"},{"text":" fit to be uncomfortable and the shoes felt like, \"they never really broke in no matter how much I walked in them.\" So if you've got wide feet, go with the Classics!\n\nBest waterproof hiking boots: La Sportiva Ultra Raptor 3 Mid GTX\n\nPrice: $219Sizes available: 5.5 - 11Shaft height: 6.25\"Waterproof: YesMaterial: GORE-TEXFootwear closure: Lace-upBest for: Hiking muddy trails, backpacking, creek and river crossingsWhat we liked: Water resistance, grip, agilityWhat we didn’t like: Narrow fit, moderate ankle support, need a break-in period\n\nThe La Sportiva Ultra Raptor 3 Mid GTX Hiking Boots are the best waterproof women's hiking boots we tested and one of the most agile, lightweight, grippy boots, too.\n\nWhile we've tested them across Colorado and Washington in creek crossings and mud, what really convinced us these are the best waterproof hiking boots was wearing them for a week straight in Iceland.\n\nIn the notoriously wet and cold weather of Iceland, we realized that the Ultra Raptor 3 Mid GTX boots aptly handle mud, ice, snow, and river crossings for multiple days.\n\nAfter four months of testing, we realized that they not only support trekking in ideal conditions, but they’re also the"},{"text":" kind of boots you want to be wearing when hikes don’t go according to plan.\n\nWe named it Best lightweight waterproof hiking boots in our Best Waterproof Hiking Boots guide and it also won Best Waterproof Hiking Boot in our Best Women's Hiking Boots guide.\n\nRead more about them in our in-depth review of the La Sportiva Ultra Raptor 3 Mid GTX boots here.\n\nHow we tested\n\nWe wore these boots for about 35 miles while evaluating qualities such as grip, durability, breathability, and weather resistance, and we directly compared them to more than 10 other pairs of hiking boots.\n\nWaterproofness\n\nMany hiking boots are built with GORE-TEX, but that doesn't mean they are equal. We tested so-called waterproof hiking boots side by side in a local Colorado creek by fully submerging them for 15 minutes. Not only did they protect us from short-term submersion, they also kept water out and our feet remained dry and comfortable at the end of our trek.\n\nWhile some boots passed the first test, only a handful of them managed to pass the second. And the Ultra Raptor 3 Mid GTX boots were the most reliable boots among them when it came to weatherproofing.\n\nExcellent grip and traction\n\nA waterproof hiking "},{"text":" out thoroughly and keep a watch on your feet for too much dampness.\n\nHow we researched and tested\n\nWearing the XTRATUF Legacy while boating in Alaska.\n\nTo ensure that we reviewed the best boots for the rainy season, we researched the most popular rain boots and came up with over 30 different choices.\n\nWe tested rain boots on soggy Alaska trails, in rivers in Maine, on commutes in the Pacific Northwest, and during farmwork in Iowa. We tested on long walks in New York City, while digging out a flooded house in California, cleaning out the chicken coop in Portland, Oregon, and guiding clients on rafting trips. We also tested waterproof hiking boots in Iceland, Colorado, Washington, and Tennessee. In all, we’ve spent over four years testing rain boots and waterproof boots for women.\n\nTo be considered for each winning category, we measured each pair of boots against standardized criteria: good tread, waterproofness, great fit, and ease of pulling on and taking off. For our waterproof recommendations, the boots had to be considered waterproof (or at least water resistant) by our own testing and a majority of customer reviews. We also included a water-resistant option for everyday walkin"},{"text":"comfortable across conditions, and is repairable too."}]}
{"item_id":"feed:https://www.treelinereview.com/gearreviews/best-mens-down-vests","version_id":"0f98ede0d27c25831c3a57c3c861c041bfff80673996c8ed6462cb8fb092f848","kind":"feed","publisher_id":"treelinereview","title":"Best Down Vests for Men of 2026","url":"https://www.treelinereview.com/gearreviews/best-mens-down-vests","published_at":"2026-10-12T15:34:00Z","source_notes":{"perspective":"independent","notes":"Hands-on outdoor gear testing: boots, socks, gloves, layers, sleep systems, packs. Affiliate model."},"selection_reason":"Under Relevance, Credibility, and Suitability and official requirements, backpacking and cold-camp testing, home-scale weights, and specific layering and armhole observations provide citable evidence about carried insulation for Mammoth, supporting Pieces 02 and 03. Commercial incentives and Uncertainty require disclosing affiliate commissions, attributing observations to this single publisher, and qualifying untested wet-weather and longevity claims; no official requirement is supplied. Freshness and novelty is supported by reported multi-year Thermawrap experience, although the newly added vest is not identified, so the update alone does not establish a new-product story.","excerpted":true,"passages":[{"text":"Best Down Vests for Men of 2026\n\nThe best men's insulated vests for warmth, aerobic activities, and more\n\nOctober 12th, 2026, updated with a new vestHome → Gear Reviews → Apparel\n\nWhen shoulder season or cold mornings settle in, a down vest is one of the easiest layers to reach for. A good vest adds warmth around your core without the bulk of a full puffy jacket, and it can work just as well on the trail as it does around town.\n\nWe tested insulated vests in Colorado and Wyoming during late summer, fall and winter, wearing them on hikes, mountain runs, backpacking trips, bike rides, camping trips, and around town. We also carried them in packs and wore them with backpacks to see how they held up during regular outdoor use.\n\nWe evaluated warmth-to-weight, shell durability and weather resistance, insulation and baffle construction, fit, comfort, layering, and overall versatility. We paid particular attention to armholes and torso fit, how easily each vest layered over or under other clothing, and how well it held up over time.\n\nTop Pick: The Rab Microlight Down Vest earns our Best Overall award. It combines warm recycled down insulation with a durable Pertex Quantum shell, a comfortab"},{"text":"le fit, and the versatility to work for hiking, everyday wear, and layering in colder conditions. Of the vests we tested, it offered the best overall balance of warmth, comfort, durability, and fit.\n\nLooking for women's down vests? See our Best Women's Down Vests guide\n\nWe create reader-supported, objective gear reviews independently selected by our editors. This story may contain affiliate links, which help fund our website. When you click on the links to purchase gear, we may get a commission, without costing you an extra cent. Thank you for supporting our work and mission of outdoor coverage for every body! Learn more.\n\nComparison table\n\nThe winners\n\nBest overall men’s down vest: Rab Microlight Down Vest\n\nWeight: 10.5 ozAvailable sizes: S-XLFit: RegularShell fabric: 30D Pertex Quantum with NetPlus and fluorocarbon-free DWRInsulation type: Recycled 700FP Down with Grangers hydrophobic finishWhat we liked: Very comfortable, recycled down fill, recycled shell, warm, great fitWhat could be better: Heavier than some vests\n\nThe Rab Microlight Vest is the best down vest overall because it’s comfortable, warm, durable, and looks great. What truly makes this vest stand out is its sustain"},{"text":"verything. It’s fantastic overall, and we almost picked it as the best overall down vest, but the Rab Microlight fits better in the arms and has recycled down fill.\n\nThe Down Sweater vest is the vest version of our overall winner for Best Down Jacket in our Best Down Jackets guide. We also dedicate a single product in-depth review to the Patagonia Down Sweater Jacket.\n\nPatagonia Down Sweater Vest\n\nMaterials\n\nThe Down Sweater Vest is made with a 100% recycled 20-denier nylon ripstop outer shell fabric that’s treated with a PFC-free durable water-repellent (DWR) coating for water repellency.\n\nYou can read more about perfluorinated chemicals (PFCs/PFAS) in waterproofing and why the outdoor industry is moving away from them here.\n\nIncredible warmth\n\nThis is one of the warmest vests we’ve worn. On chilly mornings in the Wind River Range, it was warm enough under a windbreaker to keep your core temperature up. And it’s a great addition to a fleece jacket or midlayer when it gets even colder. The Down Sweater Vest is warm enough to wear often without being too hot, so you don’t often need to take it off.\n\nWeight and packability\n\nAt 8.9 ounces, it’s light and packable enough to throw in yo"},{"text":"one of the handwarmer pockets. It’s also one of the most affordable insulated vests that we tested.\n\nFlaws but not dealbreakers\n\nWhile we love almost everything about this vest, it isn’t the most stylish. There are better choices if you’re looking for a vest that you can wear around town or to the office. Also, this vest is still not made of any recycled materials, which seems out of step in a world increasingly full of recycled nylon outdoor gear.\n\nOur verdict\n\nThis is a great choice if you’re looking for a cold-weather active layer or breathable insulation for summer backpacking. The Thermawrap is downright legendary in the backpacking world, and we think the Thermawrap Vest is an excellent choice for anyone looking for an active layer or an ultralight summer backpacking outer layer to use in place of a puffy jacket.\n\nBest men's packable insulated vest: Patagonia Nano Puff Vest\n\nWeight: 8.3 ozAvailable sizes: XS-3XLFit: RegularShell fabric: Recycled polyester ripstopInsulation type: 60 g PrimaLoft Gold Insulation Eco postconsumer recycled polyesterWhat we liked: Lightweight, breathable, comfortable zipper garage, Recycled content inside and outWhat could be better: Not as warm as"},{"text":" some vests\n\nThe Patagonia Nano Puff Vest is our favorite vest for aerobic activities. This vest is insulated with the highly breathable PrimaLoft® Gold Insulation Eco, which insulates even when wet. This insulation vents heat well, but during high-output activities you’re going to sweat, even when wearing breathable clothing. The Nano Puff will continue to insulate even when it gets wet from your sweat, so you won’t get too cold when you stop moving.\n\nPatagonia Nano Puff Vest\n\nHow we tested\n\nWe tested the Nano Puff Vest in Colorado during the summer. We wore it while biking around Denver on bike paths and city streets, wore it while hiking the Colorado Front Range on chilly mornings, and carried it with us throughout the day as an extra layer. We also have a lot of experience wearing the Nano Puff jacket, which is the same as the vest with the addition of sleeves, so we’ve spent a lot of time wearing this synthetic insulation piece from Patagonia.\n\nExcellent for layering\n\nThe Nano Puff vest is thin and lightweight and great for layering under a jacket. Its face material is thin and soft. It has a slightly slippery feel but doesn’t feel sticky as you sweat. It has zippered hand poc"},{"text":"ome shortcomings, though. First, down does not insulate when wet. If you’re using a down-insulated vest in rainy conditions or while sweating a lot, you’ll notice the vest doesn’t feel as puffy when wet. When down loses its loft, as it does when wet, a down vest won’t keep you as warm.\n\nSecondly, down is not vegan. And though some down is ethically sourced, not all of it is. If you’re concerned with the ethical treatment of birds, you should consider buying down products certified by the Responsible Down Standard or Global Traceable Down Standard.\n\nThere is even debate on whether truly ethical down can even exist on a large scale. If you’re in this camp, you might want to simply consider synthetic insulation.\n\nComparison of our two top synthetic insulated vests.\n\nSynthetic insulation uses polyester or nylon spun into loosely-bunched fibers to create an insulation material that mimics the qualities of down. Synthetic insulation is often cheaper than down, and can be made with recycled plastic. Some synthetic insulation itself can even be recycled.\n\nSynthetic insulation doesn’t compress as much as down when it gets wet, so it retains a lot of its insulating properties. If you’re goin"},{"text":" ripstop weave. This thicker shell material will be heavier, though.\n\nComparison of some of the vests side-by-side. Note how some have a more boxy fit while others are more tapered.\n\nSize and fit\n\nIf you plan to layer an insulated vest on top of warm winter layers, then take that extra bulk into account when sizing an insulated vest. If you’re only planning on wearing your insulated vest over trim-fitting shirts or base layers, then you could probably get a smaller-size vest than if you wanted to wear it over a thick sweatshirt.\n\nWeight\n\nComparison of down vests side-by-side as stored in their stuff sacks. We weighed each one on our home scale.\n\nInsulated vests are often lighter than full-sleeved puffy jackets, but there are definitely lighter-weight and heavier vests you can choose from. If you plan on carrying your insulated vest backpacking in the summer, you should consider getting a lightweight vest that will be easier to carry on your back. If you’re going to be wearing your puffer vest during cold weather, weight is also important, but an ounce or two more might mean that the insulated vest you’re skiing or snowboarding in will breathe better.\n\nCompressibility\n\nComparison of"},{"text":"anger or in a breathable cotton bag, away from direct sunlight or heat. Proper storage keeps the down fluffy and ready for the next season.\n\nField repairs\n\nFor small rips or snags, use Tenacious Tape or NOSO patches to prevent feathers from escaping. For larger tears or broken zippers, many brands now offer repair services—an easy way to extend the life of your vest instead of replacing it.\n\nWhen cleaned and stored properly, a good down vest can last a decade or more. Treat it like any other piece of outdoor apparel—maintain it regularly, and it’ll keep you warm for many winters to come.\n\nThe Outdoor Research Cold Front is a great vest for winter and offers lots of warmth during warmer months, too.\n\nFAQ\n\nWhat do you wear under a down vest?\n\nWhat you wear underneath depends on the weather and your activity. For hiking or other active use, wear a light base layer or long-sleeve shirt to prevent overheating. In colder weather or around camp, layer your vest over a fleece or midweight jacket for added warmth. If rain or snow is in the forecast, wear your vest under a waterproof shell to keep it dry and performing well. When choosing a size, make sure the vest fits comfortably over your"},{"text":" typical layering system.\n\nShould a down vest fit tight or loose?\n\nA down vest should fit snug but not tight. It should trap warmth close to your core without compressing the down, which reduces loft and insulation. Leave enough room to fit a sweater or light jacket underneath, and make sure the armholes aren’t restrictive, so you can move freely and layer as needed.\n\nWhat's the difference between down and synthetic insulation?\n\nDown insulation comes from the fine plumage of ducks or geese. It’s extremely light, warm, and compressible, making it ideal for dry, cold conditions. However, it loses warmth when wet and requires more care.\n\nSynthetic insulation, made from polyester fibers, mimics down’s loft but retains heat even when damp and dries faster. It’s easier to wash and usually more affordable, but slightly heavier and less packable than down.\n\nAre insulated vests warm enough for extreme cold?\n\nNot by themselves. Insulated vests keep your core warm, but since they don’t cover your arms, they’re best used as a layering piece in extreme cold. Pair one under a down jacket or shell for added insulation during frigid conditions.\n\nAre insulated vests easy to pack?\n\nYes—one of their "},{"text":"ross three seasons to test its warmth, comfort, durability, packability, and more.\n\nFind the best running pants for every season and terrain. Tested for comfort, breathability, and performance to keep you moving on road and trail runs in all kinds of weather.\n\nFind the best women's down vests for hiking, camping, and everyday wear. Compare six tested picks for warmth, fit, weather resistance, and packability."}]}
{"item_id":"feed:https://www.treelinereview.com/gearreviews/best-waterproof-socks","version_id":"ecf119cf5f407aea8f56f0ce4bf2bd6105c24a28d2e2850c5b2f1de32339379d","kind":"feed","publisher_id":"treelinereview","title":"Best Waterproof Socks of 2026","url":"https://www.treelinereview.com/gearreviews/best-waterproof-socks","published_at":"2026-10-09T23:42:00Z","source_notes":{"perspective":"independent","notes":"Hands-on outdoor gear testing: boots, socks, gloves, layers, sleep systems, packs. Affiliate model."},"selection_reason":"Under Relevance, Credibility, and Freshness and novelty, this updated comparison provides citable wet-weather foot-care evidence for Mammoth, including bucket tests, hikes in 45–65°F conditions, and StormSock leakage after roughly three hours of a 70-mile rainy ride; it supports Pieces 01, 02, and 03. Commercial incentives requires discounting this affiliate-funded review's praise, while Conflicting evidence and Uncertainty require flagging inconsistent RANDY SUN material descriptions and limiting conclusions to attributed observations rather than unsupported extreme-cold or lifetime-durability claims. Under Diversity of perspectives and Suitability and official requirements, Mammoth practitioner evidence is missing and required status remains unspecified; this source can contribute to, but cannot alone establish, an article's three-independent-publisher central argument.","excerpted":true,"passages":[{"text":"Best Waterproof Socks of 2026\n\nVersatile, durable and waterproof socks for hiking, running, and biking\n\nOctober 9th, 2026, updated with a new best for hiking and new winner for best for cold weatherHome > Gear Reviews > Apparel\n\nWet feet can turn an otherwise great hike, ride, or run into a miserable day. Waterproof socks add another barrier between your feet and rain, puddles, wet trails, and water that makes its way past your shoes, while still allowing more breathability than an improvised plastic barrier.\n\nI tested waterproof socks while hiking, biking, running, and walking in wet Midwest conditions, including muddy trails, steady rain, puddles, and long rides in bad weather. I also submerged the socks in water to compare how well they kept moisture out and how they performed after getting thoroughly wet.\n\nI evaluated each pair for waterproofing, fit, comfort, breathability, durability, and drying performance. I also paid attention to sock height and cuff design, since keeping water from entering through the top can matter just as much as the waterproof membrane itself.\n\nTop Pick: The Sealskinz Dunton Waterproof All Weather Ankle Length Sock with Hydrostop is our Best Overall w"},{"text":"aterproof sock. It kept my feet dry during steady-rain hiking, dried quickly, and has a secure Hydrostop cuff that helped prevent water from running into the sock from above. It’s lighter and less cushioned than some of the thicker options I tested, but it offered the best overall balance of waterproof protection, fit, breathability, and durability.\n\nIf you’re interested in warm, wool socks for cold weather, check out our Best Winter Socks guide.\n\nWe create reader-supported, mission-driven objective gear reviews that are independently selected by our editors. This story may contain affiliate links, which help fund our website. When you click on the links to purchase gear, we may get a commission — without costing you an extra cent. Thank you for supporting our work and mission of outdoor coverage for every body! Learn more.\n\nComparison Table\n\nThe Winners\n\nBest Overall waterproof socks: Sealskinz Dunton All Weather Ankle Sock with Hydrostop\n\nMaterial: 90% Nylon, 10% ElastanePrice: $60Height options: ankleAvailable sizes: S - XLWhat we liked: Technical Hydrostop elastic taping to secure sock to ankle and prevent water from getting in, durable, and washableWhat we didn’t like: Expensi"},{"text":"ve, inner material is less comfortable than other socks\n\nOne of the most technical waterproof socks we tested, the Dunton uses Sealskinz’s Hydrostop technology. This design element features elastic taping around the inside cuff of the sock to help act as an extra barrier to reduce water dripping down the leg and into the cuff of the sock.\n\nThese socks are ideal for hiking and backpacking, when you’re most likely to be moving on varied terrain that may normally cause the cuff of the sock to shift and move while you hike. The Hydrostop elastic helps prevent the risk of water getting inside of your sock by keeping it secure around your ankle.\n\nThese socks performed best while hiking in rainy conditions, keeping moisture out of the inside of my socks in a steady rain while hiking on trails in and around Chicago. We found they dried quickly after taking them off and leaving them to air dry. Another feature I appreciated was their low-maintenance care and treatment. You can tumble dry these socks on low heat, and because they’re built with a durable membrane, you don’t need to re-waterproof them throughout their lifetime.\n\nSealskinz Dunton All Weather waterproof ankle socks with Sealskin"},{"text":"an find on the market.\n\nRANDY SUN Ultra Thin Mid Length waterproof socks were, by far, the thinnest and lightest weight socks of our testing pool.\n\nThe RANDY SUN Ultra Thin socks are especially ideal for summer and warm-season hiking, biking, and/or running. However, their durability is slightly challenged because of their thinner build. We also found these socks did not dry as quickly as competitors like the Bridgedale StormSock, even though they’re a thinner sock. This has to do with the material they’re made of, which is 96% polypropylene. You might also be unwilling to buy these socks as polypropylene often contains PFAS chemicals or “forever chemicals,” which have a negative impact on the environment.\n\nWhile most people recommend wearing a liner sock underneath waterproof socks for comfort and extra warmth, I found that these socks don’t need it and feel comfortable without a liner. Still, we recommend normal-thickness waterproof socks, like the Sealskinz Dunton or Bircham, for long-distance hiking and trekking, as they will be more durable over time.\n\nBest waterproof socks for running and trail running: Drymile ACTIVE\n\nMaterial: 96% Nylon / 3% Spandex 1% Reflective YarnPrice:"},{"text":"ale StormSock is an ideal pick if you’re a hiker, biker, or camper looking for a reliable pair of waterproof socks. We put these socks to the test on a 70-mile bike ride in a continuous downpour, and on casual hikes in the Midwest rainy season. Their wide fit around the ankle, superior room in the toe and arch of the foot, and added cushion throughout won us over.\n\nThe most comfortable, cushioned high-performance waterproof sock of our testing pool, the Bridgedale StormSock rose to the top of the list for our go-to pick for all outdoor activities.\n\nThese midweight socks fall within a happy medium of weight (not too thick, not too thin) meaning they transition well across seasons. The StormSock uses the brand’s proprietary Merino FusionTech liner and a durable nylon outer sock with waterproof treatment to make them extremely breathable and water resistant. We dunked the socks in a bucket of water to test their water resistance and to see how fast they’d dry. They outperformed all other socks in our testing pool for both water repellency and drying time.\n\nWhile Bridgedale did not design the StormSock with a completely seamless toe, the seams at the toe and ankle are minimal. I found "},{"text":" ACTIVE on a particularly rainy Midwest day, finding they kept their feet not only dry but warm in chilly temps.\n\nWaterproof socks are a niche piece of gear, but in the right conditions they can make the difference between finishing your trip in comfort or slogging home with frozen, wrinkled feet. They work best in situations where your footwear alone can’t keep moisture out:\n\nAll-day rain: Even the best trail runners and hiking boots will eventually wet through in hours of steady rain. A waterproof sock adds a final barrier between your foot and the elements.\n\nMud and slush: On shoulder-season trails or during spring melt, waterproof socks keep your feet dry when mud and melting snow push in through mesh shoes.\n\nSnow travel: If you don’t have insulated boots, a waterproof sock can help keep snowmelt from chilling your feet. Pair with a warm liner sock for extra insulation.\n\nWater crossings: Shallow creeks or boggy meadows are less of a worry when you know your socks won’t instantly soak through.\n\nThat said, waterproof socks are not an everyday replacement for wool hiking socks. They run warmer than standard socks and can feel clammy in hot weather. They also can’t stop water from "},{"text":"pouring in over the cuff if you wade too deep. For most hikers, runners, and bikers, waterproof socks are a problem-solving piece of gear: stash them in your pack and pull them on when you know you’ll be in hours of rain, snow, or mud.\n\nIf you want to extend their usefulness, combine waterproof socks with gaiters or rain pants to seal off the top and prevent water from sneaking in at your ankles. And always keep a dry backup pair of regular socks in your kit — just in case conditions outlast even the best waterproofing.\n\nWhat to look for in waterproof socks\n\nConsider whether you want a lightweight sock that will wick moisture in the heat or a thicker sock that fends off winter temperatures.\n\nHere are the criteria that every good pair of waterproof socks must have to rest assured your feet will stay safe, protected, and dry on your adventures:\n\nMaterial\n\nMaterials play a big role in the overall performance, durability, fit, and comfort of a waterproof sock. According to the Portland brand Showers Pass, waterproof socks are typically constructed with three key elements: knit exterior, waterproof membrane, and knit interior.\n\nIn general terms, the combination of material used in the d"},{"text":" and if they came out smell-free. From our research, we learned it’s key to wash waterproof socks inside out and we washed each pair of socks we tested in the washing machine before tumble drying and air drying.\n\nDuring the drying process, we ran two tests: let them tumble dry on a low heat cycle and, alternatively, let them air dry on a drying rack. Durability also plays a role in the washing process. We monitored for any pilling of fabric or fraying of stitches caused by washing each pair.\n\nPros and Cons of Waterproof Socks\n\nWaterproof socks have their time and place in the outdoors. While not every bike ride, long-distance trek, or casual hike requires them, it’s helpful to have a pair of waterproof socks in your gear closet for rainy, snowy, or humid weather. And while they are an investment, they’re also definitely better than wet, rubbing, or blistering feet…and are a much better option than plastic bag liners. Literally, people put plastic sandwich bags on their feet to serve as a barrier between the rain and their bare skin. Not only is this not sustainable but plastic has no added breathability, meaning whatever sweat builds up inside the bag will surround your feet. Breat"},{"text":"g water to bead and roll off the surface instead of soaking in. This allows the outer layer to stay dry in light rain or snow.\n\nIt’s important to note that DWR alone does not completely prevent water from penetrating hiking boots in the long term, including lightweight hiking boots without a waterproof membrane. When the outer fibers of a boot or shoe that relies only on DWR become saturated, or if they’re compromised from excessive wear and tear, water can eventually seep through.\n\nEven the most robust waterproof rain boots provide a seal around your entire foot yet water can seep in through the collar, especially during extreme downpours or over longer periods spent outdoors. This can especially be an issue with waterproof hiking shoes, which have low collars.\n\nThe seal of a waterproof sock provides an extra layer of protection should water enter your boot or shoe. They also tend to have a tighter fit around the ankle to prevent water from seeping in. When paired together in inclement weather, the combination of waterproof socks and waterproof boots or shoes can have a major impact on your ability to keep your feet dry, warm, and comfortable while adventuring outdoors.\n\nCare and "},{"text":"ens of socks over thousands of miles of hiking, here are the best wool and synthetic hiking and backpacking socks — hiking socks to prevent blisters, the best socks for hiking in hot weather, compression, and more."}]}
{"item_id":"feed:https://www.treelinereview.com/gearreviews/best-sleeping-pad-inflators","version_id":"678284250968c73e7b8f391aedae91c9d1fddc3fdad6b309f0fd685c1e536b86","kind":"feed","publisher_id":"treelinereview","title":"Best Sleeping Pad Inflators of 2026","url":"https://www.treelinereview.com/gearreviews/best-sleeping-pad-inflators","published_at":"2026-10-08T21:40:00Z","source_notes":{"perspective":"independent","notes":"Hands-on outdoor gear testing: boots, socks, gloves, layers, sleep systems, packs. Affiliate model."},"selection_reason":"Under Relevance, Freshness and novelty, and Credibility, this newly reported 1.5-year comparison provides citable sleep-system evidence relevant to carried weight and camp chores after exertion, including roughly two-minute Lux inflation, external-battery runtime, adapter difficulties, and field exposure to sand and varied temperatures. Commercial incentives requires disclosing Treeline's affiliate commissions and discounting its praise as independent confirmation; Conflicting evidence and Uncertainty require flagging inconsistent weight and noise figures and distinguishing measured results from speculative water resistance and projected inflation counts. Suitability and official requirements supports practical weight, power, and compatibility tradeoffs without specifying required status; this single publisher can contribute evidence but cannot alone meet Uncertainty's three-publisher standard for an article's central argument.","excerpted":true,"passages":[{"text":"Best Sleeping Pad Inflators of 2026\n\nPad pumps for inflatable sleeping pads for backpacking and camping mattresses\n\nOctober 8th, 2026Home → Gear Reviews → Backpacking & Thru-Hiking\n\nFor ultralight backpackers, thru-hikers, and backpackers of all types, inflating a sleeping pad is the worst camp chore. It either requires huffing and puffing or capturing air in a stuff sack and pumping it, which is significantly less fun to do if it is windy, buggy, cold, rainy, or you're at altitude. With 3.5\" and 4\" inflatable backpacking pads becoming the trend, there is more inflating needed than ever. Pad pumps, aka sleeping pad inflators, use batteries and fans to inflate your sleeping pad, eliminating one of the worst camp chores.\n\nElectric pad inflators are USB-powered or battery-powered, and are faster and require less human power and attention than manual pumps, pump sacks that come with your pad, or breathing into your pad. Pad pumps are often lighter than manual pump sacks, too. This guide doesn't cover manual sleeping pad inflators, which I find annoying and way less convenient than electric pad inflators.\n\nWe spent 1.5 years testing as many sleeping pad inflators as we could find, from "},{"text":"those that work for every backpacking pad or camping mattress out there to ones that only work for one manufacturer. We tested while backpacking on the Arizona Trail and off-trail on the Grand Enchantment Trail, as well as while backpacking in Northern California, the Washington Coast, the Eastern Sierra, Colorado, and while thru-hiking the West Coast Trail. We also ran side-by-side lab tests, noting inflation time on the most popular standard test pads, battery drain, valve compatibility, and noise measured by decibels.\n\nTo judge pad pumps, we considered weight, inflation speed, battery life, power sources, valve compatibility, noise level, durability, and value. We covered everything from sub-30g USB fans to 100g+ multi-function electric vacuum pumps. Ultimately, the right pick depends heavily on what pad you have and how much you value weight vs. speed.\n\nTop pick: The Alpenglow Gear Alpenblow Lux is the best overall backpacking sleeping pad inflator. With an inflator, deflator, easy USB-C operation off your phone, and features like a noise machine and camping lantern, it has quickly become a must-have for ultralight backpackers looking to reduce their camp chores and is so cleve"},{"text":"r, traditional backpackers will love it, too.\n\nWe create reader-supported, objective gear reviews independently selected by our editors. This story may contain affiliate links, which help fund our website. When you click on the links to purchase gear, we may get a commission — without costing you an extra cent. Thank you for supporting our work and mission of outdoor coverage for every body! Learn more.\n\nComparison table\n\nThe winners\n\nBest overall sleeping pad inflator: Alpenglow Gear Alpenblow Lux\n\nPrice: $60Weight: 0.5 ozPower source: USB-C from phone or power bankInflation time (regular pad): ~2 minDeflate function: YesNoise level: Quiet. 43 dB at 30 cm (according to Alpenglow Gear specs)Additional features: Lantern, white noise, ocean noiseWhat we liked: Extremely light, can deflate and inflate, bright colors make it harder to lose, lantern and white noise modes make it multi-useWhat we didn't like: Snapping on adapter valves is annoying (especially between inflate and deflate)\n\nFor most backpackers, the Alpenglow Gear Alpenblow Lux is the best overall backpacking sleeping pad inflator. This new version of the Alpenglow Alpenblow wins for its full features, including an inflato"},{"text":"r, deflator, lantern, and noise machine. It is incredibly battery efficient. Since it is a system that runs off your battery pack or phone instead of requiring its own charging, you don't have to worry about charging a separate battery (and the time and USB ports it requires in a trail town to do so). It runs off USB-C and barely uses power, is relatively quiet, and weighs less than 0.5 oz, making switching to a pad pump a minimal weight penalty for backpackers.\n\nWe were able to run the Alpenblow Lux for 7 hours and 35 minutes off one fully-charged 10,000 mAh battery pack, the longest of any pad pump we tested. Using those numbers as estimates, at 2 minutes per inflation, by our measurements, you could potentially get 227 inflations before needing to recharge.\n\nSee more in our in-depth review of the Alpenglow Gear Alpenblow Lux.\n\nThe Alpenblow Lux connects to a sleeping pad to handle inflation without having to sit and blow up the pad by mouth.\n\nThe Alpenglow Gear Alpenblow Lux weighs less than half an ounce, making it easy to justify carrying even for ultralight backpacking.\n\nA tester uses the Alpenblow Lux to inflate a NEMO sleeping pad; the pump works with adapters for NEMO and "},{"text":"enjoy doing.\n\nWe also appreciate the adaptability of the Alpenblow Lux, which works with nearly every brand of backpacking pad. The Alpenglow Lux comes with four sleeping pad valve adapters, which are clearly marked with diagrams on its packing tube. You can buy adapters for three other brands of sleeping pads. It comes with four valve adapters that work for Therm-a-Rest, NEMO, Sea to Summit, Big Agnes, and EXPED pads.\n\nWhile not the fastest or lightest pump out there, the Alpenblow Lux hit all the marks we looked for in a pad inflator and then some without costing more than competitors. The Lux is the best overall pad inflator for backpackers, ultralighters, and anyone who hates inflating their backpacking pad.\n\nThe Alpenblow Lux took us about 2 minutes to fully inflate a NEMO Tensor All-Season sleeping pad. It registered only 40 dB on our noise-measuring app, which is better than we can say for other pad pumps. After it finished filling, it requires about two additional mouth inflations to get this pad as firm as I like— it’s unfortunate that the Alpenblow can't fully achieve that level of firmness, like the FLEXTAIL ZERO PUMP can. Still, I'm willing to do two cursory breaths bef"},{"text":"ze.\n\nUnlike the Alpenglow models, it has a small, internal lithium battery that charges via USB-C while you are in town. A full charge is good for 25 pumps. You have to pop the battery out of the inflator to charge it. Since it is separate and a self-contained battery system, it won't run down your phone battery or your power bank. That leaves you free to use those items to charge your other electronics.\n\nThe Zero Pump really shines for its fully-enclosed fan and protected USB-C port, that aid in durability. Because the battery charges via a USB-C cord that stores inside the unit, you don't have to worry about grit getting in the USB-C port like you do with the Alpenglow models. Unlike the FLEXTAIL Tiny Pump, it doesn't have an explicit water resistance rating. But because of the lack of open ports, I would feel much more comfortable accidentally exposing this to some raindrops than other pad pumps.\n\nThe FLEXTAIL ZERO PUMP comes with rubber adapter nozzles that work on 6 types of pads. It isn't that loud, registering 80 decibels on our decibel app.\n\nThe FLEXTAIL weighs an ounce more than the Alpenblow systems (1.7 oz compared to 0.3 oz and 0.5 oz for the Alpenblow and Alpenblow Lux"},{"text":", respectively) and is about the same price as the Alpenglow models. It's also black, which isn't as easy to find as the bright pink Alpenglows if you accidentally left it on a log.\n\nBest ultralight sleeping pad inflator: Alpenglow Gear Alpenblow Classic\n\nPrice: $60Weight: 0.3 ozPower source: USB-C from phone or power bankInflation time (regular pad): 3-5 minDeflate function: NoNoise level: Quiet. 58 dBAdditional features: N/AWhat we liked: Extremely light, lots of adapters, quiet, runs off USB-C, pink and purple is easy to find while packing up campWhat we didn't like: Not as fast as other inflators, not as fully featured\n\nThe Alpenglow Gear Alpenblow is the original Alpenblow inflator. It has many of the features we liked in the Alpenblow Lux, including running off USB-C battery packs or your USB-C-powered phone.\n\nWhile it isn't quite as fast as Alpenblow Lux or the FLEXTAIL ZERO PUMP, the original Alpenblow weighs less—just 0.3 oz. At the end of the day, we often end up spending 3 minutes doing camp chores, so the difference between 2 minutes of the Lux and 3 minutes of the original isn't a huge deal. The original Alpenglow is also noticeably quieter than the Lux and wins the aw"},{"text":"s super annoying, and we think it is worth getting the camping mattress inflator whose battery will never die so you aren't stuck manually pumping.\n\nInflation speed\n\nDifferent backpackers and campers value inflation speed differently. It depends on how patient of a person you are. For backpacking, I think getting a slower inflation speed is fine because I'll set up my pad pump and then do other camp chores while I am waiting for it. One of my backpacking partners likes to stare at the pad while it is inflating and cannot multitask, so speed matters a lot more to him. Figure out your camp chore style and choose accordingly.\n\nFor what it is worth, many of these inflators can be plugged in and left to do their thing. The pump will usually stall or stop pushing air before the pad bursts. But check with your specific pad inflators' instructions before deciding to step away from your pad whilst inflating.\n\nBattery life and power consumption\n\nWe tested how much battery life a pad pump uses based on power consumption to inflate a standard pad and run-time (for self-contained units). In most cases, you can measure the battery life on pumps in terms of runtime or the number of inflations. Th"},{"text":"tion time, battery drain per inflation, noise level (using the Decibel X decibel testing app), valve fit across multiple pad brands, and signs of cracked or broken parts or sand clogging fans or USB-C ports over a trip. We can't honestly say we always put them inside on a totally clean tent floor. We always used a groundsheet when setting up while cowboy camping. We usually stored it in a DCF ditty bag but sometimes, it ended up in a backpacking pack's hipbelt pocket, where it had the potential for more exposure to water and sand.\n\nTreeline Review creates objective gear reviews on gear we find interesting, independently selected by our editors. Treeline Review does not accept guest posts, pop-up ads, or link placement.\n\nBackpacking and car camping trips\n\nSide-by-side inflation-time tests\n\nBattery drain measurements\n\nNoise testing with a decibel app\n\nValve compatibility across pad brands\n\nInflation speed\n\nBattery efficiency\n\nNoise level\n\nValve fit and compatibility\n\nDurability and reliability\n\nWhy you should trust us / About the author\n\nI’ve backpacked over 20,000 miles and have at least 1,000 nights sleeping outdoors. On most of these overnight trips, I’ve slept on inflatable sleep"},{"text":"g the best headlamps for brightness, battery life, comfort, and ease of use for backpacking, hiking, running, climbing, and camping."}]}
{"item_id":"feed:https://www.treelinereview.com/gearreviews/best-mens-winter-boots","version_id":"85df28e1e6b983751fc364c96f8060a9d025a6fe52989cc6bed14e98cb8e0d5c","kind":"feed","publisher_id":"treelinereview","title":"Best Winter Boots for Men of 2026","url":"https://www.treelinereview.com/gearreviews/best-mens-winter-boots","published_at":"2026-10-08T19:55:00Z","source_notes":{"perspective":"independent","notes":"Hands-on outdoor gear testing: boots, socks, gloves, layers, sleep systems, packs. Affiliate model."},"selection_reason":"Under Relevance, Credibility, and Making the decision, the described winter hiking experience—including VECTIV use on Katahdin in January, compromised protection when saturated, and heavy boots' walking limitations—provides citable evidence for Pieces 02 and 03 on warmth, wet-weather performance, and endurance tradeoffs. Commercial incentives requires noting affiliate commissions despite the author's stated absence of brand sponsorship; Uncertainty and Freshness and novelty require attributing these observations to one publisher, distinguishing manufacturer temperature ratings from field results, and not assuming the 2026 update establishes new testing or that decades-old Caribou durability proves current-model performance. Suitability and official requirements leaves required status unspecified because no official Mammoth passage is supplied.","excerpted":true,"passages":[{"text":"Best Winter Boots for Men of 2026\n\nWinter Boots for Warmth, Water Resistance, Fit, Comfort, And Ice Traction\n\nOctober 8, 2026Home > Gear Reviews > Footwear\n\nCold is no stranger to me. I grew up in Colorado and have spent winters in Alaska, Maine, and New Hampshire, where winter boots have had to handle everything from deep snow and icy streets to slush, outdoor work, and long days in the cold.\n\nWe’ve tested men’s winter boots from Alaska and Maine to Colorado, Iowa, Quebec, and the Yukon. I’ve also spent years working and recreating outside in winter, including guiding in Alaska and Colorado and responding to Search and Rescue, ambulance, and fire calls during some seriously cold conditions. That experience has given me a pretty good sense of what works and what doesn’t when your feet need to stay warm, dry, and comfortable for hours.\n\nFor this guide, we looked most closely at traction, waterproofness, fit, comfort, shaft height, and warmth. We also considered how each boot worked for its intended use, whether that meant walking around town, hiking, working outside, or dealing with extreme cold.\n\nTop Pick: The SOREL Caribou is our favorite overall. My dad bought his pair in 1992, a"},{"text":"nd I’ve watched him wear them through winters in New York, Colorado, and Maine. The newest model is the same, and the removable 9 mm liner, waterproof leather, roomy fit, and strong traction make them especially well suited to cold, snowy conditions. His pair lasted long enough to need a replacement liner only after about 20 years of use.\n\nLooking for women's winter boots? See our Best Women's Winter Boots guide.\n\nWe create reader-supported, objective gear reviews independently selected by our editors. This story may contain affiliate links, which help fund our website. When you click on the links to purchase gear, we may get a commission — without costing you an extra cent. Thank you for supporting our work and mission of outdoor coverage for every body! Learn more.\n\nComparison table\n\n* Prices reflect MSRP and are subject to change. We note any discounts we see on our Deals page, which is updated daily.\n\nThe winners\n\nBest overall men’s winter boot and best winter boot for extreme cold: SOREL Caribou\n\nInsulation: Removable 9 mm washable felt inner boot with snow cuffShaft Height: 9.25\"Temperature Rating: -40°FFootwear Closure: LaceWaterproof: YesWhat we liked: long-term durability,"},{"text":"Caribous aren't just practical—they’ve become a symbol of reliability through years of snowy adventures. If you need dependable boots for extreme cold, with a legacy to match, the SORELCaribou remains a top contender.\n\nThey’re so beloved that they are also the winner of our Women’s Winter Boot for Extreme Cold.\n\nFor even more details, you can read our in-depth review of the SOREL Caribou.\n\nBest men's winter boots for walking: Blundstone Classic Chelsea Boots\n\nInsulation: None (insulated version available)Shaft Height: 4.5” Temperature Rating: None stated (insulated version also unrated)Footwear Closure: Slip on, over the ankleWaterproof: No, water-resistantWhat we liked: Classic look, well-made, lasts many years and becomes more comfortable with more wear, water-resistantWhat we didn’t like: Not suited for extreme cold or deep snow\n\nIf you’re looking for a winter boot that can go from the office to the barn and on to the bar, the Blundstones may very well be the best choice for you. With a few technological upgrades—like their cushioning Shock Protection System (SPS) midsole—they’ve kept the classic look while keeping the footwear relevant for a new era. SPS is designed to disperse"},{"text":" shock when your foot strikes the ground, which reduces the amount of stress on your skeletal system. (Read: comfier, happier feet and joints!)\n\nBlundstone leather boots also have removable, anatomically correct EVA footbeds for extra cushion and support. The soles hold a 4mm tread for good grip on ice, slush, mud, and pavement alike. The soles also hold a steel shank that runs down the middle, providing enough rigidity to support and stabilize these ankle boots.\n\nThe Blundstones are a breeze to slip on and off. While the majority of the boot is leather, the stretchy sides are made of elastic to aid with easy on-and-off. Front and back loops add to the ease.\n\nAlthough an Australian-based company, Blundstone offers plenty of warm options. Love the Blundstone look but need more insulation? We also tested the Blundstone Thermal All-Terrain boot, a thermal waterproof boot with Thinsulate and shearling insulation.\n\nYou can read our in-depth review of those Blundstone Thermal boots for more information.\n\nBest men's casual winter boots: L.L.Bean Flannel-Lined Insulated Bean Boots\n\nInsulation: 200 g PrimaLoftUpper Lining: 100% cotton brushed chamois cloth; 7.5 oz per sq. yd.Bottom Unit Lin"},{"text":"tester has owned (and thrashed) his pair of flannel-lined Bean boots for 8 years and counting and still calls them “the waterproof-est and only boot I’ll wear in New England winter. But if you run warm, beware of a little boot sweat.” He suggests sizing down a half-size unless you plan to wear expedition-weight wool socks with them, as these boots do run large.\n\nIf you want even more information, see our in-depth review of the L.L.Bean Boots.\n\nBest men’s winter boots for hiking: The North Face VECTIV Fastpack Insulated\n\nInsulation: 200 g Shaft Height: Mid-ankleTemperature Rating: None statedFootwear Closure: LaceWaterproof: Yes, Waterproof/breathable DryVent membraneWhat we liked: Comfortable, lightweight, good groundfeel, thoughtful unique featuresWhat we didn't like: When fully saturated, the midfoot and toe box protection was somewhat compromised\n\nThe North Face VECTIV Fastpack Insulated men’s winter boots won our award for Best Winter Boots for Hiking thanks to their snow-tire-like tread, their grippy rubber soles, and their comfortable feel that enhances propulsion and protects the foot.\n\nWhile a lot of winter hiking boots feel like walking with bricks on your feet, these warm"},{"text":"ve a D-ring to work with winter hiking gaiters, so they play well with other winter hiking gear.\n\nFor most hikers, we think the VECTIV Fastpack will be all you’ll need.\n\nBest men's winter work boots: Muck Boot Arctic Sport Boots\n\nInsulation: 5 mm neoprene, fleece linedShaft Height: 16”Temperature Rating: -40°FFootwear Closure: Pull-onWaterproof: YesWhat we liked: Very convenient when working outside and coming inside throughout the day, waterproof, warm, easy slip-on/pull-off, arch supportWhat we didn’t like: Heel rubbing, tight on wider shins after prolonged use\n\nIf you don’t want to deal with lacing up your boots every time you go outside, the Muck Boot Arctic Sport Boots are for you. The Muck Boot Arctic Sports are warm, waterproof, durable, and exactly what you want on your feet in harsh conditions and extreme cold.\n\nCalled the workhorse of the men’s boot world for a reason, the Arctic Sports are way less of a fashion statement than our winner for walking, the Blundstones, or L.L.Bean Boots, but infinitely more functional.\n\nWhile definitely on the heavier side for winter boots, the Arctic Sports are filled with 5 mm of neoprene insulation, lined with fleece, and have 2 mm of th"},{"text":"are mostly hiking, you may be able to get away with some hiking boots or even lightweight hiking boots depending on temperatures and conditions.\n\nFor more, see tips from our guide on what to wear winter hiking.\n\nAnd learn basic snowshoeing skills before you head out.\n\nFor cold-weather travel\n\nHeading out for a ski trip or visiting a colder climate?\n\nChoose lightweight, packable boots that are easy to slip on and off.\n\nInsulation and waterproofing are important, but you may not need heavy-duty options if your time outdoors is limited.\n\nFor outdoor work\n\nWinter work boots should prioritize:\n\nEase of use: Slip-on options are convenient for quick tasks, while lace-ups provide extra support for long hours.\n\nDurability: Reinforced toes and rugged soles stand up to wear and tear.\n\nWarmth and waterproofing: Insulated, weatherproof designs are a must for staying comfortable.\n\nQuick tips for common concerns:\n\nCold feet? Try boots with thicker insulation or a fleece lining for extra warmth.\n\nOverheating? Opt for lightly insulated or non-insulated boots, especially for active use.\n\nDeep snow? Look for taller shafts or add gaiters to your setup.\n\nWet conditions? Waterproof materials and sealed "},{"text":"em may make an otherwise good pair more comfortable again.\n\nStorage\n\nWhen the snow melts and it’s time to start thinking about flip-flops, clean your boots and let them dry completely before putting them away. Storing damp boots is an easy way to end up with mildew, odor, or damaged materials by next winter.\n\nIdeally, store them somewhere cool and dry where they won’t get crushed. If you’re like me and don’t exactly have your gear storage on lock, under the bed works too. The important part is making sure they’re clean and completely dry first.\n\nFAQ\n\nCan I use hiking boots in the winter?\n\nSometimes. A waterproof hiking boot can work well in mild winter conditions, especially if you’re moving and don’t need much insulation. I’ve worn hiking boots in winter plenty of times when the temperatures and snow conditions made sense.\n\nWhere they start to fall short is deep snow, serious cold, or long periods standing around outside. Many hiking boots aren’t tall enough to keep snow out and don’t have the insulation of a true winter boot. If you mostly hike in warmer months and only occasionally get out in winter, though, you may not need a separate heavy winter hiking boot.\n\nAre all winter b"},{"text":"acrificing the grip or comfort needed to actually spend time outside in winter.\n\nFrom there, we looked more closely at what mattered for each award. A winter hiking boot needed traction and support for icy terrain and enough versatility for different conditions. A work boot needed to stay warm and comfortable through long stretches outside while still being easy to get on and off. Boots for city use had different priorities, including comfort, traction, and practicality for moving between indoor and outdoor environments.\n\nWe used those field observations alongside product specifications and research to decide which boots made the most sense for each use case.\n\nSnow and icy terrain\n\nDeep-cold winter conditions\n\nWet snow and slush\n\nWinter hiking and outdoor work\n\nLong-term everyday winter use\n\nTraction on snow and ice\n\nWaterproofness\n\nFit and comfort\n\nWarmth and insulation\n\nShaft height and intended use\n\nAuthor’s Expertise: Why you should trust us\n\nCold is no stranger to me. I grew up in Colorado with New York-expat parents and spent winters in Alaska, Maine, and New Hampshire. I’m no stranger to cold feet. I wouldn’t give up the cold for a sunny beach day, ever — but I do prefer to "},{"text":"assador, or an employee of any of the companies mentioned here. This is unbiased, and we do this to help you find the best product for the price. Treeline Review doesn’t accept sponsored content, native advertising, or paid reviews.\n\nYou can read all of my articles on my author page.\n\nWe reviewed 27 men’s ski jackets to find the 7 best hardshells, backcountry, insulated, 3-in-1, sub-$350, and waterproof coats. Here are our top rated winners.\n\nProtect your head on the slopes with the best ski and snowboard helmets, reviewed and tested by our Treeline experts. Get ready for your next snow adventure!\n\nLooking for a way to stay upright in icy conditions? Sure-footed traction in snow and ice allows you to carry your outdoor life into the shoulder seasons and beyond."}]}
{"item_id":"feed:https://www.treelinereview.com/gearreviews/exped-terra-sleeping-bag-review","version_id":"9b22e426245124bef28813636a999eca8b200950b63c6e6ccd0028b7a03f4d41","kind":"feed","publisher_id":"treelinereview","title":"Exped Terra Sleeping Bag Review: Big Down Warmth Without the Big Price","url":"https://www.treelinereview.com/gearreviews/exped-terra-sleeping-bag-review","published_at":"2026-10-08T14:10:00Z","source_notes":{"perspective":"independent","notes":"Hands-on outdoor gear testing: boots, socks, gloves, layers, sleep systems, packs. Affiliate model."},"selection_reason":"Under Relevance, Freshness and novelty, and Credibility, nine months of newly reported sleep-system testing provides citable observations relevant to Mammoth carry and overnight comfort, including 9 L compressed volume and zipper snagging that challenges the anti-snag claim, supporting pieces 01, 02, and 03. Commercial incentives and Uncertainty require noting Treeline's affiliate commissions, attributing its findings to this single publisher, and limiting warmth conclusions to the above-freezing conditions actually tested; three independent publishers would still be needed for an article's central argument. Under Suitability and official requirements, required status remains unspecified because no official Mammoth passage is supplied.","excerpted":true,"passages":[{"text":"Exped Terra Sleeping Bag Review: Big Down Warmth Without the Big Price\n\nA down mummy bag for backpackers who need reliable warmth at an excellent price\n\nOctober 8th, 2026Home > Gear Reviews > Backpacking\n\nOur verdict\n\nThe Exped Terra sleeping bag is a down sleeping bag for backpackers and campers looking to level up their game without spending too much money. If you already own a sleeping bag and have found it insufficient, the Exped Terra is a quality bag that gives you a lot of features for a reasonable price.\n\nIt's soft, lofty, relatively wide, and comfortable, and comes with Exped's legendary repairs and customer service. It comes with its own compression sack (usually an extra $40) and compacts down surprisingly well.\n\nMultiple Treeline Review testers took the Exped Terra 15 out for nine months of testing, including a month straight of camping in the Sierra at 8,000 feet and camping in Pinnacles National Park in the spring. We had testers of multiple sizes, heights, and familiarity with camping test this bag. Some of our testers were self-described \"cold sleepers,\" whereas others run warmer. We had side, belly, and back sleepers in the test pool.\n\nExped Terra Sleeping Bag\n\nThe"},{"text":" Exped Terra comes in multiple sizes, lengths, and temperatures, so you can find the one that works best for your body and where you do your trips. This is important because sleeping bags insulate best when there's the least amount of space inside the bag for your body heat to warm it up, and when your body parts aren't putting pressure on the down loft (which reduces its insulating ability). In other words, it’s important that the bag's size matches your height and build. Having the Terra available in so many sizes also means you can have matching bags for everyone in your family or group, which can reduce decision fatigue.\n\nIt's worth noting that the Exped Terra 30 has a Comfort Rating of 39°F and a Lower Limit of 30°F, so when you're choosing your bag, consider whether you sleep hot or cold. Our main tester, for example, sleeps colder, so the number on the bag's name is not the temperature at which she would have wanted to take this bag.\n\nIf you've wanted to upgrade to a down sleeping bag that can be reliably used for backpacking, you'd be hard-pressed to find a better deal on a quality bag than the Exped Terra.\n\nWe create reader-supported, objective gear reviews independently s"},{"text":"elected by our editors. This story may contain affiliate links, which help fund our website. When you click on the links to purchase gear, we may get a commission — without costing you an extra cent. Thank you for supporting our work and mission of outdoor coverage for every body! Learn more.\n\nExped Terra Sleeping Bag specifications\n\nAll variants\n\nInsulation: 650+ grey duck down, RDS-certified, OEKO-TEX® 100 certified\n\nShell: 20D recycled ripstop polyester, OEKO-TEX® 100 certified, PFAS-free DWR\n\nLining: 20D recycled ripstop polyester, OEKO-TEX® 100 certified\n\nShape: Mummy\n\nZipper: Left-hand, anti-snag\n\nDraft tube: Yes\n\nDraft collar: Yes\n\nHood: Yes, contoured with drawcord\n\nInterior pocket: Yes\n\nStuff sack included: Yes (compression)\n\nStorage sack included: Yes\n\nSustainability: OEKO-TEX Standard 100 fabrics, RDS-certified down, recycled shell and lining, PFAS-free\n\nTerra 30°F (0°C)\n\nTerra 15°F (-9°C)\n\nTerra 0°F (-18°C)\n\nBest for\n\nCampers and backpackers purchasing their second sleeping bag after being disappointed with lower-quality down bags or synthetic sleeping bags\n\nCampers and backpackers who want one bag for both activities\n\nCampers and backpackers considering the NEMO Disco "},{"text":"who want similar features and to spend less money\n\nComparison table\n\nWhat we liked\n\nGood value for the features\n\n3D foot box means feet don't get cold as easily\n\nAvailable in lots of lengths and widths, so you could get the same bag for everyone in your group\n\nComes with a compression stuff sack, which usually costs extra\n\nRecycled shell and lining fabrics, OEKO-TEX 100 certified, with a PFAS-free DWR\n\nInternal pocket is handy to keep your phone from freezing overnight or keep your lip balm close by\n\nWhat could be better\n\nLimited color options\n\nIt's heavier than down sleeping bags that use higher fill (it only has a 650 fill)\n\nDoesn't use waterproof down\n\nOnly available with the zipper on the left side of the bag\n\nThe 30°F bag is independently tested to have a “Comfort rating” of 39°F and a “Lower Limit” of 30°F, so we think the model name should read 35°F instead of 30°F\n\nHow we tested\n\nMultiple Treeline Review testers used this sleeping bag for over a month straight while backpacking and camping in the Eastern Sierra. We also tested it while camping in Pinnacles National Park.\n\nWe've tested the Exped Terra sleeping bag using camping cots, camping mattresses, and on backpacking sl"},{"text":"eeping pads.\n\nTreeline testers have slept in the Exped Terra sleeping bag in camping tents, backpacking tents, and indoors in rustic cabins. We've even used it for sleepovers on the ground in the living room and on couches at friends' houses.\n\nWe've had back-sleeping, side-sleeping, and belly-sleeping testers. We had testers of different body shapes and sizes, ages, and metabolism rates, from self-described \"hot sleepers\" to \"cold sleepers.\"\n\nMost importantly, we tested the Exped Terra sleeping bag side by side against similarly priced bags: the REI Co-op HunkerDown 20, the Kelty Cosmic, and even a budget sleeping bag we got for free from a car insurance company.\n\nWe tested the Exped Terra for different needs, considering the trade-offs among price, weight, packability, long-term durability, features, and comfort. Warmth wasn't really negotiable.\n\nPerformance in the field\n\nWarmth and temperature accuracy\n\nWe tested the Exped Terra 15 sleeping bag and found it lofty, soft, and warm. We tested in temperatures above freezing. While we didn’t test at the limit, we were surprised by how warm, even hot, this bag felt, even for cold sleepers. Some of the other sleeping bags we tested side"},{"text":" by side that claim to be 15°F bags felt cold under similar conditions, even though we were testing them literally side by side in the same tent and on the same NEMO Tensor Extreme Conditions sleeping pad.\n\nIt's worth noting that Exped did their temperature rating assuming you are using a sleeping pad with an R-value of 7. R-value is a measure of insulative capability. The R-value of your sleeping pad can arguably be more important than your sleeping bag's temperature rating for keeping you warm. Most independent tests for sleeping bag warmth assume you're sleeping on a pad with an R-value of 5, whereas we occasionally see an R-value of 7+ be rated for -40°F temperatures. That means we take the temperature ratings listed in the name with a grain of salt.\n\nExped recommends using a sleeping pad with an R-value of at least 6.5. You can read more about R-value in our sleeping pads guide.\n\nComfort and fit\n\nThe mummy shape on the Exped Terra 15 sleeping bag was quite comfortable. We tested in a long wide size and found it reminiscent of the Spoon shape on the NEMO Disco sleeping bag. There was room to move; the hood was easy to secure. For cold sleepers, you may find the wide unnecessary"},{"text":", but we appreciated the ability to move around and roll throughout the night.\n\nMoisture performance\n\nThe Exped Terra uses 650-fill-power grey duck down that doesn't have any water-resistant treatment. The face fabric comes with a PFAS-free DWR (durable water repellent) that allows some moisture to bead up, but once the DWR wears off or the fabric becomes too saturated, moisture will reach the down and get wet.\n\nPackability\n\nWe were stoked that this bag came with a compression sack, as these usually cost $40 extra. Our 15°F long wide bag compressed down to 9 L. This didn't take up much room in our 60L backpacking pack and meant it was also easy to stuff into our car while car camping.\n\nDurability\n\nWe feel pretty confident stuffing, throwing, and crushing this bag. It doesn't require babying, which makes it a good choice for first-time backpackers, campers, or anyone who wants one bag to last a long time.\n\nAlthough it uses a robust zipper lined with webbing to prevent snagging, we found ourselves catching the shell fabric. We speculate this is because the shell fabric is thinner on the Terra than on the DeepSleep.\n\nFeatures\n\nSustainability and recycled materials\n\nUnlike the Exped De"},{"text":"bers.\n\nJenson\n\nPros: Price matching, free shipping, no membership required, limited-time sale offers.\n\nExped\n\nYou can sometimes find more options when purchasing directly from the manufacturer. Purchasing directly from the manufacturer may make it easier to process a warranty return for a defect.\n\nExped Terra FAQ\n\nHow do the three Exped Terra temperature ratings compare?\n\nThe Exped Terra 30°F is Comfort Rated to 39°F and has a Lower Limit of 30°F, which should keep \"hot sleepers\" comfortable. The Exped Terra 15°F has a tested Comfort rating of 25°F and a Lower Limit rating of 12°F. The Exped Terra 0°F has a Lower Limit of 1°F and a Comfort Rating of 14°F.\n\nIs the Exped Terra good for backpacking or just camping?\n\nThe Exped Terra is a down bag that is very compact (it comes with its own compression stuff sack) and relatively lightweight, making it a good sleeping bag for backpacking and camping.\n\nHow does the Exped Terra compare to the Exped DeepSleep?\n\nThe Exped Terra is a down sleeping bag that is lighter and more compact than the synthetic Exped DeepSleep. The Exped Terra costs more because down insulation often costs more than synthetic insulation, but down bags often last longe"},{"text":"r. Some down bag owners have found their bags still warm after 10+ years, whereas synthetic bags often feel colder after 3 years, depending on how you care for them.\n\nDoes the Exped Terra perform well in wet conditions?\n\nThe Exped Terra shell material has some durable water repellency (DWR), meaning raindrops and other precipitation will bead up on the surface and roll off. However, the Terra doesn't use waterproof down insulation, so once water penetrates the shell material, wet down will lose its insulating properties.\n\nWhat sizes does the Exped Terra come in?\n\nThe Terra is available in five sizes — Small, Medium, Medium Wide, Long and Long Wide — fitting people up to 67, 72 or 77 inches, with shoulder girths from 58 to 68 inches.\n\nIs the Exped Terra's temperature rating accurate?\n\nThe 30°F Terra's ISO Comfort is 39°F and Lower Limit 30°F, so cold sleepers should go with the 15°F for warmth. Testers found the 15°F warm, even for cold sleepers, in above-freezing conditions.\n\nDoes the Exped Terra come with a compression sack?\n\nYes. The Terra includes a compression stuff sack plus a storage sack, and the 15°F bag compresses to about 9 L.\n\nWhy you should trust us / About the author\n\n"},{"text":" as Yale and MIT, as well as Capitol Hill, the Trust for Public Land, the American Hiking Society, and BikeTexas.\n\nYou can read more about Liz on her Wikipedia page and on her Treeline author page!\n\nSkip the huffing and puffing. Find the best electric sleeping pad pumps, tested for weight, inflation speed, battery life, and compatibility with your pad.\n\nCold and warm sleepers tested the Exped Terra down sleeping bag for nine months. See how its temperature ratings, mummy fit, and packability held up.\n\nWe spent 400+ nights testing the best headlamps for brightness, battery life, comfort, and ease of use for backpacking, hiking, running, climbing, and camping."}]}
{"item_id":"feed:https://www.treelinereview.com/gearreviews/brawyn-essential-supreme-bra-review","version_id":"d31e9f337a8af67a512cb7f07814e94acf86a69cdd3058918f2608d24de42d6d","kind":"feed","publisher_id":"treelinereview","title":"BRANWYN Essential Supreme Bra Review: A Merino Sports Bra With Breathing Room","url":"https://www.treelinereview.com/gearreviews/brawyn-essential-supreme-bra-review","published_at":"2026-10-08T13:09:00Z","source_notes":{"perspective":"independent","notes":"Hands-on outdoor gear testing: boots, socks, gloves, layers, sleep systems, packs. Affiliate model."},"selection_reason":"Under Relevance, Credibility, and Freshness and novelty, four months of newly reported backpacking and heat testing provide citable observations about comfort under pack straps, repeated-wear odor, durability, and washing-hook problems, while limited bounce control constrains suitability for Mammoth. Under Commercial incentives and Uncertainty, Treeline earns affiliate commissions, its testers represent one publisher, and its findings warrant attribution rather than a complete Mammoth conclusion; prolonged cold and Mammoth-specific performance remain untested. The item supports Pieces 01, 02, and 03 through new field results, comparison with the manufacturer's light-support claim, and practical fit and activity tradeoffs under Suitability and official requirements, with required status unspecified.","excerpted":true,"passages":[{"text":"BRANWYN Essential Supreme Bra Review: A Merino Sports Bra With Breathing Room\n\nCenter ruching ends the uniboob, merino keeps the funk away, and it's comfy enough to forget you're wearing it.\n\nOctober 8, 2026Home > Gear Reviews > Apparel\n\nOur verdict\n\nBRANWYN’s Essential Supreme Bra, an evolution of their merino wool Essential Busty Bra, provides a solution for the dreaded “uniboob”—that is, the amorphous shape created when breast tissue is smooshed down while wearing a compressive, cup-less bra.\n\nAs my own, now-independent boobs can attest, BRANWYN succeeded in this quest by adding center ruching to create separation, along with sleeves for removable pads (which must be purchased separately) that provide additional definition.\n\nAs someone who fluctuates between a 36C and D cup, usually on the lower end, I’ve been wearing the BRANWYN Essential Supreme Bra in size medium since late spring 2026. I’ve worn it on day hikes and walks, backpacking trips, multi-day travels, at the gym, and around the house.\n\nThanks to its soft feel, odor resistance, contoured shape, lightweight support, and overall versatility, it’s become the bra I reach for most often. It’s kept me comfortable in a varie"},{"text":"ty of situations, including lifting weights, physical therapy, and backpacking in the mountains, and it fits well under a variety of clothing, including dressier options.\n\nThat said, the Essential Supreme Bra doesn’t provide enough support for higher-impact activities (although I have done a few very short, very light jogs while wearing it), and its sizing isn't as inclusive as it could be for those with larger busts. But it is a great all-around option for people who want a comfortable, everyday sports bra with light support that also gives your boobs a little bit of breathing room.\n\nBRANWYN Essential Supreme Bra\n\nWe create reader-supported, objective gear reviews independently selected by our editors. This story may contain affiliate links, which help fund our website. When you click on the links to purchase gear, we may get a commission — without costing you an extra cent. Thank you for supporting our work and mission of outdoor coverage for every body! Learn more.\n\nBRANWYN Essential Supreme Bra specifications\n\nPrice: $76 (set of optional cup pads is an additional $4.50)\n\nCup/band size range: Cup sizes from A to G, band sizes from 28” to 44”\n\nSupport level: Low\n\nStyle: Convertib"},{"text":"hing provides cup separation, which gives breathability and a more defined silhouette than cupless sports bras\n\nOptional pads provide additional definition and modesty\n\nThe straps are convertible and lie flat beneath backpack straps\n\nThe use of merino wool is great for comfort, odor resistance, and regulation of heat and moisture\n\nWhat could be better\n\nAs with most sports bras, the removable pads can migrate in the pocket and feel a bit thin overall\n\nFabric can get stuck in the hooks at the end of each strap if not hand-washed\n\nSizing could be more inclusive\n\nHow we tested\n\nThe Essential Supreme is made mostly of merino wool and is quick-drying; the author sweated profusely on this day, and the bra dried quickly in the sun.\n\nAs someone who fluctuates between a 36C and D cup, I chose the medium size, which provided just the right amount of support — not too tight and not too loose. The version I tested was the original model, which came with circular cup pads; in the newer model, the pads are an optional, separate purchase and are more triangular, creating a less obtrusive silhouette under the bra material.\n\nI spent four months testing the BRANWYN Essential Supreme Bra in a variety "},{"text":"of settings: as a daily driver at home and around town, during out-of-town travels where I wanted a jill-of-all-trades bra that could work as well during a morning hike as it could during an evening out, and for light-impact activities that included walking, hiking, backpacking, yoga, gym sessions (weightlifting, step machine, cycling), and physical therapy/mobility work.\n\nWeather conditions for testing ranged from a few nights with lows hovering around freezing (at high camps in the Sierra) to daytime temperatures climbing into the low triple digits.\n\nFellow Treeliners Liz Thomas and Stasia Stockwell also provided feedback on their experiences wearing the Essential Supreme Bra in similar, low-impact settings. We tested this bra alongside others in our Best Sports Bras for AA-D Cups, Best Sports Bras for D+ Cups, and Best Sports Bras for Hiking guides.\n\nPerformance in the field\n\nThe under-bust band is a generous 2.5” wide and lies relatively flat, although it can curl up a bit during heavier activity.\n\nSupport and bounce control\n\nBRANWYN indicates that the Essential Supreme Bra is meant for light activity, and that holds true. Choosing a bra on the lower end of the spectrum for my "},{"text":"cup and band size (a medium) meant I felt a bit more compression than I do in their Essential Busty Bra in a large.\n\nFellow tester Stasia agrees that it’s more supportive than the Busty Bra, “but would still consider it very much a low support/impact bra,” and I agree. For me, using the cup pads provided a little extra hold, as did crossing the straps in the back. I also felt that the thick (2.5”) band was more supportive under my bust than thinner-banded sports bras that I use, and I rarely have to fiddle with it to get it to lie flat during activity.\n\nI’ve felt perfectly supported across the range of activities I’ve tried while wearing this bra, and I haven't needed to adjust it in motion, even during heavy upper-body workouts (say, shoulder day at the gym). While I felt comfortable enough doing short, light jogs while testing, I wouldn’t use this bra for a real run or, say, a HIIT class, since it doesn’t provide enough support or compression for bounce control.\n\nComfort over extended wear\n\nI owned four BRANWYN bras before testing this one because they are cute, comfortable, and durable — and the same holds true for the Essential Supreme Bra. As someone with some sensory sensitiv"},{"text":"ity, this is the rare bra (sport or otherwise) I can wear all day without feeling hyper-aware of it. Fellow tester Liz Thomas agrees with its unobtrusiveness, saying, “I literally can’t remember exactly when I wore it because it just feels so comfortable that it doesn’t stick out.”\n\nThe bra features a seamless construction, which means I haven’t felt any specific points of irritation while wearing it. While some online reviewers mention a slight scratchiness to the wool on first wear, my experience is that it has only grown softer as I’ve worn and washed it—which is to be expected with wool garments.\n\nThe 0.75”-wide straps sit flat against the shoulder, which means that they’re comfortable underneath backpack straps.\n\nAdding to the all-day comfort for me is that the 2.5”-wide band stays (mostly) put, lying flat and offering a bit of light under-bust support without feeling as though it's cutting off my circulation, and the 0.75” straps are wide enough to provide support without digging in as the day goes on. I also saw several reviews from people with larger busts than mine mentioning how comfortable they felt in the Essential Supreme Bra, but they didn’t necessarily specify length"},{"text":"rted wearing the bra.\n\nMy only quibble is that I’ve had the strap material get caught under the hook at the end of the strap in the wash; the hooks do have some give, which means that you can slightly pry them open or clamp them shut, and I suspect the design of certain washing machines and dryers might do the same to the hardware when your bra is in the wash.\n\nBRANWYN does, however, say that although the bra is machine washable, best practice is to wash it by hand with wool-friendly soap — which I plan on doing to prevent any further machine mishaps. If you machine wash it, use a cotton laundry bag to protect the straps. Lay flat to dry.\n\nDurability\n\nAfter four months of considerable wear, my bra is nearly in the same shape as it was the first day I wore it. In fact, all of my BRANWYN bras have held their shape, including ones I’ve had for several years and have been tossing in the wash with abandon. I suspect that switching to hand-washing will only increase their longevity.\n\nFeatures\n\nThe Essential Supreme bra features ruching that creates separation and increases definition.\n\nEthically and sustainably sourced merino blend\n\nThe material is 81% extra-fine merino, which is ZQ+ cer"},{"text":"nd band (28” to 44”). Online reviewers and this tester believe the sizing to be accurate.\n\nDoes the BRANWYN Essential Supreme retain odor?\n\nNo, the 81% merino wool content provides a high level of odor resistance.\n\nIs the BRANWYN Essential Supreme good for high-impact activities like running?\n\nNo, it is designed for light-impact activities and everyday use.\n\nDoes the BRANWYN Essential Supreme work for larger cup sizes?\n\nYes, up to a G cup—XXL covers G cups and 44\" bands—but it's a low-support bra, and sizing stops at G.\n\nAre pads included?\n\nNo. The pad sleeves are built in, but BRANWYN sells the triangle pads separately ($4.50 if you purchase them with the bra).\n\nHow should I wash the Essential Supreme bra?\n\nIt's machine-washable, but BRANWYN recommends hand-washing. In the machine, I had strap fabric catch under the hooks. Lay flat to dry.\n\nSupreme vs. Busty?\n\nThe Supreme adds center ruching and pad sleeves for separation and definition ($76 vs. $62).\n\nShould you buy the BRANWYN Essential Supreme Bra?\n\nBuy if\n\nYou prefer the look and feel of encapsulation versus compression\n\nYou prefer natural materials to synthetic materials\n\nYou are looking for an all-around sports bra that work"},{"text":"customers.\n\nWhy you should trust us / About the author\n\nShawnté Salabert is an avid hiker, backpacker, trail runner, gym rat, and yoga fiend who wears a sports bra for at least an hour or two every day — if not all day. As someone who fluctuates between a 36C and 36D, she spent four months testing a medium BRANWYN Essential Supreme at home, in the gym, on the trail (on day hikes and overnights), and while traveling. She has years of experience testing sports bras for this and other outlets, and, as someone with some sensory sensitivity, she is very particular about what she likes and doesn’t like.\n\nShawnté is the author of Hiking the Pacific Crest Trail: Southern California (Amazon | Bookshop), a guidebook to the first 942.5 miles of the iconic National Scenic Trail, with another book coming from Mountaineers Books in 2027. She is also the host of the Gear Abby podcast, where she draws on her experience as an outdoor journalist and educator to answer listeners' questions about outdoor people, places, products, and pastimes.\n\nYou can read more on her website at shawntesalabert.com and discover her other Treeline Review stories on her author page.\n\nFind the best running pants for eve"},{"text":"ry season and terrain. Tested for comfort, breathability, and performance to keep you moving on road and trail runs in all kinds of weather.\n\nFind the best women's down vests for hiking, camping, and everyday wear. Compare six tested picks for warmth, fit, weather resistance, and packability.\n\nWe tested men’s down vests on hikes, backpacking trips, alpine runs, bike rides, and cold camps for warmth, comfort, fit, packability, and durability."}]}
{"item_id":"feed:https://www.treelinereview.com/gearreviews/best-walking-shoes","version_id":"e629098f36e81f676d9c57f5a0980758b87d7fb36153363d10bc430d87696bdc","kind":"feed","publisher_id":"treelinereview","title":"Best Walking Shoes for Women of 2026","url":"https://www.treelinereview.com/gearreviews/best-walking-shoes","published_at":"2026-10-07T13:05:00Z","source_notes":{"perspective":"independent","notes":"Hands-on outdoor gear testing: boots, socks, gloves, layers, sleep systems, packs. Affiliate model."},"selection_reason":"Under Relevance, Credibility, and Freshness and novelty, the updated field observations—particularly HOKA Transport 2 hikes in Colorado and Michigan and comparative gravel traction testing—provide citable footwear evidence beyond the guide's mostly casual and workplace focus. Commercial incentives requires flagging Treeline's affiliate commissions; Suitability and official requirements and Uncertainty limit use to attributed observations, since loaded multi-day rucking and Mammoth performance were not tested. It best supports Piece 03's practical discussion of traction, fit, and footwear testing before the event.","excerpted":true,"passages":[{"text":"Best Walking Shoes for Women of 2026\n\nComfortable, supportive women's shoes for travel, fitness, work, and all-day wear\n\nOctober 7th, 2026, updated with 6 months of testing notesHome → Gear Reviews → Footwear\n\nThe best walking shoes for women should stay comfortable beyond the first hour. A pair that feels fine grabbing coffee can turn into heel rubbing by hour three of a work shift, or arch fatigue halfway through a travel day spent on airport tile and concrete.\n\nOur testers wore these shoes on daily commutes, errands, travel days, and full shifts on their feet, including two useful tests: seven hours behind a bar on a Saturday night and eight hours into a nursing shift. We tracked performance over a year, from icy sidewalks in January to sun-baked pavement in July, and tested comparable models side by side to see how cushioning and support actually held up against each other rather than just against a spec sheet.\n\nWhat mattered most was how the shoes felt after hour four rather than minute five: whether the cushioning packed out, whether the fit stayed secure without new hot spots forming, and whether the sole gripped equally well on wet tile and dry sidewalk. We also paid attent"},{"text":"ion to which pairs looked at home on a walk or at a desk, since most people don't want to swap shoes between the two.\n\nTop Pick: The HOKA Bondi SR earns our pick for the best walking shoes for women overall. It held its cushioning through full-day shifts without going flat, gripped equally well on wet tile and dry pavement during testing, carries the American Podiatric Medical Association Seal of Acceptance, and looks unassuming enough to wear from a fitness walk straight into work.\n\nLooking for men’s walking shoes? See our Best Walking Shoes for Men guide for our recommendations\n\nWe write mission-driven gear reviews. To find the best gear, we combine expert reviews, everyday user opinions, and rigorous independent trail testing—never native ads or sponsored posts. We are 100% reader-supported and may make a small commission from some qualifying affiliate links. Learn more.\n\nWomen’s walking shoes comparison table\n\nThe winners\n\nBest overall women’s walking shoes: HOKA Bondi SR\n\nWeight: 23.4 oz (women’s) Drop: 6 mmArch support: NeutralCushioning: MaximumAvailable sizes: 5 - 11 (women’s)What we liked: Cushioned, stable, supportive, comfortable, slip resistant, and can be used for exer"},{"text":" restaurants, and hospitals.\n\nWe tested these shoes on fitness walks, days running errands, and during 7-hour brewery shifts. The slip-resistant tread and balance of cushion and support made them great for long periods on our feet. We also loved how they could blend well as athletic shoes or as a trendy casual look with everyday clothes. As a result, we found ourselves reaching for these shoes the most.\n\nThe downside to the HOKA Bondi SR shoes is their bulk. They’re chunky shoes, which may be a style you like, but can also feel cumbersome to others. The weight isn’t a problem for walking, but you wouldn’t want to run in these shoes.\n\nHowever, if you work on your feet all day, or simply want a walking shoe that is versatile and really comfortable, the HOKA Bondi SR is our top pick.\n\nBest women’s walking shoes for plantar fasciitis: Birkenstock Arizona Soft Footbed Sandals\n\nWeight: 16.2 oz (women’s)Drop: 0 mmArch support: Neutral (forms to your foot)Cushioning: ModerateAvailable sizes: 5 - 12.5 (women’s)What we liked: Cork footbed contours to your foot and offers excellent support and comfort; durable and lasts for many yearsWhat we didn't like: Sandals aren’t ideal for all walking c"},{"text":"l in particularly wet or cold environments (though we often pair our Arizonas with wool socks when it’s chilly). But if you’re looking for footwear that comfortably supports your feet and will last you many years, the Arizonas are hard to beat.\n\nBest women’s walking shoes with high cushion: On Cloudsurfer 2\n\nWeight: 15.9 oz (women’s)Drop: 9 mmArch support: NeutralCushioning: MaximumAvailable sizes: 5 - 11 (women’s)What we liked: Excellent cushion and comfort; great for running and other exercise What we didn't like: Not for casual wear; not as responsive as other running shoes\n\nThe On Cloudsurfer 2 road running shoes are our pick for best cushioned shoes because they have a comfortable, plush midsole. Because of their high cushion, they won Best Cushioned in our Best Women’s Road Running Shoes guide and Best for Long Distances in our Best Men’s Road Running Shoes guide.\n\nWhat makes the midsole so plush and comfortable is On’s proprietary Helion superfoam and Cloudtec midsole technology. This makes the shoes pillowy-soft but still responsive when you push off, so you feel comfortable and energized. Because of this, we also named these shoes Best for Recovery Runs in our Best Recover"},{"text":"y Shoes guide.\n\nLearn more in our in-depth review of the On Cloudsurfer 2 shoes.\n\nLike the HOKA Clifton 10s, the On Cloudsurfer 2s are great for active pursuits beyond walking. Both shoes are extremely similar, the main separating being we found we just prefer the Cloudsurfer 2s when cushion and general comfort was the main priority. In reality, both shoes serve similar purposes (and as a result, share the same drawback that these are not ideal for styling and everyday wear).\n\nBest women’s walking shoes for style: Icebug Mosi RB9X\n\nWeight: 16.2 oz (women’s)Drop: 8 mmArch support: NeutralCushioning: FullAvailable sizes: 5 - 11 (women’s)What we liked: Everyday style, comfortable midsole and fitWhat we didn't like: Not ideal for athletic wear\n\nThe Icebug Mosi RB9X shoes look like ordinary lifestyle sneakers, but they have some technical features to make them great walking shoes, too. We also appreciate the use of more sustainable materials, like BLOOM foam in the midsole.\n\nThe shoes have a microsuede caging reinforcement that is durable and a recycled mesh knit upper that is breathable. The upper is made of bluesign, solution-dyed, 100% recycled GRS certified PET polyester. The midsol"},{"text":"tab makes slipping the shoe on and off easier, and the traditional laces allow for a more precise fit.\n\nIf a sidewalk-to-trails shoe is what you’re looking for, the HOKA Transport 2 is a great option to consider. It also comes in a waterproof version if you’ll be walking and hiking frequently in wet conditions.\n\nBest women’s walking shoes for active recovery: HOKA Clifton 10\n\nWeight: 17.1 oz (women’s)Drop: 7.2 mmArch support: NeutralCushioning: MaximumAvailable sizes: 4 - 12 (women’s)What we liked: Excellent cushion; comfortable; responsive enough for running and other exerciseWhat we didn't like: Not as versatile for casual wear\n\nThe HOKA Clifton 10s are a staple running and walking shoe because of their balance of cushion, responsiveness, and support. We recommend them as cushioned daily training running shoes and as active recovery shoes, which includes walking.\n\nThe Clifton 10s are highly cushioned and have an “early-stage Meta-Rocker,'' which means the heel-to-forefoot motion of the foot has a smooth ride. This is an ideal shoe for those who walk for exercise and want a shoe that can handle running or other athletic endeavors as well.\n\nLearn more in our in-depth review of the "},{"text":"HOKA Clifton 10 shoes.\n\nWe’ve tested the Clifton 10s extensively, alongside other walking, running, and recovery shoes, and alongside previous models of the Cliftons. This is a well-rounded athletic shoe. However, that also means it’s not the best for casual or work wear (style-wise), if that’s a priority for you.\n\nOther walking shoes we tested\n\nHOKA Bondi 9\n\nWeight: 18 oz (women’s)Drop: 5 mmArch support: NeutralCushioning: MaximumAvailable sizes: 4 - 12 (women’s)\n\nThe HOKA Bondi 9s are the road running shoe version of our best overall pick, the HOKA Bondi SR. These shoes are highly cushioned and have Meta-Rocker technology in the midsole to encourage slight forward motion and a smooth ride. Like the HOKA Clifton 10s and On Cloudsurfer 2s, we love these shoes for active recovery and daily road running. Ultimately, the HOKA Clifton and On Cloudsurfer won out in these guide because we like them more for slower paces and a focus on cushion, but the HOKA Bondi 9s work very well as a walking-and-running shoe, too.\n\nLearn more in our in-depth review of the HOKA Bondi 9 road running shoes.\n\nThe HOKA Bondi 9 walking shoes are most at home on roads, sidewalks, and paved paths.\n\nWe tested th"},{"text":"r pair of shoes to buy (i.e., another reason to spend money), consider them an investment in your health. For example, the author and main tester for this guide spent four years as a high school teacher and cross country/track coach, which meant at least 10 hours a day on her feet. On top of that, she was usually training for marathons, which meant even more time on her feet. Since she often wore unsupportive flats while teaching, eventually she developed a gnarly case of plantar fasciitis, which took about 9 months to heal and a lot of expensive doctor appointments. A quality pair of walking shoes would have been cheaper (and much less painful and frustrating).\n\nWhat to look for in women’s walking shoes\n\nArch support\n\nWalking shoes should be supportive to prevent foot fatigue or pain, so proper arch support should be a priority when selecting a walking shoe. However, not everyone’s arches are the same, so it’s a good idea to figure out if you have high arches, average arches, or flat feet. You can do this at home by wetting your foot and stepping on paper. If you see about half your arch region, you have average arches. If the arches are more filled in, you may have low arches or "},{"text":"fasciitis. The shoes did not get rid of plantar fasciitis, but they offered the support and structure needed while she followed her doctor’s advice.\n\nWhat's the best way to break in new walking shoes?\n\nThe best way to break in new walking shoes is to start off wearing them on short walks until you feel comfortable wearing them for longer periods. Most walking shoes are comfortable out of the box and do not require much, if any, break-in time. However, stiffer materials, or shoes with a different fit or drop than you’re used to, should be eased into. This also gives you a chance to assess the fit and see if any part of the shoe rubs uncomfortably, causing hot spots or blisters.\n\nHow we researched and tested\n\nWe tested walking shoes on a variety of surfaces, including dirt/gravel roads in the Upper Peninsula of Michigan.\n\nWe tested these walking shoes walking hundreds of miles on sidewalk/pavement, dirt and gravel trails, and while running errands, commuting, and traveling. Our testers wore these shoes in all seasons and weather conditions, from hot and dry summer days to cold and icy winter mornings, and everything in between.\n\nMost testing surfaces were on paved paths or in indoor "},{"text":"eeline Review author page.\n\nWe tested women’s rain boots for waterproofing, traction, comfort, fit, and durability through downpours, muddy trails, farm work, and everyday walking.\n\nFind the perfect women’s walking shoes—comfortable, supportive, and durable options for daily walks, travel, fitness, and all-day wear.\n\nFind the perfect walking shoes—comfortable, supportive, and durable options for daily walks, fitness, and all-day wear."}]}
{"item_id":"feed:https://www.treelinereview.com/gearreviews/lems-zenspeed-plus-road-running-shoes-review","version_id":"15a35cd64abe2d51730f5051a2208c0645f58664091d4c9e6b7775be16b6c63f","kind":"feed","publisher_id":"treelinereview","title":"Lems ZENSPEED+ Review: A Foot-Shaped Road Running Shoe That Doesn't Stop at the Pavement","url":"https://www.treelinereview.com/gearreviews/lems-zenspeed-plus-road-running-shoes-review","published_at":"2026-10-07T07:01:00Z","source_notes":{"perspective":"independent","notes":"Hands-on outdoor gear testing: boots, socks, gloves, layers, sleep systems, packs. Affiliate model."},"selection_reason":"Under Relevance, Credibility, and Freshness and novelty, this review adds three months and over 100 miles of footwear testing, including 15-mile runs, heat, mixed surfaces, and observed wear, offering citable evidence about endurance footwear tradeoffs. Under Commercial incentives, Treeline earns affiliate commissions and the author mentions receiving a test pair without specifying its terms; under Suitability and official requirements and Uncertainty, attribute comfort and traction findings to this reviewer, distinguish observed wear from predicted longevity, and acknowledge that loaded rucking, sustained cold or rain, and Mammoth use were not tested, leaving required status unspecified. It supports Piece 01’s newly reviewed gear, Piece 02’s performance scrutiny, and Piece 03’s practical fit, terrain, and transition considerations.","excerpted":true,"passages":[{"text":"Lems ZENSPEED+ Review: A Foot-Shaped Road Running Shoe That Doesn't Stop at the Pavement\n\nWe ran over 100 miles in this 5 mm drop, wide-toe-box shoe on roads, gravel, and dirt across Colorado and Utah.\n\nOctober 7, 2026Home → Gear Reviews → Running\n\nOur verdict\n\nThe Lems ZENSPEED+ is a comfortable and lightweight road running shoe that applies Lems' natural foot-shape principles to running.\n\nLike other Lems shoes, they put your foot in a more natural position while you run. They come in two widths, have a wide toe box, and a 5 mm heel-to-toe drop. The midsole feels responsive, yet grounded, with a full-coverage rubber outsole that grips better than most road running shoes.\n\nI tested the ZENSPEED+ while running on pavement, gravel, and dirt roads in Colorado and Utah during the summer and early fall. I’ve put over 100 miles on them so far, in conditions ranging from hot, dry summer afternoons in Denver to cooler 50-degree fall mornings in Moab and Escalante, Utah. I’ve worn them on purely pavement runs and longer runs that start on pavement but lead to dirt roads and smooth singletrack trails. These aren’t a trail shoe or even a “gravel” shoe, but they work well on a variety of surfa"},{"text":"other benefits. However, that might not be how you feel about running shoes. Fortunately, we have guides to women’s running shoes and road running shoes for men if you’re looking for a different style.\n\nIf you're looking for an all-around running shoe that bucks many modern, maximalist running-shoe trends, I think the Lems ZENSPEED+ is a great option.\n\nWe create reader-supported, objective gear reviews independently selected by our editors. This story may contain affiliate links, which help fund our website. When you click on the links to purchase gear, we may get a commission — without costing you an extra cent. Thank you for supporting our work and mission of outdoor coverage for every body! Learn more.\n\nLems ZENSPEED+ Road Running Shoes specifications\n\nCushion: Moderate\n\nStability: Neutral\n\nHeel-to-Toe Drop (mm): 5 mm\n\nStack Height: 31-26 mm\n\nLugs: 0 mm\n\nUpper: Engineered Mesh\n\nMidsole: EVA & 10% PEBA ZENCELL Midsole\n\nOutsole: 3.5 mm super-light floating rubber\n\nWeight (pair): 1 lb, 2.8 oz (men’s); 14.8 oz (women's)\n\nComparison table\n\nBest for\n\nEveryday training runs\n\nLong-distance running\n\nRunners who prefer a mid or lower heel-to-toe drop\n\nMixed pavement and gravel runs\n\nWalki"},{"text":"ng\n\nWorking out\n\nWhat we liked\n\nLightweight\n\nComfortable\n\nWide toe box and natural foot shape\n\nCork insole prevents your foot from sliding\n\nSpringy midsole that still feels natural\n\nWhat could be better\n\nNot the best for rough dirt roads\n\nSome might not love the look\n\nNeeds transition time if you are accustomed to highly cushioned running shoes\n\nHow we tested\n\nI tested the Lems ZENSPEED+ while running on paved roads, dirt roads, gravel roads, and smooth singletrack trails in Colorado and Utah during the summer and early fall. I also wore them while walking around, working out, and during daily life.\n\nI tested the Lems ZENSPEED+ while running in Denver, Colorado, in the summertime. There, I wore them in temperatures ranging from 50 to 100 degrees, on paved roads, sidewalks, and bike paths, and on dirt trails through city parks. I also wore them on dirt and paved roads in Colorado’s Arkansas Valley, near Leadville, Buena Vista, and Salida. Those runs were mostly on dirt roads, with paved highways when needed. I also used them in southern Utah, near Escalante and Moab, on paved roads and dirt roads.\n\nOver three months of testing, I ran over 100 miles in these shoes. Most of my running"},{"text":" was in dry conditions, but I occasionally ran on wet pavement and dirt surfaces. I also tested the ZENSPEED+ alongside several other road running shoes and gravel running shoes.\n\nPerformance in the field\n\nFit and Comfort\n\nThe ZENSPEED+ running shoes are comfortable right out of the box. When I received my test pair, I put them on to check the fit, then immediately went on an 8-mile run with no issues.\n\nI tested them in size 10 wide, and they don’t feel too loose or sloppy through the midfoot. I typically wear a men’s size 9.5, but I’ve worn other Lems shoes in size 10.\n\nIf you're trying Lems shoes for the first time, pay attention to the width options. The ZENSPEED+ comes in D width, similar to the original Lems wide fit, and a narrower B width, which Lems calls medium. I find the wide size feels similar to the Altra Torin or Altra Timp—wider through the toe box but narrower through the midfoot and heel than the Altra Lone Peak. I expect the B width version fits similarly to the women’s version of these shoes, too.\n\nThe heel cup locks the back of my foot in place. And the roomy toe box prevents my toes from rubbing against each other during long, hot runs. The longest run I’ve com"},{"text":" and heel cup that keeps your heel locked in place. They have a second set of eyelets for the heel-lock lacing technique, but I find these shoes hold my heel in place without any special lacing patterns.\n\nMidsole and Cushioning\n\nThe ZENSPEED+ has 31 mm of stack in the heel and 26 mm in the forefoot, with a 5 mm drop. You might think of Lems as a zero-drop shoe brand, but they intentionally designed these shoes with a 5 mm drop so they’re more approachable for average runners.\n\nThe midsole uses 90% EVA (Ethylene-Vinyl Acetate) and 10% PEBA (Polyether Block Amide). EVA is the foam most commonly used in running shoe midsoles, and PEBA, common in “super shoes,” adds extra bounce.\n\nThe cork insole provides a subtle amount of extra cushioning as well. Much like a Birkenstock sandal, which has a cork midsole, the cork insole in the ZENSPEED+ will conform to your foot over time, making it more comfortable as they break in.\n\nThis foam combination feels responsive yet grounded. It's not a soft, bouncy midsole, and it doesn't have the most cushion. But it’s very comfortable and has a lot more bounce than a minimalist shoe.\n\nCompared to other trail and road running shoes, the ZENSPEED+ has ave"},{"text":", and the shoes feel solid when changing direction quickly. I’ve mostly used them in dry, summertime conditions in Colorado and Utah, but I’ve never slipped on patches of wet pavement, either.\n\nVersatility\n\nThe ZENSPEED+ is a road shoe that can do so much more than just roads. They're definitely more road-oriented than an off-road shoe, but they work well for running out your front door and onto far-out dirt roads. They’re also a great option for everyday wear, walking, travel, light hiking, and gym use.\n\nResponsiveness\n\nThe midsole feels responsive without feeling mushy. The 10% PEBA adds extra bounce without making it feel unnatural. The ZENSPEED+ isn't built to be the fastest shoe on the road, but it’s lightweight, and the midsole feels firm with a subtle energy return as you push off.\n\nWarmth\n\nThe ZENSPEED+ has a breathable mesh upper that won’t keep your feet warm in cold weather. If you plan to use these in cold weather, consider thicker socks or check out some other tips for winter running.\n\nBreak-in Time\n\nI pulled these shoes out of the box, put them on to check the fit, and then immediately went on an 8-mile run over pavement and dirt. They’re very comfortable right out of"},{"text":" the box, and I had no issues on that first run or on any subsequent runs or walks. However, if you aren’t accustomed to running shoes with a lower stack height and less cushion, you may want to ease into them gradually by starting with shorter runs and alternating with your old pair of running shoes.\n\nUpper and Breathability\n\nThe ZENSPEED+ shoes are very breathable. I’ve worn them in temperatures up to 100 degrees, and they’ve never caused my feet to overheat. I’ve also had no issues with hotspots or blisters, even when running in very hot conditions.\n\nDurability and Longevity\n\nSo far, I've run over 100 miles on roads of all sorts in these shoes. The upper shows no signs of tearing or abrasion, the midsole still feels like new, and the outsole shows no premature wear.\n\nAfter 100+ miles, the midsole still feels as good as new. I would expect this midsole to last at least as long as other running shoes with an EVA midsole. For reference, most running shoe midsoles last about 300 to 500 miles, depending on the terrain and other factors.\n\nAlso, the ZENSPEED+ has no exposed midsole foam on the tread, which prevents rocks or other sharp objects underfoot from tearing the midsole and sho"},{"text":"rtening the shoes' longevity.\n\nThe engineered mesh upper feels durable enough for road running conditions. If you start using these on rocky trails, they might not last as long, though. For what it’s worth, I’ve worn my pair on a fair amount of rocky singletrack trails, and they’re still going strong.\n\nThey have TPU reinforcement around the toe box, and these overlays wrap along the base of the upper and around the heel as well. This helps protect the mesh upper in higher wear areas. So far, the uppers on my test pair of the ZENSPEED+ have no tears or abrasions.\n\nThe full-coverage rubber outsole is firm, so it won’t wear out as quickly as some other rubber types. Also, because there is more rubber on the outsole, it distributes wear over a larger area, increasing longevity.\n\nPrice and Value\n\nAt $175 per pair, the ZENSPEED+ is priced about at par with other running shoes. But if you’re looking for a mid-drop road-running shoe that’s versatile across different terrain types, I think this is a good value.\n\nSustainability/Ethical company\n\nLems partners with several organizations that strive to create a better planet. They have several fully vegan shoes and are working to incorporate re"},{"text":"he midsole is made with 90% EVA (ethylene-vinyl acetate) and 10% PEBA (polyether block amide). EVA is the same midsole foam material commonly used in running shoes. PEBA foam is commonly used in \"super shoes\" and provides extra bounce.\n\nFull-Coverage Injected Rubber Outsole\n\nThe outsole uses a full-coverage 3.5 mm strip of injected rubber.\n\nEngineered Mesh Upper\n\nThe ZENSPEED+ upper uses engineered mesh for comfort and breathability.\n\nCork Insole\n\nIt has a minimal cork insole that's comfortable and doesn’t get slippery when it’s wet from foot sweat.\n\nPadded Heel Collar\n\nThe ZENSPEED+ features a structured, padded heel collar to lock your heel in place.\n\nReflective Material\n\nThe Lems logo is reflective, and the engineered mesh upper reflects light, too. If you’ll be running in the dark, check out our Best Reflective Gear for Running in the Dark guide for additional safety gear.\n\nWho should buy the Lems ZENSPEED+?\n\nBuy if\n\nBuy the Lems ZENSPEED+ if you want a comfortable all-around running shoe with a wide toe box and mid-drop that can handle mixed surfaces, especially runs that start on pavement and continue onto gravel or dirt roads.\n\nSkip if\n\nSkip the Lems ZENSPEED+ if you want th"},{"text":"uke Trail, and the Colorado Trail twice, always in trail running shoes.\n\nYou can read more about Sam on his author page, website, or Instagram.\n\nFind the best running pants for every season and terrain. Tested for comfort, breathability, and performance to keep you moving on road and trail runs in all kinds of weather.\n\nWe ran 100+ miles in the Saucony Ride 19. It's a balanced, no-fuss neutral daily trainer for beginner to elite runners, and still a great value at $145.\n\nIs the Lems ZENSPEED+ right for you? We tested this moderately cushioned, wide-toe-box road shoe on roads and gravel and compared it to Altra and HOKA."}]}
{"item_id":"feed:https://www.treelinereview.com/gearreviews/best-walking-shoes-men","version_id":"269805f4b18e128a306ef239975a5de1cdff96e6551ee3fa8a07d48fc2b86e9a","kind":"feed","publisher_id":"treelinereview","title":"Best Walking Shoes for Men of 2026","url":"https://www.treelinereview.com/gearreviews/best-walking-shoes-men","published_at":"2026-10-06T15:53:00Z","source_notes":{"perspective":"independent","notes":"Hands-on outdoor gear testing: boots, socks, gloves, layers, sleep systems, packs. Affiliate model."},"selection_reason":"Under Relevance, Credibility, and Making the decision, the Transport 2 testing on Colorado and Northeast hikes and Michigan dirt roads offers citable traction and wide-foot fit observations relevant to Mammoth footwear, despite the guide's predominantly casual and workplace focus. Commercial incentives requires noting Treeline's affiliate commissions and discounting its praise as independent confirmation; under Uncertainty and Suitability and official requirements, these single-publisher observations support Piece 03's footwear testing considerations, but do not establish loaded multi-day performance or required status.","excerpted":true,"passages":[{"text":"Best Walking Shoes for Men of 2026\n\nComfortable, supportive men's shoes for travel, fitness, work, and all-day wear\n\nOctober 6th, 2026Home → Gear Reviews → Footwear\n\nAnyone who has ever walked a mile in ill-fitting, unsupportive, or generally uncomfortable shoes knows how important a quality pair of walking shoes is, especially if you’ll be on your feet all day. A pair that feels fine taking out the trash can turn into heel rubbing a couple of hours into a work shift or arch fatigue while hurrying to catch your layover flight.\n\nWe tested the top walking shoes and tracked performance for over a year, from icy sidewalks in January to scorching pavement in July, to help you prevent those aforementioned discomforts. We wore them on daily commutes, errands, travel days, and up to 12-hour hospital shifts on our feet. We tested comparable models side by side to see how cushioning and support actually held up against each other, rather than just against a spec sheet.\n\nWe paid close attention to whether the cushioning packed out, whether the fit stayed secure, whether new hot spots formed, and how well the sole gripped on wet, slick tile and trails. We also factored in style and how easily "},{"text":"he downside to the HOKA Bondi SR shoes is their bulk. They’re chunky shoes, which may be a style you like, but can also feel cumbersome to others (and can take up a lot of space in an adventure travel pack). Some of our testers really did not like this style of shoe, and if that’s you, you may want to look at our other favorite options. And while the weight isn’t a problem for walking, you wouldn’t want to run in these shoes. So if you want a more versatile shoe for running, you’ll want to consider our favorite running shoes in this guide.\n\nHowever, if you work on your feet all day, need slip resistance for workplace safety, or simply want a walking shoe that is cushioned, grippy, and really comfortable, the HOKA Bondi SR is our top pick.\n\nBest men’s walking shoes for active recovery: HOKA Clifton 11\n\nWeight (per pair): 19.94 ozDrop: 8 mmCushioning: BalancedAvailable sizes: 7 - 16What we liked: Excellent cushion; comfortable; responsive enough for running and other exerciseWhat we didn't like: Not as stylish for casual/crossover wear\n\nThe HOKA Clifton 11s are a staple running and walking shoe because of their incredible balance of cushion, responsiveness, and support.\n\nThe Clifton "},{"text":"s is the best waterproofing you’re going to find in athletic footwear, so despite our limited testing in them, we have a lot of faith in this pair if you’ll be walking or commuting in wet conditions.\n\nBest men’s walking shoes with high cushion: HOKA Bondi 9\n\nWeight (per pair): 21.0 ozDrop: 5 mmCushioning: MaximumAvailable sizes: 7 - 16What we liked: Excellent cushion and comfort; great for running and other exerciseWhat we didn't like: Not for casual wear; not as responsive as other running-focused walking shoes\n\nThe HOKA Bondi 9s are the non-slip-resistant, road running shoe-version of our best overall pick, the HOKA Bondi SR. These shoes are heavily cushioned and have HOKA’s MetaRocker technology in the midsole to encourage forward motion and a smooth ride.\n\nThe HOKA Bondi 9s differ from the HOKA Bondi SRs primarily in that they don’t have the slip-resistant grip on the outsole and they weigh less/are less clunky for running and faster walking. The Bondi 9s cost $5 less than the Bondi SRs.\n\nThe HOKA Bondi 9s are featured in the Best Recovery Shoes guide.\n\nLearn more in our in-depth review of the HOKA Bondi 9 road running shoes.\n\nLike the HOKA Clifton 11s, we also love these shoes"},{"text":" for active recovery and daily road running. The main difference between the Clifton 11s and Bondi 9s is that the Bondis have more plush cushioning and are less responsive than the Cliftons. The Cliftons are better suited for a wider range of running paces, while the Bondis are more comfort-driven and better reduce impact on the joints.\n\nWe tested the men’s and women’s HOKA Bondi 9s as both a running shoe and a walking shoe. They perform well in both roles, and are a great option for those looking for lots of impact absorption to reduce aches in joints.\n\nBest men’s walking shoes for trails: HOKA Transport 2\n\nWeight (per pair): 22.6 ozDrop: 5 mmCushioning: BalancedAvailable sizes: 7 - 15What we liked: Grippy Vibram outsole, balance of cushion and support, comfortableWhat we didn't like: Not available in extra-wide sizing, not as versatile as other shoes in terms of style and occasion\n\nIf you’re looking for a walking shoe that crosses over to hiking, the HOKA Transport 2 is our top choice. Like all HOKA shoes we’ve tested, these shoes balance cushion and support for all-day comfort regardless of what surfaces you’re standing or walking on, while the Vibram outsole handles more techni"},{"text":"cal terrain better than other walking shoes. Think of this as a hybrid shoe that combines the lightweight technicality of a trail runner or hiking shoe with the more casual style of a walking shoe.\n\nLearn more in our in-depth review of the HOKA Transport 2 shoes.\n\nWe tested the HOKA Transport 2 shoes on city walks on paved/gravel paths, gym days, and hikes in the Front Range of Colorado, throughout the Northeast, and on dirt roads in the Upper Peninsula of Michigan. The traction on these shoes handles most trails really well, and we found we really enjoyed wearing them for hikes that weren’t overly technical. While they weren’t our favorite walking shoes for paved paths (the grippy outsole felt unnecessary in those cases), they weren’t distractingly grippy.\n\nThe laces are adjusted with a pull tab, which makes slipping the shoes on and off easier. The shoes also come with traditional laces you can swap in if you prefer, which is better for people who want a more precise fit.\n\nOne of our testers has extremely wide feet and found the Transport 2 to be too narrow, even when other HOKA wides will work for that tester. Unfortunately these do not come in extra-wide sizing, which is a big "},{"text":"e a worthy choice for travelers, commuters, casual walkers, folks with mobility limitations/arthritis, or anyone who likes the style of On shoes.\n\nOn’s proprietary CloudTec midsole provides plenty of cushion for all-day comfort, while their proprietary Speedboard technology encourages a little pep in the step, which can help you feel more energized. Equipped with a speed lacing system, this shoe can be slipped on and off with ease, while the snug heel cup and midfoot feel secure.\n\nLearn more in our in-depth review of the On Cloud 6 shoes.\n\nThe Cloud 6 shoes are better suited for aiding in mobility and more durable than the similar On shoes. The On Cloud 6 shoes fit narrow, but fortunately these shoes come in wide sizing.\n\nIf you’re looking for a lifestyle walking shoe that you can slip on without having to adjust, and you like the style of On shoes, the On Cloud 6 is a worthwhile option.\n\nBest men’s walking shoes for plantar fasciitis: Birkenstock Arizona Soft Footbed Sandals\n\nWeight (per pair): 21.2 ozDrop: 0 mmCushioning: ModerateAvailable sizes: 6 - 13.5What we liked: Cork footbed contours to your foot and offers excellent support and comfort; durable and lasts for many yearsWha"},{"text":"t we didn't like: Sandals aren’t ideal for all walking conditions\n\nThe Birkenstock Arizona Soft Footbed sandals are our pick for best walking shoes for plantar fasciitis because of the foot-contouring cork footbed that molds to support your feet. Many Treeline testers have sworn by these shoes for decades, including the author of this guide, who wore them almost exclusively for nine months while healing plantar fasciitis.\n\nLearn more in our in-depth review of the Birkenstock Arizona Soft Footbed sandals.\n\nIn addition to the cork midsole that molds to your feet (mimicking expensive arch supports!), another standout feature is that the sandals are hardy and can last you many years, making them a great value. The suede upper is soft, flexible, and durable, and the buckles allow you to adjust the shoe to your foot volume. The outsole isn’t the most grippy, but the grip is enough for most walking surfaces (including non-technical trails).\n\nThe author of this guide was a high school English teacher and a cross country/track coach who also trained for marathons, so she spent at least 10 hours a day on her feet, often in unsupportive shoes. As a result, she developed gnarly plantar fasciit"},{"text":"is in both her feet. After doing lots of research, she started wearing the Birkenstock Arizonas for better support while her plantar fascia healed.\n\nTo be clear, the Birkenstock Arizonas do not cure plantar fasciitis, and you should see a medical professional if you have foot pain. This is simply the best shoe we’ve tested to help support our feet while our plantar fascia heals.\n\nNot everyone enjoys walking in sandals, though, and these may not be your best option for particularly wet or cold environments (though we often pair our Arizonas with wool socks when it’s chilly). But if your priorities include bespoke arch support and footwear that will last you many years, the Arizonas are a tried-and-true favorite.\n\nBest men’s zero-drop walking shoes: Lems Mesa Verde\n\nWeight (per pair): 20.1 ozDrop: 0 mmCushioning: ModerateAvailable sizes: 4.5 - 15What we liked: Offers good style for a barefoot shoe, has a wide toe box, and has some cushion for those transitioning to barefoot shoesWhat we didn't like: Zero-drop isn’t for everyone, and some barefoot shoe veterans may want something even more minimalist than this\n\nThe Lems Mesa Verde is a zero-drop, minimalist walking shoe that still off"},{"text":"n for those who want a minimalist shoe for walking. These shoes are our best everyday barefoot shoes pick for our Best Barefoot Shoes guide.\n\nLearn more in our in-depth review of the Xero Shoes Prio Neo.\n\nThey do not provide a lot of cushion like most of the walking shoes we tested, but they are lightweight, breathable, and flexible for more natural movement. They have a wide toe box for your feet and toes to splay out naturally. The upper is thin and flexible, and snug when on. While testing, we occasionally wore these as gym shoes if we were walk-commuting to the gym.\n\nThese shoes are minimalist, and designed for a “barely there” feel, while also still feeling comfortable enough for all-day walking or travel. What you may lose in cushioning or foam you gain in groundfeel, gradual foot strength, and balance. The flex on these shoes is amazing, and our testers (who are accustomed to zero-drop, minimalist footwear) felt comfortable in them for over 55 miles of walking during testing.\n\nThe Prio Neo is Xero’s most stable, all-around “athleisure” shoe. Xero also offers the Prio Suede, as a separate model. They also have a more budget-friendly price than the other shoes we tested, so if"},{"text":"sion for running into coaching, crewing, writing, creating training plans for her friends, hiking, snowshoeing, and running as much as her hip allows. She earned her MFA in Creative Writing from the Mile-High MFA Program at Regis University. Becca is based in Denver, Colorado.\n\nYou can find her at her website, Instagram, and her Treeline Review author page.\n\nWe tested women’s rain boots for waterproofing, traction, comfort, fit, and durability through downpours, muddy trails, farm work, and everyday walking.\n\nFind the perfect women’s walking shoes—comfortable, supportive, and durable options for daily walks, travel, fitness, and all-day wear.\n\nFind the perfect walking shoes—comfortable, supportive, and durable options for daily walks, fitness, and all-day wear."}]}
{"item_id":"feed:https://www.treelinereview.com/gearreviews/best-womens-winter-boots","version_id":"fe3351b24a28559fae53c196e3fc07fd88906b30031612f8aedfbe2d4c208fdb","kind":"feed","publisher_id":"treelinereview","title":"Best Winter Boots for Women of 2026","url":"https://www.treelinereview.com/gearreviews/best-womens-winter-boots","published_at":"2026-10-05T17:41:00Z","source_notes":{"perspective":"independent","notes":"Hands-on outdoor gear testing: boots, socks, gloves, layers, sleep systems, packs. Affiliate model."},"selection_reason":"Under Relevance, Credibility, and Freshness and novelty, this updated review supplies transferable field evidence, including VECTIV testing on a three-night Maine winter trip in 15–45°F conditions and Oboz traction observations on wet rocks, alongside overheating, stiffness, and saturation limitations. Commercial incentives requires discounting affiliate-funded praise despite the author's denial of brand sponsorship; Uncertainty and Conflicting evidence require attributing observations to this single publisher and preserving the tension between the VECTIV saturation caveat and its broad performance praise. It supports Pieces 01, 02, and 03 through updated findings, comparisons with claimed waterproofing and insulation, and practical footwear tradeoffs, while Mammoth-specific loaded-ruck testing remains absent and required status stays unspecified under Suitability and official requirements.","excerpted":true,"passages":[{"text":"Best Winter Boots for Women of 2026\n\nThe Top Women’s Winter Boots for Warmth, Waterproofness, Fit, Comfort, and Ice Traction\n\nOctober 5th, 2026 with a new winner for best winter hiking boot, best insulated winter boot, best pull-on, best for walking, and most comfortable winter bootHome > Gear Reviews > Footwear\n\nWinter boots have a big job. They need to keep your feet warm and dry through snow, slush, ice, and cold without being so bulky or uncomfortable that you don’t want to wear them. I’ve spent winters in Alaska, Maine, New Hampshire, New York, and Colorado, and I’ve learned that the right winter boot depends as much on what you’re doing as how cold it is outside.\n\nWe’ve tested women’s winter boots in icy conditions, wet snow, deep snow, and extreme cold, using them for everything from hiking and snowshoeing to shoveling, walking the dog, running errands, and working outside. Some of these boots have stayed in our rotation for years, which has also given us a good sense of how they hold up over time.\n\nWe looked most closely at warmth, tread, waterproofness, fit, and shaft height. We also considered ankle and arch support, how well each boot worked with different socks, durabil"},{"text":"ity, and whether the boot made sense for its intended use.\n\nTop Pick: The SOREL Joan of Arctic Boots are our favorite overall. We’ve worn them through winters from Maine to Alaska and keep coming back to their combination of warmth, waterproofness, traction, durability, and style. They’re sturdy enough for snow and outdoor chores but still easy to wear around town, and the replaceable liner helps extend the life of the boot.\n\nLooking for men’s boots? See our Best Men’s Winter Boots guide.\n\nLooking for kids’ boots? See our Best Kids’ Snow Boots guide.\n\nWe create reader-supported, objective gear reviews independently selected by our editors. This story may contain affiliate links, which help fund our website. When you click on the links to purchase gear, we may get a commission — without costing you an extra cent. Thank you for supporting our work and mission of outdoor coverage for every body! Learn more.\n\nWomen’s Winter Boots Comparison Table\n\n* Prices reflect MSRP and are subject to change. We note any discounts we see on our Deals page, which is updated daily.\n\nThe Best Women’s Winter Boots\n\nThe Best overall Women’s Winter Boots: SOREL Joan of Arctic Boots\n\nInsulation: 9 mm washa"},{"text":"on the SOREL Caribou boots and deep lugs mean that you have traction even in icier conditions.\n\nThe felt liners in the SOREL Caribou are removable so you can clean or replace them if needed, as we’ve done with this men’s pair.\n\nThe pull tab in the back of the SOREL Caribou is sturdy and makes it easier to take these boots on and off.\n\nMy father has owned his pair of SOREL Caribous since 1992. I’ve never seen a winter without him wearing those boots—from New York to Colorado to Maine! In fact, many reviewers brought up family members having owned these boots for 15+ years, with the boots still going strong.\n\nThe women’s version of the Caribou is just as warm and durable. These boots are rated by SOREL down to -40°F. The author can tell you they’re quite comfy down to at least -20°F. Three different Treeline Review testers can verify, having used the SOREL Caribou from Maine to Colorado to Washington.\n\nThe tread on the Caribous offered some of the best grip on packed snow, while the felt cuff and mid-calf shaft kept the snow, slush, and puddles far away from reviewers’ feet. For these reasons, the Caribou boots won our award for best women’s winter boots for extreme cold.\n\nBest women"},{"text":"'s winter boots for walking: L.L.Bean Women’s 8”Bean Boots\n\nInsulation: Available in: PrimaLoft, shearling liningShaft height: 8”Temperature rating: -30FWaterproof: YesFootwear closure: LaceBest for: Around town, styleWhat we liked: style, warmth, durability, made in US, variety of heights, colors, insulation, and sizes availableWhat we didn’t like: can be hard to get due to high demand, price\n\nIt’s a given; we couldn’t ignore the classic L.L.Bean Duck Boot. With a history dating back to 1912, these made in the USA boots can be backordered by the 100,000.\n\nThe Bean Boot has a classic look that works in urban settings or on a trail. It's comfortable enough for walking commutes and wearing all-day in the office.\n\nIt’s a look that has been mimicked by boot makers—from J.Crew on the fancy end to XTRATUF on the outdoorsy side. It’s not only comfortable and functional, but it has an aesthetic that works for a lot of settings—and we think that is a factor that makes it a winning boot.\n\nThere are a lot of contenders for the best winter boots for walking. But ultimately, we think the L.L. Bean Boot is a better value than most and is still made in the USA.\n\nFor more information, see our in-d"},{"text":"hilly winter hiker’s dream—and one we found useful for shoveling snow, walking the dog, and winter activities where good traction is essential.\n\nOboz is primarily a hiking boot and hiking shoe company, and you’ll see them in our Best Hiking Boots and Best Hiking Shoes guide. To make their winter hiking boot, they took the design and technology and added extra height and insulation.\n\nWe chose the Oboz Bridger 9” as the best non slip winter boots due to the excellent traction and grip that the Oboz Bridgers have on slippery, icy, and snowy surfaces. The Oboz Bridger is also our winner in our Best Winter Hiking Boots guide.\n\nFor more information, see our long-term review of the Oboz Bridger 9\" Winter Boots.\n\nThe Oboz Bridger winter hiking boots on an early winter day in Maine.\n\nWe were impressed by the traction on the Oboz Bridger on slippery, wet rocks and other obstacles we encountered while hiking.\n\nClose-up of the winterized rubber outsole on the Oboz Bridger 9-inch Insulated boots.\n\nThe Oboz Bridger insulated boots also work well in deep mud (and clean off easily, too).\n\nClose-up on the protective toe cap on the Oboz Bridger Insulated boots.\n\nThe author tested the Oboz Bridger in"},{"text":" deeper snow in Maine and New Hampshire and appreciated the high lacing to really get the fit dialed in, especially with thicker socks.\n\nIn testing, we found the Oboz Bridger is also incredibly warm — with 400 grams of Freeze Guard insulation. With 3 mm thickness, that Freeze Guard insulation is more than enough insulation to keep even the coldest toes cozy. It’s also ringed by wool on the top. You may even find these boots too warm!\n\nOne potential ding against the Oboz Bridger is that it’s a stiffer boot than most hiking boots. (With the insulation, waterproof rubber, and waterproof nubuck leather, it’s not a crazy claim.) However, if ankle support is an important aspect of winter boots for you, the stiffness of the Oboz Bridger is actually a plus. They also provide great ankle support by lacing below and above the ankle — another perk of their height.\n\nWhile this wasn't our testers' experience, customer reviewers also warned that the Oboz Bridgers tend to run small, so sizing up a half size isn’t a bad idea, especially if you are wearing thick winter socks.\n\nBest Winter Hiking Boots: The North Face VECTIV Fastpack Insulated Mid Waterproof Hiking Shoe\n\nInsulation: 200 g Heatseeker"},{"text":" Synthetic InsulationShaft height: Mid (4-6”, depending on size)Temperature rating: Not stated, 10-20F in testingWaterproof: YesFootwear closure: LaceBest for: Hiking, winter backpacking, snowshoeingWhat we liked: Comfortable, lightweight, good groundfeel, thoughtful hiking-specific featuresWhat we didn’t like: When fully saturated, the midfoot and toe box protection was somewhat compromised, can be hard to find the women's version\n\nIf you want a lightweight hiking boot with insulation and waterproofing, the best winter hiking boots are The North Face VECTIV Fastpack Insulated Mid. These boots are hands-down the favorite of hikers who prefer wearing mesh lightweight trail running shoes in the summer but find they need insulation and waterproofing in the winter.\n\nIn our testing, they keep you warm down to 10-20F, just as long as you are moving. They are designed for movement in snow and ice. These boots have some unique features, like a rocker midsole to smooth out the hiking gait, a TPU plate to enhance propulsion and protect the forefoot from rocks, a knob on the heel to keep snowshoe straps from sliding down, and pull tabs on both the tongue and heel to make it easy to pull the b"},{"text":"ob designed to keep a snowshoe strap from slipping down while hiking.\n\nA close-up of The North Face VECTIV Fastpack Insulated Mid in a snowshoe shows how the heel design helps hold the rear strap securely in place.\n\nThe boot’s soft collar includes a fleece fabric that creates a comfortable closure around the lower leg and helps make long miles in these boots very comfortable. The North Face uses only Leather Working Group tanneries, sourcing their leather from approved tanners that reduce water and energy use. These boots use Heatseeker Eco insulation which is composed of 70-85% post-consumer recycled materials and sources 10% of the outsole’s rubber from small farmers committed to agroforestry principles.\n\nWe've tested The North Face VECTIV Fastpack Insulated on numerous overnight snowshoeing trips in the Pacific Northwest. We also tested it on a 3-night, 4-day winter trip into Baxter State Park in Maine. Over the course of the trip, the tester summited Katahdin, Hamlin, and North Brother, which are three peaks on the New England 67 4,000-footers list, and notoriously difficult in winter. These boots performed perfectly in a wide variety of temperatures, from 15-degree cold to a 4"},{"text":"e Cold pick. If you tend to run warm or frequently move between indoors and outdoors, the warmest boot on paper may actually be more insulation than you want.\n\nHow do I choose between leather, suede, rubber, and synthetic winter boots?\n\nThink about how you plan to use them. Rubber is an easy choice for wet snow, slush, mud, and chores because it keeps water out and is easy to clean. Leather and suede can work well for everyday winter wear, but their care needs depend on the specific material and waterproofing treatment. Synthetic materials can keep weight down and are common in winter hiking boots.\n\nRather than choosing only by material, look at the whole boot: waterproofing, insulation, traction, fit, and how much maintenance you’re willing to do.\n\nWe set rigorous criteria for what makes a good winter boot, then tested the most promising models in rugged conditions. Photo courtesy Josette Deschambeault.\n\nHow we researched and tested\n\nWe started by looking at the most popular women’s winter boots on the market, but the real test was getting them outside. We wore boots in winter conditions from Alaska and Colorado to New York, New Hampshire, Chicago, and Maine, including ice, wet sn"},{"text":" from Maine to Alaska. We review the best boots for warmth, water resistance, fit, comfort and traction\n\nWe researched the 100 most popular winter gloves, liner gloves, heated gloves, and mittens to find the best gloves for running, cycling, extreme cold, and touchscreen usage."}]}
{"item_id":"feed:https://www.treelinereview.com/gearreviews/best-winter-gloves","version_id":"78c32c47fb0757265f105588490347bcc10df722a863df51f98a13444e64e425","kind":"feed","publisher_id":"treelinereview","title":"Best Winter Gloves of 2026","url":"https://www.treelinereview.com/gearreviews/best-winter-gloves","published_at":"2026-10-02T16:28:27Z","source_notes":{"perspective":"independent","notes":"Hands-on outdoor gear testing: boots, socks, gloves, layers, sleep systems, packs. Affiliate model."},"selection_reason":"Under Relevance, Credibility, and Suitability and official requirements, described field tests provide citable evidence about cold-weather dexterity, bulk, and touchscreen limitations relevant to Mammoth, including Nathan testing at 15–50°F and reduced Achiou touchscreen effectiveness below about 20°F. Freshness and novelty is supported by two updated winners and reported multi-year use; Commercial incentives requires disclosing affiliate commissions and weighing praise accordingly, despite the author's stated absence of brand sponsorship. This supports Pieces 01, 02, and 03 with attributed observations under Uncertainty; Mammoth-specific testing and official requirements are absent, and this publisher alone cannot establish an article's central argument.","excerpted":true,"passages":[{"text":"Best Winter Gloves of 2026\n\nWarm, thin, lightweight gloves for running, cycling, and extreme cold\n\nA good pair of winter gloves can make all the difference for all-season running, cycling, hiking, and snowplay. Photo by Robert Curzon.\n\nOctober 2nd, 2026, updated with two new winnersHome > Gear Reviews > Apparel\n\nWhen cold weather gets serious, your hands often feel it first. I’ve spent winters in Alaska, Colorado, and Maine, and I’ve worn winter gloves while skiing, hiking, saddling horses in snowstorms, digging vehicles out of snowbanks, and chasing Alaskan huskies across frozen lakes. I love being outside in winter, but only if I can still feel my hands.\n\nFor this guide, we tested winter gloves for everything from everyday chores and dog walks to running, cycling, skiing, hiking, and long days outside in the cold. We used them in snowstorms, icy conditions, wet snow, and deep cold to see which ones stayed warm and comfortable without making it impossible to actually use our hands.\n\nWe paid closest attention to warmth, weather protection, comfort, durability, dexterity, and fit. Depending on the glove, we also tested touchscreen use, cycling grip, and heated settings, because what"},{"text":" matters in a lightweight running glove is very different from what matters in a mitten made for extreme cold.\n\nTop Pick: The North Face Denali Etip Gloves are our favorite overall because they hit a useful middle ground between warmth, dexterity, durability, and price. They’re warm enough for many everyday winter activities without feeling bulky, and we found them easy to use for the kinds of tasks where you still need your fingers to work.\n\nIf you’re looking for ski and snowboard-specific gloves, check out our Best Ski Gloves and Best Snowboard Gloves and Mittens guides\n\nWe create reader-supported, objective gear reviews independently selected by our editors. This story may contain affiliate links, which help fund our website. When you click on the links to purchase gear, we may get a commission — without costing you an extra cent. Thank you for supporting our work and mission of outdoor coverage for every body! Learn more.\n\nWinter Gloves Comparison Table\n\n*We note any discounts we see on our Deals page, which is updated daily.\n\nFor more of our most popular Winter Gear Guide stories:\n\nBest Base Layers\n\nBest Women’s Winter Boots & Men’s Winter Boots\n\nBest Snowshoes\n\nBest Winter Tr"},{"text":"action Devices (Microspikes + Crampons)\n\nThe Best Winter Gloves for Men and Women\n\nBest Overall Winter Gloves: The North Face Denali Etip Gloves\n\nTouchscreen compatible? Yes, thumb and index fingerMaterial: Polyester fleeceInsulated: FleeceClosure: Zipper on back of hand What we liked: Warmth, durability, and dexterity make these an all-around glove for most usersWhat we didn’t like: Won’t be the warmest gloves available\n\nThe North Face Denali Etip Gloves earned our award two years in a row for Best Gloves for Winter Hiking and Running because of their warmth, dexterity, and touchscreen compatibility. They hit the sweet spot of warmth, price, durability, and dexterity, making them a good choice for most winter outdoor activities, and our best overall choice.\n\nThe author testing the North Face Etip Denali on winter chores. Photo courtesy Josette Deschambeault.\n\nThe North Face Denali Etip Gloves are mid-weight gloves made from fully recycled materials: Polyester (shell), nylon (back-of-hand overlay), polyester brushed tricot (palm lining), and TKA 100 polyester fleece (back-of-hand lining).\n\nThey’re warm and breathable, but not meant for extremely cold weather. Reviewers from Ireland"},{"text":"s not have Raynaud’s but has very poor circulation, was thrilled to finally have warm hands in sub-zero temperatures. (It helps that these mittens come down to mid-forearm, so the warmth from your puffy jacket extends to the warmth of your mittens.)\n\nBut, this winter glove might be overkill as your daily-driver gloves unless you are highly susceptible to cold or suffer from Raynaud’s. For well-below-freezing temps, however, testers called these mittens “lifesavers” and \"the only thing that works.\" The same can now be said for hauling wood and walking the dog in super-cold circumstances, according to one Colorado-based, cabin-dwelling tester.\n\nBest waterproof Winter Glove: MCTI Waterproof Ski Gloves\n\nTouchscreen Compatible? YesMaterial: Thinsulate, synthetic leather, waterproof membraneInsulated: YesClosure: Adjustable wrist bucklesWhat we liked: Great weatherproofness and functional pocket. Overall, excellent value in moderately cold conditionsWhat we didn’t like: A little bulky, and long dry times despite good breathability\n\nWe chose the MCTi Waterproof Winter Gloves as the Best Waterproof and Windproof Gloves out of their competitors based on their waterproofness, windproofness, "},{"text":"warmth, comfort, and dexterity—and affordable price.\n\nThe MCTi Waterproof Winter Gloves are made from windproof polyester, 3M Thinsulate insulation, a waterproof thermoplastic polyurethane (TPU) membrane, and 100% brushed polyester lining. Note that some reviewers mentioned the lengthy drying process–one reason why they didn’t win for best budget ski gloves. Still, if you don't plan to get your gloves wet, you'd be hard-pressed to find a glove this warm and functional at this price.\n\nThey also have rubber fingers for reinforced durability and a polyurethane coating on the palm. A zippered pocket on the back of the hand (big enough to fit keys, an ID, cash, or a disposable hand warmer) and adjustable wrist buckles are bonus features on an already-stellar glove.\n\nWhile we ultimately picked the MCTi Waterproof Winter Gloves for our Best Waterproof and Windproof Gloves, the Give’r 4 Season Gloves were a close second and admittedly are much more stylish. The Give'r received great reviews from REI users and external media alike–and we really enjoyed wearing them all winter in Maine.\n\nBut at the end of the day, the MCTi Gloves were ⅓ of the price of the Give’r 4 Seasons.\n\nThere were a few"},{"text":"loves. He’s an avid adventurer who’s used to the Pacific Northwest chill but found Colorado’s high altitude even more of an issue for his cold fingers. He was shocked when he put them on (in 15°F at an outdoor brewery). “My big problem is that gloves are cold when I go to put them on, which means I have to warm up the glove to warm up my fingers. Having pre-heated gloves is a game-changer. I don’t have to flap my arms anymore while everyone else is eating snacks just to feel my fingers.”\n\nOf course, the price is a deterrent for most people. Other dings were the weight, and the dexterity (more insulation = less dexterity). Still, multiple everyday users wrote in that these gloves were lifesavers that allowed them to be outdoors in the winter even as they experience Raynaud’s.\n\nTo learn more, check out our in-depth review of the Outdoor Research Prevail Heated GORE-TEX Gloves and Mittens.\n\nBest Leather Winter Gloves: Hestra Fall Line\n\nTouchscreen Compatible? NoMaterial: Impregnated cowhideInsulated: YesClosure: Neoprene cuff, hook, and loopWhat we liked: Warmth and protection against elements while retaining dexterity and general hand use. Strong waterproof barrierWhat we didn’t like"},{"text":"ures. But we think for most people building up some heat from working in the yard, snowshoeing or any number of other outdoor activities, they provide the right amount of warmth without causing your hands to sweat.\n\nIf you’re more of a mittens fan than you are of gloves, Hestra also makes a 3-finger mitten, as well as a traditional mitten.\n\nWe were happy about the Fall Lines’ warmth and durability. They excel at dexterity, like when zipping up a winter jacket. Some testers raised the point that the gloves were very warm during activity, but less so with inactivity (think hiking versus walking around town).\n\nBest Gloves For Running: Nathan Hypernight Reflective Convertible Mitt\n\nTouchscreen Compatible? Yes, index fingersMaterial: 88% Polyester 12% Spandex Pro stretchInsulated: NoClosure: Elastic cuffsWhat we liked: Versatile–warm as mitt and cooling as gloves, reflective, touchscreen compatibleWhat we didn’t like: Not best for extreme cold\n\nThe Nathan HyperNight Reflective Convertible Mitt is the ideal running glove for cold-weather runners who prefer the warmth and protection of a mitten and the dexterity of a glove. With a pull-over mitten option, you can choose your own adventure"},{"text":"n an ounce each, to be exact) and meant for chilly weather, but not too far below freezing. However, for versatility, windproofness and water resistance, there were few who objected to crowning the Castelli Perfetto RoS (which stands for “Rain or Shine”) gloves the winner.\n\nBest Touchscreen Winter Gloves: Achiou Winter Knit Gloves\n\nTouchscreen Compatible? YesMaterial: Acrylic, polyester, spandexInsulated: N/AClosure: Pull-on elasticWhat we liked: Anti-slip palm, great price for warm, comfortable touchscreen gloves.What we didn’t like: Touchscreen feature not as capable in temperatures below 20°F. Will snag on velcro\n\nWe chose the Achiou Winter Knit Gloves as the Best Gloves for Touchscreen out of the hundreds of competitors based on their touchscreen compatibility, grip, warmth, comfort, and budget price (think the price of a burger and fries!). The Achiou Winter Knit Gloves overwhelmingly received positive reviews across the board for their effectiveness with touchscreens on phones and smartwatches. Both everyday customer reviews and outdoor/clothing media were impressed by how the Achiou performed—especially for their budget price.\n\nThe Achiou Winter Knit Gloves are made from kni"},{"text":"How we judged: To be considered for each category, we measured each glove or mitten against standardized criteria: they had to be warm, affordable, durable, comfortable, and dexterous.\n\nFrom there, we tested for features applicable only to specific categories. For example, with our heated glove pick, we tested for features valued by those who suffer from chronically cold fingers: long-lasting batteries, adjustable heat settings, durability, dexterity, and of course, warmth. For most cycling gloves, we tested compatibility with handlebars. For best touchscreen gloves, we tested on Apple and Android phones.\n\nSkiing, hiking, running, and cycling\n\nWinter chores and outdoor work\n\nSnowstorms and icy conditions\n\nWet snow and deep cold\n\nTouchscreens, bike grips, and heated settings\n\nWarmth and weather protection\n\nComfort and fit\n\nDexterity without excess bulk\n\nDurability over repeated use\n\nActivity-specific features\n\nThe author cutting her Christmas tree in trusty Kinco gloves! Photo courtesy of Chad Culler.\n\nAuthor’s expertise: why you should trust us\n\nSpending winters in the cold climates of Alaska, Colorado, and Maine, I’m no stranger to frozen fingers. I’ve taught ski school in a blizz"},{"text":"elf) is sponsored, an ambassador, or an employee of any of the companies mentioned here. This is unbiased, and we do this to help you find the best product for the price. Treeline Review doesn’t accept sponsored content, native advertising, or paid reviews.\n\nSee all of Josette's articles on her author page.\n\nTested on ice and snow from Maine to Alaska for 5 years, here’s our review of the best men’s winter boots for warmth, water resistance, fit, comfort, and traction.\n\nWe spent 8 years testing the top women’s winter boots for ice and snow from Maine to Alaska. We review the best boots for warmth, water resistance, fit, comfort and traction\n\nWe researched the 100 most popular winter gloves, liner gloves, heated gloves, and mittens to find the best gloves for running, cycling, extreme cold, and touchscreen usage."}]}
{"item_id":"feed:https://www.rokslide.com/tract-toric-lrf-rangefinding-binocular-review/","version_id":"220ed6eb0893442944fab61d7dad5202a7be74c9abe5e6d3b342f9fc0db3d2a2","kind":"feed","publisher_id":"rokslide","title":"Tract Toric LRF Rangefinding Binocular Review","url":"https://www.rokslide.com/tract-toric-lrf-rangefinding-binocular-review/","published_at":"2026-10-10T13:18:20Z","source_notes":{"perspective":"practitioner","notes":"Backcountry hunter reviews: rangefinding binoculars, packs, optics. Ad supported."},"selection_reason":"Under Relevance, Credibility, and Freshness and novelty, this newly reported hands-on review provides citable weight, cold-soak, rain/fog ranging, optical comparison, and ballistic field observations that transfer to Mammoth, supporting pieces 01, 02, and 03. Commercial incentives require noting Rokslide's ad support and purchase callout, while product provision, affiliate compensation, and sponsor ties are unspecified; under Uncertainty and Suitability and official requirements, attribute results to this reviewer, acknowledge untested long-term durability, and leave required status unspecified.","excerpted":true,"passages":[{"text":"More and more electro-optics are coming to market for the western hunter. Multiple use is the name of the game, and a single device that replaces two or more makes all the more sense to hunters that are already stuffing their pack with gear.\n\nThis is why range-finding binoculars make so much sense. They eliminate the need for a separate binocular and laser rangefinder, and add the convenience of having ranges available immediately. Add in onboard environmental sensors, inclinometers, compasses, and advanced ballistic solvers, and you are really eliminating some gear.\n\nSounds like a no-brainer, right? The issue has always been that there are required compromises when bringing optics, lasers, and displays together. Early versions were noticeably poor optically. So much so, many hunters returned to separate binoculars and rangefinders.\n\nFlagship RF binos from the big European companies of Zeiss, Leica, and Swarovski largely mitigated the issue with their recent models, and now hunters have the luxury of having top-shelf optics and ballistic rangefinding in one package. The rub on those? Well, that’s the usual one: they cost as much as that high-mileage used car you’ve been looking at "},{"text":"for your teen driver.\n\nOther companies have brought to market more budget-friendly options. Generally, these mid-tier offerings have excellent rangefinding and advanced ballistics–but pair that up with optical performance that, while improved from the 1st generation, still falls behind better mid-priced dedicated binoculars in optical performance.\n\nThe Jons (Jon Allen and Jon LaCorte) of Tract Optics intended to shake up that status quo with the introduction of the Tract Toric LRF 10×42 Rangefinding binocular by improving optical performance and combining it with an advanced laser and computations from Eagle Ballistics.\n\nAt just under 6 inches long, the Toric LRF is more than a half inch shorter than the Zeiss Victory RF’s 7.6 inches. The Toric LRF is compact, but not exactly lightweight. The Toric LRF tips the scale at 2 lbs. 5.5 ounces with an Outdoorsman’s stud attached. The physically larger Victory RF (also with an Outdoorsman’s stud) is actually lighter at 2 lbs. 3 ounces.\n\nThe Toric LRF is fully rubber armored with non-removable 4-position twist-out eyecups. Push-pull “locking” diopter rings are on each barrel. The left barrel adjusts the optic, and the right adjusts the dis"},{"text":"play. The locking feature is rather loose, and I did have one occasion where the diopter had shifted out and moved slightly, so that is something for the hunter to keep an eye on. Additionally, adjusting the screen will affect the optic diopter, so make sure to adjust the screen first, and then move to the left barrel. One note on this. I normally have diopters close to the “0” setting on most optics. In order to get the screen sharp, I had to adjust the screen diopter pretty far to the “-“ side. Accordingly, I had to adjust the left diopter far “-“ as well, and ended up near the far limit of adjustment. Still, after adjustment, I ended up with a sharp, easy observation view and crisp display.\n\nThe Toric LRF is powered by a single CR2 battery that is housed within the focus wheel.\n\nThis conveniently allows the front hinge to accommodate standard 1/4-20 adapters. I immediately mounted a Outdoorsman’s binoculars adapter stud and left it on indefinitely..\n\nThe right side of the Toric LRF has a single rubberized button marked “FIRE.” The button is raised slightly from the surrounding armor and has stippling around the text. The left side has four buttons. Opposite the FIRE button is a "},{"text":"it had not moved. I did a 10-hour cold soak in the freezer with the battery removed. I pulled the frosty Toric LRF out of the freezer, put in the battery, uncapped the lenses, and immediately received instant ranges with ballistic information. Of course, like any frozen optic, it did not take long for the exterior lenses to ice over in the warmer air, naturally interfering with observation and ranging, but I could see the display operating within the right barrel. As the Toric LRF equalized with the air temperature, everything returned to proper working order.\n\nThe Toric LRF has a suitably wide FOV at 330 ft. at 1000 yards. This is similar to traditional “wide field” 10×42 roof prism binoculars, but falls just short of the European competition like the Zeiss at 345 ft. at 1000 yards.\n\nApparent sharpness is excellent, and I could resolve the same element on the chart as I could with the Zeiss Victory RF in close-range chart testing. This wasn’t too surprising as both optics have a relatively low 10x magnification, but I have seen lower apparent resolution on some other mid-tier RF models from other manufacturers, so this was a good sign for the Toric LRF.\n\nColor bias and transmissio"},{"text":"n are where the expensive European models often have a clear advantage over the mid- and lower-tier options. In normal use, the Toric LRF has a pretty neutral to slightly cool image and doesn’t have the obvious color issues of other mid-tier competitors. However, when looking at a white background, I did notice a warm bias at the top of the image and a cool bias at the bottom. This led to testing the left and right barrels individually by shining light from the objective ends and observing the pupil left on a white sheet of paper. In this testing, it was clear the one barrel had a warm bias, and the other barrel was cool. This difference was much lower than other RF binoculars I have looked at, but still present.\n\nRunning the same test on the Zeiss yielded no apparent difference between barrels.\n\nTo be absolutely clear, this result for the Toric LRF is not poor. The color difference is small, and color issues during glassing are not noticeable under most conditions. In fact, the Tract has the mildest version of this effect I have seen from a non-Euro RF binocular.\n\nLow light performance is quite good. The Tract did a good job keeping up with the much more expensive Zeiss until the "},{"text":"very end of twilight, when the Zeiss did appear a touch brighter. Still, the Toric LRF had very good performance for an RF binocular.\n\nDistortion is very well controlled in the Toric LRF. There is a low amount of pincushion distortion, which is almost identical to that of the Victory RF\n\nAt the edge, fringing is easily visible and more intense than the Zeiss.\n\nThe Tract Toric LRF has the best optics I have tested in a non-Euro RF binocular. I would gladly use the Toric LRF as my primary optic, and it pushes the very best competition in performance, even if it doesn’t quite match them.\n\nThe rangefinding capabilities of the Toric LRF are what really stand out with this device. The on-board sensors include temperature, pressure, inclination, and compass bearing. The device syncs via Bluetooth with the Eagle Ballistics app. The device can store five profiles at a time. The ballistic display can be customized to display environmentals, earthbase effects (sic), bullet flight, windage and elevation, gun profile, and startup logo. The hunter can switch on and off any of those options.\n\nThe Toric LRF reticle is customizable with circle, cross, oval, 1 MOA dot, and 2 MOA dot options.\n\nOne of"},{"text":" the best features is that the user can actually “sight in” the laser to co-witness the reticle to the location the laser is actually hitting. Most rangefinders I have used don’t hit exactly in the reticle, and I have always just tested to find where they are actually hitting relative to the reticle (the Zeiss is on the left side of the circle). With the Tract, the hunter can precisely align the reticle. I did just that, and liked using the scan mode on a road reflector to dial it in. The user can adjust up to 0.5 mil in each direction.\n\nTract has several options for entering environmental data. You can select to use the Toric LRF’s on-board sensors, manually input data, or connect via Bluetooth to the Eagle Ballistic App and have the App do the calculations using the closest weather station data.\n\nThere is a bow mode that has a simple display result and can be set to horizontal or line-of-sight distance.\n\nTarget options include best, near, far, rain/snow, and extended. The best, near, and far return nearly instantaneous results while the other modes have a short delay. The rain/snow setting works well in those conditions but does not help in thick fog/clouds. In those conditions I"},{"text":"ices like a kestrel wind meter to get the best solutions.\n\nI used the Tract Toric LRF in the field for scouting, shooting, shed hunting, and bow hunting for elk. The minor optical issues I identified above were not a concern in field use. The optical performance was very close to the European competition in actual field use. Despite the small color variation between barrels, the Toric LRF provides a very comfortable viewing experience with no eye strain after prolonged use.\n\nThe display is excellent, the laser is powerful, and the returns are nearly instantaneous. Simple 1/4-20 mounting of tripod studs makes tripod glassing a breeze and stabilizes long-distance ranging attempts. Here is a video demonstrating ranging speed and performance:\n\nAs far as ergonomics go, the Toric LRF is kind of a “brick” binocular. I got along with it fine, but I needed the inter-pupillary distance a little closer than I would like to get the perfect view of the display. The eyecups are decent, and I suspect they will fit most faces well enough. I preferred the ergonomics of both the Zeiss Victory RF and the Leica Geovid Max, but my friend who has used original Swarovski SLCs forever really liked the fee"},{"text":" level. This is an excellent tool for hunters. The optics are very, very good, and just behind the Euro leaders. The rangefinder is as good as anything available. The Toric LRF’s ballistic rangefinding capabilities are more extensive and precise than even those from Zeiss and Swarovski’s top RF offerings, while being highly competitive with the brand-new Leica Geovid MAX, which costs DOUBLE the Toric LRF.\n\nEagle Ballistics is a full-featured app and on-device ballistics engine that integrates with the Toric LRF effortlessly.\n\nConsidering the price and performance, the Toric LRF is easily one of the most impressive optics I have seen in recent years.\n\nI need more time with the Toric LRF to really evaluate the long-term durability, but it is comforting to know Tract covers the Toric LRF with the “Tract Trust Assurance” warranty, which is their fully transferable lifetime warranty that covers both the optics AND electronics. Not many electro-optics are covered so thoroughly. Available here.\n\nComment or ask Matt questions here.\n\nChoose your glass wisely\n\nMeopta Optika LR VS Leica Geovid R Binoculars\n\nSwarovski ATC 17-40x56mm Spotting Scope Review"}]}
{"item_id":"feed:https://www.rokslide.com/kuiu-icon-6-3500-backpack-review/","version_id":"e05dabe7e03e73aeb70fd9c735e547f1271e3a12c4896b1ef987af9b31254b57","kind":"feed","publisher_id":"rokslide","title":"KUIU Icon 6 3500 Backpack Review","url":"https://www.rokslide.com/kuiu-icon-6-3500-backpack-review/","published_at":"2026-09-23T12:00:06Z","source_notes":{"perspective":"practitioner","notes":"Backcountry hunter reviews: rangefinding binoculars, packs, optics. Ad supported."},"selection_reason":"Under Relevance, Credibility, and Freshness and novelty, this new pack review supplies transferable evidence from comparative 1.2-mile loops with 510 feet of elevation gain and loss at 28- and 92-pound loads, plus a measured equipped weight of 5 pounds 12.5 ounces and specific usability drawbacks. Under Commercial incentives and Uncertainty, Rokslide is ad supported, no product-specific sponsorship or free sample is disclosed, and comfort findings should remain attributed to this reviewer; the short tests do not establish multi-day durability or satisfy the three-independent-publisher standard for an article's central argument. It supports Piece 01's new-design assessment, Piece 02's examination of KUIU's comfort and simplicity claims, and Piece 03's carrying-weight, fit, and usability tradeoffs, with required status unspecified under Suitability and official requirements.","excerpted":true,"passages":[{"text":"Home\n\nRokstaff\n\nForums\n\nRok Blog\n\nGearArchery\n\nBackpacks\n\nClothing\n\nEssentials\n\nFirearms\n\nFootwear\n\nMuzzleloader\n\nOptics\n\nSleep Systems\n\nStoves\n\nTips & TacticsAntelope\n\nArchery\n\nBackpacking\n\nBear\n\nElk\n\nFirearms\n\nGeneral\n\nHorse Packin’\n\nHunting Tips\n\nMountain Goat\n\nMule Deer\n\nShed Hunting\n\nSheep\n\nTURKEY\n\nVideos\n\nPodcast\n\nLoad Dev\n\nStore\n\nForum\n\nAdvertisers\n\nContact\n\nStore\n\nHome\n\nRokstaff\n\nForums\n\nRok Blog\n\nGearAllArcheryBackpacksClothingEssentialsFirearmsFootwearMuzzleloaderOpticsSleep SystemsStoves OpticsTract Toric LRF Rangefinding Binocular Review\n\nGearGear Reviews: Lathrop Super Wides, Peak Refuel, KUIU Icon 6, and…\n\nBackpacksKUIU Icon 6 3500 Backpack Review\n\nOpticsHow To Choose a Thermal Scope\n\nTips & TacticsAllAntelopeArcheryBackpackingBearElkFirearmsGeneralHorse Packin’Hunting TipsMountain GoatMule DeerShed HuntingSheepTURKEY ElkRifle Elk Hunting 201: Finding Bulls Everyone Else Misses with Cody…\n\nGearGear Reviews: Lathrop Super Wides, Peak Refuel, KUIU Icon 6, and…\n\nElkStories from Western Hunting Legend Jim Zumbo\n\nFirearmsHow AI Is Helping Hunters Score Bucks Faster and Smarter\n\nVideos Sleep SystemsTentipi Safir 7P Canvas vs. Sil-Nylon Review\n\nArchery2023 Head to Head Bow T"},{"text":"est: Part 2\n\nBackpacksStone Glacier Terminus Review\n\nArcheryHead to Head Bow Test: Part I\n\nMule DeerThe Best of Hunting Big Mule Deer Trailer\n\nPodcast\n\nLoad Dev\n\nStore\n\nA backcountry hunter’s backpack is an indispensable tool. It serves as pantry, dresser, optics case, first aid cabinet, seat, and so many other things. The best hunting backpacks do all of those things, but also haul that precious cargo of meat out after the tags are punched.\n\nWay back in 2011, I used the original KUIU Icon frame and bag and appreciated the quality craftsmanship and clever organization. The carbon frame was innovative and did a good job with moderate loads. Under really heavy loads, though, it got overwhelmed, and some hunters had durability issues, including frames cracking and breaking. KUIU went through more iterations and improvements, always preserving their organizational prowess but improving fit and finish. Performance did improve, and many hunters really found the newer Icon Pro and Pro LT to be a good fit for their hunts. I liked the weight and function of the newer packs, but still felt heavy loads got too uncomfortable, and the suspension just didn’t quite fit me right.\n\nSignificantly im"},{"text":"prove heavy-weight carrying capacity and comfort.\n\nSimplify the overall design while dramatically improving performance.\n\nTo reach these goals, KUIU started from scratch, which means the new Icon 6 packs and frames are not backward compatible with the older Icons, including the Pro and Pro LT models.\n\nIt all starts with the new patent-pending frame, which combines a “corrugated carbon architecture” made of thermoplastic carbon fiber. The new frame is taller and wider than previous KUIU frames and is specifically designed to flex where it is needed and stay stiff for load-bearing. The new frame promises increased strength and durability as well as improved “damage tolerance.” KUIU is confident the new frame satisfies design goal #1.\n\nThe suspension fits through hook-and-loop attachment and offers excellent adjustability in both length and angle. The hip belt and shoulder straps have been redesigned in shape and materials, with new foam mapped to be thinner and thicker depending on anatomic need. A thick lumbar pad matches a new wider hip belt.\n\nThe available pack bags include the tested 3500 CI daypack, a versatile 4500 CI option, a multi-day 6000 CI freighter, and an expedition-rea"},{"text":"dy 8200 CI behemoth. Quality 330 denier Cordura and Waterproof Cordura are the primary fabrics used in the construction of the packs. My 3500 test pack is well made with quality stitching and materials throughout. Packs attach to the frame simply by placing the packbag’s hypalon stay pockets over the top stays and tensioning by way of two hook-and-loop straps under the frame by the lumbar pad.\n\nDesign goal #2 was design simplicity. This goal speaks to me because some of my favorite backpacks have high performance and function, but take a lot of time and effort to do things like use the load shelf or switch bags. Here is a huge win for KUIU, as this is one of the simplest designs in a hunting pack.\n\nTo use the load sling the hunter just needs to unbuckle the compression strap bucks, detach the bag attachment straps and lift the hypalon frame stay pockets off the upper frame stays. The top straps will squeeze through the frame stay pockets without removing the buckles from the straps. Switching pack bags is a breeze. The Icon 6 doesn’t have load stabilizer straps, so there is nothing to unthread when removing the pack, attaching the pack, or loading the sling.\n\nIn typical KUIU fashio"},{"text":"n, the 3500 pack has plenty of storage options. The main compartment access is a full u-shaped zipper, so the hunter can position the zippers for easy top or side access. The main compartment has a zippered mesh long side pocket on one side and an open sleeve pocket on the other side. I liked the sleeve for my preferred hard side hydration water bottle. Bladder users have a traditional bladder pocket with hanging strap. This pocket will fit really large water bladders.\n\nThe outer panel has a large zippered mesh pocket on the inside and two faux-lid pockets on the outside. This is a convenient alternative to a traditional pocketed lid. Under those pockets is a tall pocket on the outer panel which makes for quick use items like rain gear or sundries, but is also long enough to fit a full size spotting scope. There are two traditional open top pockets on each exterior side of the main bag for trekking poles, tripods, or additional water bottles. There are two hypalon handles on the back panel which are a nice touch for moving around the loaded pack.\n\nThere are two removable hip belt pockets as well. These pockets can be easily removed to attach other accessories to the underlying webb"},{"text":"h daypack loads is awesome, but obviously it is not meant for hauling meat.\n\nThe previous generation pack-on-frame Icons were certainly capable of hauling just about anything I could strap to them. However, they just didn’t carry those loads as comfortably as some of the top performers. For me, two of those top performers are the Stone Glacier XCurve frame (with Sky 5900 pack) and the Kifaru ARK frame (with Bedlam pack). For this review, I made 1.2-mile loops (510 vertical feet gained and lost) with each pack loaded with 28 pounds of day hunting gear, followed by re-hiking the same loop with an additional 64 pounds of blasting media in a game bag to simulate an elk quarter. Each loop included portions on trail, off trail, and through deadfall.\n\nWith the packs loaded with day gear, it was difficult to really find the difference in comfort. I had zero belt slippage, no hot spots, and no shoulder discomfort with any of these three pack systems. The ARK frame was the stiffest, while the Icon 6 flexed a little more with the body, and the Xcurve was pretty laterally compliant.\n\nOf course, I fully expected bigger differences to emerge during the heavy load loops. I was pleasantly surprise"},{"text":"tigue with heavy loads. It didn’t matter how I tried to adjust the suspension. The Icon 6 completely remedied this for me, and brings the Icon 6 on the level with the very best frames available. Compared to the Xcurve, the Icon 6 had a much stiffer feel, but still flexed a bit when crawling over downed logs. The ARK is stiffer laterally, and … while still very comfortable … has more of a packboard-type feel. As a personal preference, I preferred the feel of the Icon 6 and ARK to the Xcurve in the heavy load testing.\n\nThe Icon 6 comes in two frame sizes. The Regular frame is 24.5 inches and the Tall frame is 26.5 inches. There are specific men’s and women’s suspensions available for these frames. My test Regular frame just under 10 inches wide and lumbar pad and top stays.\n\nI weighed the Icon 6 3500 with all the accessories I would actually use including both hip belt pockets and the Pack Load Hauler. The real world empty weight was 5 lbs. 12.5 ounces. This is in line with the Stone Glacier Sky 5900 (with belt pocket, lid, and water bottle holder) at 5 lbs. 13 ounces. The Kifaru ARK/Bedlam tips the scales over a pound heavier at exactly 7 lbs with a medium belt pocket and water bott"},{"text":"le holder.\n\nThe Icon 6 3500 base system including accessories comes in at $599. This pricing puts it squarely in the premium pack market but lower than top competitors. The Stone Glacier Sky 5900 is $725 without any accessories. The Kifaru ARK with the Bedlam runs a base price of $850. The Icon 6 is a downright bargain in the premium pack area especially when accounting for the included accessories.\n\nKUIU has elevated their Icon backpack line with their new Icon 6 frame and bags. The expressed design goals of improved heavy load comfort and simplified use have been satisfied. They have managed to maintain the low weights and organization they are known for while bringing the load-carrying performance and comfort up to compete with the very best in the market. It can not be overstated just how welcome and convenient a simple and intuitive design is for changing bags and converting for load hauling.\n\nEverything isn’t perfect with the Icon 6. The all-new design of the Icon 6 leaves existing Icon/Icon LT users with an incompatible system. I don’t quite get the daisy chain straps, and the Pack Load Hauler is finicky to get on the straps. Even so, there is no denying the Icon 6 system is"},{"text":" a big step forward for KUIU’s frame and pack system.\n\nComment or ask Matt questions here.\n\nRead all the Rokslide pack reviews here.\n\nTeratorn Backpack Review\n\nAkek Striker 2100 Pack Review\n\nKifaru 44 Mag, Gen 2\n\nHome\n\nRokstaff\n\nForums\n\nRok Blog\n\nGearAllArcheryBackpacksClothingEssentialsFirearmsFootwearMuzzleloaderOpticsSleep SystemsStoves OpticsTract Toric LRF Rangefinding Binocular Review\n\nGearGear Reviews: Lathrop Super Wides, Peak Refuel, KUIU Icon 6, and…\n\nBackpacksKUIU Icon 6 3500 Backpack Review\n\nOpticsHow To Choose a Thermal Scope\n\nTips & TacticsAllAntelopeArcheryBackpackingBearElkFirearmsGeneralHorse Packin’Hunting TipsMountain GoatMule DeerShed HuntingSheepTURKEY ElkRifle Elk Hunting 201: Finding Bulls Everyone Else Misses with Cody…\n\nGearGear Reviews: Lathrop Super Wides, Peak Refuel, KUIU Icon 6, and…\n\nElkStories from Western Hunting Legend Jim Zumbo\n\nFirearmsHow AI Is Helping Hunters Score Bucks Faster and Smarter\n\nVideos Sleep SystemsTentipi Safir 7P Canvas vs. Sil-Nylon Review\n\nArchery2023 Head to Head Bow Test: Part 2\n\nBackpacksStone Glacier Terminus Review\n\nArcheryHead to Head Bow Test: Part I\n\nMule DeerThe Best of Hunting Big Mule Deer Trailer\n\nPodcast\n\nLoad Dev\n\n"},{"text":"Store\n\nKifaru Crater Review\n\nSeek Outside Lanner 5400 Backpack Review\n\nTT#38 Saving a Buck with Robby Denning and Travis Hobbs"}]}


